{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/packet.json'","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\",\n   \"period\": \"Next annual report (Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, likely filed early 2027): the outstanding amount of credit support/guarantees; set by the reason (obligation already undertaken, tracked at the next annual report).\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K), including its guarantees and credit-support commitment disclosures.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"There is no open thesis; this asks specifically how much credit support/guarantee exposure NVIDIA is committing to backstop third parties' compute infrastructure, a balance-sheet and credit-risk question whose answer can move independently of NVIDIA's product revenue.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout\",\n   \"publisher\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑scale AI services.\\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\\nTo address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced a new business model under which AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, aligning the parties through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\",\n     \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus Technologies is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Firmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Firmus Technologies\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\",\n   \"period\": \"Check date of 31 December 2027 (18 months from the 1 July 2026 item date): whether the guarantee is signed by then; set by the rule for an unsigned obligation (a check date within 18 months of the item).\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's 8-K/10-Q/10-K filings disclosing guarantees and commitments, or an official NVIDIA confirmation / the definitive agreement being announced as executed.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks the aggregate outstanding credit support extended under the revenue-sharing 'AI Compute Partnership' model as disclosed at the next annual report; the OpenAI Ohio lease guarantee is a distinct, much larger, specific obligation still under negotiation that may never be signed and may sit outside that revenue-sharing program. NVIDIA could report a large clean-1 aggregate from other cloud partners without ever signing the OpenAI guarantee, or sign the OpenAI guarantee while accounting for it separately, so the two answers can move apart.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45\",\n   \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"text\": \"It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balance sheet to gain market leverage, quietly evolving from a chip vendor into the 'central bank' of the AI computing ecosystem by offering financial backing to emerging cloud service providers in exchange for a share of their revenue.\\nOn July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\\nIn return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\\nOn July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\\nAccording to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program. This move marks a significant strategic shift for NVIDIA:\\nOn one hand, it lowers financing barriers for emerging cloud service providers to expand its customer base; on the other, it directly participates in profit distribution within the downstream computing market through revenue sharing, thereby extending its control further down the AI value chain.\\nBusiness Model Shift: From Selling Chips to Sharing Cloud Revenue\\nAccording to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream. The core objective of this model is to overcome the long-standing financing barrier that has constrained startups from accessing large-scale computing power.\\nNVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. These deployments directly reflect NVIDIA’s latest progress in transforming computing demand into deployable, financeable infrastructure.\\nNVIDIA noted that emerging AI companies have historically faced significant constraints in accessing capital-intensive infrastructure—even long-term contractual commitments have often been insufficient to secure financing for compute capacity procurement. This means that numerous AI-native firms, model developers, and inference service providers face lengthy delays when scaling their computing capabilities: site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware commissioning—each step can take several months or even longer.\\nThe promise of this new model is to realign economic structures so these groups can rapidly access full-stack accelerated computing capabilities without waiting for the traditional infrastructure build-out cycle to complete.\\nUnderlying Rationale: Addressing the Core Challenge of GPU Financing\\nReportedly, GPUs typically represent the most expensive component in AI data centers. For chip buyers with lower credit ratings, securing sufficient loans is itself a major hurdle.\\nA data center executive commented on such NVIDIA deals as “killing two birds with one stone.” He explained that if NVIDIA merely provided a guarantee for a data center facility lease, “you’d still face the question of ‘how to finance the GPUs’”; but if NVIDIA commits to purchasing any unsold compute capacity within the facility, “the GPU financing issue is resolved—and so is the data center’s financing problem.”\\nIn other words, NVIDIA’s backstop commitment effectively serves as a credit enhancement tool, enabling emerging cloud service providers—who would otherwise struggle to obtain bank loans—to mobilize significantly larger amounts of capital and accelerate data center construction.\\nStrategic Intent: Breaking the Dominance of Large Customers\\nNVIDIA’s launch of these initiatives is grounded in a clear strategic context. Currently, a small number of large cloud service providers—including Amazon, Microsoft, SpaceX, Oracle, Meta, and Google—purchase the majority of NVIDIA’s chip output. However, several of these companies are developing their own competitive AI chips, posing a potential threat to NVIDIA.\\nTo reduce its reliance on these giant clients, NVIDIA has spent the past few years actively supporting a cohort of emerging GPU cloud service providers, such as CoreWeave. The newly announced 'AI Compute Partnership Program' represents a continuation and deepening of this strategy.\\nAccording to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\nCapital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees\\nNVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial.\\nTo date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA’s own researchers have also used GPU servers leased back from Lambda.\\nOn the capacity guarantee front, NVIDIA began advancing related deals in the autumn of last year. In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. This move effectively alleviated investor concerns about CoreWeave’s highly leveraged business model and drove its stock price up nearly 30% within the following week.\\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\\nTaken together, NVIDIA is constructing a multi-layered mechanism to align interests: equity investments, capacity leasebacks, lease guarantees, and now revenue-sharing arrangements. Each layer deepens the financial ties between NVIDIA and downstream cloud service providers, enabling NVIDIA to directly share in the incremental returns from the commercialization of AI computing power beyond chip sales alone.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The Information's prior reporting, relayed in the item, said NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion. The guarantee is described as under negotiation, not signed.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"rumour\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\",\n   \"period\": \"End of calendar 2026, the date the item supports ('could begin shipping later this year' relative to its 2 July 2026 date).\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"product_node\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA quarterly earnings releases and 10-Q/10-K disclosures confirming Vera Rubin first shipments/revenue, and shipping confirmations.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Vera Rubin first shipments are a manufacturing/ramp milestone settled by NVIDIA's own delivery and revenue disclosures; the credit-support theses (clean-1, clean-2) track the dollar amount of guarantees/backstops NVIDIA extends to cloud partners. NVIDIA could ship Vera Rubin on schedule while extending little credit support, or delay shipments while guaranteeing partners' capacity, so the two answers move independently.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-02\",\n   \"title\": \"Microsoft vs. Nvidia: Which Is the Better AI Stock to Own for the Next 3 Years?\",\n   \"text\": \"Microsoft ( MSFT +1.28% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) are two heavyweights in the technology world and key players in artificial intelligence (AI). Microsoft seems to have its hand in every tech market segment, especially anything involving enterprise customers. Meanwhile, Nvidia has become the de facto leader in GPU chips used in AI data centers.\\nBoth stocks have made investors very wealthy over the years. But which of these top AI stocks is the better buy for the next three years? This article will explain why I like Microsoft a tad more. Although, as you'll see below, it's hard to go wrong with either one.\\nVera Rubin likely means Nvidia has more growth ahead\\nNvidia has enjoyed one of the most impressive growth spurts in history over these past few years as the AI build-out juiced demand for its GPUs. This supercycle continues to rage on. Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\nWall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year. Nvidia's valuation is already well below its high at less than 19 times sales, and it might become much cheaper as these next 24 months play out.\\nMicrosoft is pivoting to capitalize on its enterprise customer base\\nMicrosoft originally tied its AI hopes to OpenAI. Although Microsoft has done well with its OpenAI investment and enjoyed significant cloud computing growth as a result of the partnership, it has still struggled to establish itself as a key AI player. Users, especially enterprises, have opted for Anthropic's Claude among other AI models. As a result, Microsoft shifted to a multi-model strategy and has reportedly considered adding open-source models to Copilot Cowork to lower token costs.\\nInvestors should applaud Microsoft's willingness to adapt. The company has deeply entrenched relationships with the enterprise world, which gives Microsoft more margin for error in fine-tuning its AI strategy than most companies. Plus, Microsoft's Azure remains a formidable growth engine, with a staggering $627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations.\\nNvidia's data center business has grown so much that the stock has become a concentrated investment in this ongoing supercycle. That means Nvidia has a sky-high ceiling with Vera Rubin shipping soon, but it's riskier in that the floor is much lower if companies stop pouring all this money into AI infrastructure. Meanwhile, Microsoft remains highly diversified across software, cloud computing, and AI. That lowers the company's ceiling, but it's also the safer stock.\\nMicrosoft's impressive track record typically earns it a premium valuation. The stock has traded at an average of almost 33 times its trailing-12-month earnings over the past decade, but trades at just 22 times its earnings today. Meanwhile, Wall Street analysts expect the company to grow earnings by 16% to 17% annually over the next three to five years.\\nWhile some may opt for Nvidia's immense growth potential , Microsoft rarely offers a valuation compelling enough to buy into.\\nJustin Pope is a contributing Motley Fool stock market analyst covering information technology, consumer discretionary, consumer staples, and industrials. Prior to The Motley Fool, Justin was a business manager for an industrial company.\\nBy Justin Pope – Jul 2, 2026 at 12:43PM EST\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is reported to be in full production and could begin shipping later in calendar 2026.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in cumulative orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang)\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": [\n     {\n      \"measure\": \"orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips\",\n      \"period\": \"through 2027\",\n      \"value\": \"$1 trillion\",\n      \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n      \"basis\": \"Expectations laid out by CEO Jensen Huang; cumulative orders across Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\"\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 (the item's 'next year' from its 2026 date), when the four hyperscalers report full-year capex; set by the item, which projects the figure could reach $1 trillion in 2027 up from around $650 billion in 2026.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"The four leading AI hyperscalers' quarterly and full-year capital expenditure disclosures in their earnings releases and 10-K/10-Q filings for FY2027, aggregated.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 tracks whether Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform reaches first shipments by end-2026, a product-timing question; this thesis tracks the industry-level capex quantity that drives total GPU demand. They can move independently: Rubin could ship on schedule while hyperscaler capex is cut back, or Rubin could slip while aggregate capex stays high on spending for existing platforms.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-04\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) investors have had it good for several years. In 2023, 2024, and 2025, it produced market-crushing returns, making it one of the best stocks to own during each of those years. However, 2026 hasn't been that way. The stock is up 5% so far in 2026, which isn't nothing, but compared to the returns that Nvidia investors have enjoyed in recent years, it feels like an absolute failure. On another note, its market-crushing status is in jeopardy for 2026, as the S&P 500 is up nearly 10% for the year.\\nWith Nvidia stock looking like a dud this year, many investors are wondering whether now is the time to cash out and look for other investment opportunities, or to load up on Nvidia shares. Let's take a look, as not everything is as it appears with Nvidia.\\nNvidia was the top AI stock to own over the past three years, due to its market position. Nvidia designs graphics processing units (GPUs) and the hardware that supports them. Although it doesn't own any of the fabrication process itself, it works with several companies that manufacture its GPUs, which it then sells to a wide range of customers.\\nBefore 2023, Nvidia's business was more balanced. However, the massive demand for AI data centers has driven this lineup of GPUs into high demand, and Nvidia's product line has become the go-to for several AI hyperscalers and cloud providers. Nvidia has a complete ecosystem of products that gives its clients everything they need to run an Nvidia-powered data center, locking in clients once they've chosen to use its products.\\nThis bodes well for the future, as AI hyperscalers are spending a massive amount of money this year and beyond to continue building out their AI computing footprints. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion. That means more growth is coming to Nvidia, and the investing story is far from complete. Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\\nHowever, the stock price doesn't reflect that.\\nNvidia trades at 21.7 times forward earnings, essentially the same price tag as the S&P 500. This conveys that the market believes Nvidia will be a market-average stock after 2026's growth is accounted for. However, Wall Street analysts don't believe that, as they're guiding for major growth next year. That's why the stock looks so cheap when next year's earnings are used to value the stock.\\nAs a result, I think Nvidia is an excellent stock to buy now. None of next year's growth has been priced in, which could lead to strong returns toward the end of this year.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Big four AI hyperscalers (aggregate spending plan)\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027 (next year), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar 2026-2027 memory contract cycles; set by the item's forward cost period (second half of 2026 and beyond).\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Quarterly contract-price releases from TrendForce/DRAMeXchange (DRAM, NAND, HBM) and Nvidia's rack BOM/cost disclosures.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Memory contract prices can move independently of the big four hyperscalers' aggregate data-center capex: a supply-driven memory squeeze can lift Nvidia's per-rack cost and price even if capex totals stall, and memory prices can fall while capex keeps climbing. An analyst would bet on the memory-price direction separately because it drives Nvidia's per-rack cost, pricing and supply availability.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd\",\n   \"publisher\": \"marketscale.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams put it to work with Executive Thought Leadership .\\nKey facts, context, and what it means.\\nMemory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack due to increased LPDDR5X capacity and 3D NAND storage, making it a strategic budget variable across all technology purchases\\nGPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51% while memory rose to 25-30%, requiring infrastructure planners to model AI spend on total system cost not accelerator count\\nMemory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\\nTurn your Software & Technology expertise into content.\\nWhat infrastructure planners should account for\\nThe single most expensive part of Nvidia's next-generation AI rack is no longer just the GPU. According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation. For any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\\nMorgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\\nOnly a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\\nFor enterprise technology buyers, that means memory has become a strategic budget variable rather than a rounding error, whether the purchase is a data center commitment or a fleet refresh.\\nThree considerations follow directly from the Morgan Stanley breakdown.\\nModel your AI spend on total system cost, not GPU count. The generation-over-generation story is that peripheral components, memory, printed circuit boards, networking, cooling, and power, are absorbing a larger share of every dollar. Budgets built around GPU headline prices will understate the real bill.\\nTreat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\\nRevisit the build-versus-rent math. As rack prices climb toward $8 million and memory pricing stays volatile, the calculus between owning infrastructure and consuming cloud capacity shifts. Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\\nNvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance, and demand is not in question. What has changed is where the money goes. The AI infrastructure bill is increasingly a memory bill, and the organizations planning capacity for the next two years will be the ones that budget for the full system rather than the chip on the front of the box.\\nNvidia's memory costs soar 485%, latest AI systems now cost $7.8 million to build ↗ · Tom's Hardware\\nNvidia Vera Rubin rack hit with 435% memory price surge ↗ · Wccftech\\nNvidia's $7.8 million Vera Rubin rack rewrites AI profit map as GPU share shrinks ↗ · BigGo Finance\\nNVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack memory costs explode as HBM4 and LPDDR5X prices cross $2 million ↗ · OnMSFT\\nEvery story in MarketScale Software & Technology starts with a company putting its solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers on the record. Buyers are already reading this topic. The only question is whose experts they find.\\nBuyers ask AI engines who to consider, and published expert answers are what those engines cite.\\nMarketScale gives Software & Technology B2B marketing teams a full content studio: record, produce, and distribute your own channel. No agency, no crew, no guessing.\\nBefore they reach out, Software & Technology buyers ask AI engines which vendors to trust. Explore how your experts, customers, and partners can become useful content for buyers and AI search.\\nThis article was produced through MarketScale. The same platform turns your solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers into the articles, video, and social content Software & Technology buyers are searching for. Start a free trial and see it with your own people. For qualifying companies, no credit card, no demo required.\\nUber deployed ChatGPT Work to more than 30,000 employees seven weeks after cutting about 3,300 jobs (10% of corporate staff). The company framed layoffs as organizational restructuring, while the rollout expands a pilot that embedded AI engineers with teams to automate pieces of their work.\\n01 Uber expanded ChatGPT Work from a 1,000-person pilot to over 30,000 employees across operations, finance, marketing, and product teams.\\n02 During the pilot, 80% of early adopters were non-engineers, and AI pods of 30 engineers paired with teams to automate work such as analyzing operational data, localizing training materials, and building dashboards.\\n03 Uber’s rollout shows AI deployments can go beyond licenses: curated integrations, embedded technical support, an internal skills library, and plans to measure adoption and outcomes.\\nTwo critical unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619) with CVSS 9.2 scores affected 34.4 million vulnerable services as of October 23, with no patches available for PHP 8.1 and older. Exploitation attempts reached millions within hours of disclosure, though confirmed compromises remain unproven, while attackers tested a Mirai-derived botnet variant against production servers.\\nUNICOM Engineering chief technologist Austin Hypes compares rear door, single-phase, two-phase and immersion cooling for AI racks on Flawless Execution. He says two-phase cold plates use dielectric refrigerant and can avoid chillers and evaporative towers in hot, dry sites, and immersion can keep servers up ten to 15 minutes and sometimes an hour or more after a cooling loss. Schneider Electric engineers cite estimates that single-phase holds about 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market.\\n01 A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\\nStories like this one run on content MarketScale captures from real practitioners. See how your team's expertise becomes coverage in Software & Technology and beyond.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Morgan Stanley Research\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Morgan Stanley Research\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"MarketScale\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\",\n   \"period\": \"2028 — the release date SemiAnalysis reports Kyber has been pushed to from an original 2027; the question is settled when Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra first ships (the item supports the original-2027-vs-2028 timing).\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"product_node\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA shipment confirmation or a SemiAnalysis follow-up report on Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra first shipments and volume ramp; NVIDIA product roadmap disclosures at GTC.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 tracks first shipments of the earlier Vera Rubin generation by end-2026; this tracks the following Rubin Ultra generation and its Kyber rack (originally 2027, reportedly 2028). The timelines can move apart — Vera Rubin could ship on schedule while the Rubin Ultra/Kyber ramp slips, and NVIDIA publicly disputes the Kyber delay ('roadmap is intact'), so an analyst would bet on them separately.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d\",\n   \"publisher\": \"datacenterdynamics.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia pushes Kyber release to 2028 following manufacturing concerns – report\",\n   \"text\": \"By Charlotte Trueman\\nNvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported.\\nThe Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027.\\nKyber allows GPUs to be mounted in compute trays that sit vertically rather than horizontally, a design that Nvidia has described as being “like books on a shelf,” and which reportedly boosts density and reduces latency.\\nHowever, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules together have caused the setback.\\n“Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,” SemiAnalysis said, adding that Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks back-to-back in order to achieve a similar power output, has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its “odd design and heavy operational burden.”\\nThe manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted. All this leaves Nvidia with “no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,” SemiAnalysis said.\\nFirst announced at Nvidia’s GTC conference in 2025, Nvidia has previously said the liquid-cooled Kyber racks will offer a 4x performance improvement over its Blackwell NVL72 system – also known as Oberon – and consume some 600kW.\\nThe news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\nIn response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported, per DataCenterDynamics, that NVIDIA has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release to 2028 from an originally planned 2027 date. SemiAnalysis attributed the setback to manufacturability challenges in the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules, saying the 'Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028.'\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported.\",\n     \"The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027.\",\n     \"However, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules together have caused the setback.\",\n     \"“Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,” SemiAnalysis said\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's alternative plan to achieve similar power output by placing two Oberon racks back-to-back was scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against what the firm described as its 'odd design and heavy operational burden.'\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"adding that Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks back-to-back in order to achieve a similar power output, has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its “odd design and heavy operational burden.”\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said the manufacturing issues will also impact NVIDIA's planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over co-packaged optics (CPO) between the NVSwitches, so the system will likely be delayed or limited to small volumes; the firm concluded NVIDIA has 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.'\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted.\",\n     \"All this leaves Nvidia with “no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,” SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis, in a separate report about a week before the item's July 6, 2026 publication, said NVIDIA had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of a two-chiplet design due to 'manufacturing execution concerns,' and that the two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.”\",\n     \"The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"In response to DataCenterDynamics' request for comment on the Kyber claims, an NVIDIA spokesperson said: 'Our roadmap is intact.'\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\",\n   \"period\": \"Next annual report (Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, likely filed early 2027); set by the reason — an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's annual report on Form 10-K, purchase commitments / commitments and contingencies note, for the fiscal year ending late January 2027.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks credit support NVIDIA extends to AI cloud partners (its exposure if they fail to pay); this thesis tracks NVIDIA's own commitments to pay suppliers, the cash it must fund if AI demand disappoints. The two can move in opposite directions and are disclosed in different notes, so an analyst would bet on them separately.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32\",\n   \"publisher\": \"247wallst.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n   \"text\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\\nBy Danielle Liverance\\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in.\\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\\nBut the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money, what executives are worried about, and what any of it means for the person checking their 401(k) on a Sunday afternoon. I write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\\nSK Hynix surged 14% Tuesday without a single company-specific headline, and the real test comes July 29 when its earnings either confirm or shatter the bullish memory narrative Micron just set.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. states that NVIDIA has $119 billion in supply commitments, which it says hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": [\n     {\n      \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n      \"period\": null,\n      \"value\": \"$119B\",\n      \"kind\": \"actual\",\n      \"basis\": null\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar 2026, measured at the hyperscalers' full-year 2026 disclosures (early 2027); next/current calendar year set by the item's 6 July 2026 date and by the rule that a shared quantity takes its release date.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"The four hyperscalers' quarterly earnings releases and full-year 2026 filings/earnings calls (including Amazon's disclosures on the size of its Trainium business) and their capex segment disclosures.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-4 tracks the aggregate data center capex of the big four; that total can rise while the share of it going to in-house silicon also rises, so NVIDIA's GPU revenue per dollar of hyperscaler capex can fall even as clean-4's answer turns positive. The two questions can move in opposite directions and an analyst would bet on them separately.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3\",\n   \"publisher\": \"247wallst.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\\nAmazon, Google, Microsoft, and Meta are all building proprietary silicon to cut costs and gain negotiating leverage over NVIDIA.\\nNVIDIA's NVLink Fusion pivot keeps its interconnect sticky even as hyperscalers shift compute to custom chips, but the strategy implicitly concedes the battle over GPUs.\\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells.\\nNVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18 and a high of $236.26. Q1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\\nAbout 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\nGrowth accelerates at NVIDIA’s scale. Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%. Networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\\nBlackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\\nAll of this says NVDA stock is set to keep delivering, as long as the broader market remains bullish.\\nThe customer list is the threat. Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative. Custom silicon “not only gives you a differentiation factor where you can be cheaper than competitors, but it also allows you to have some leverage over NVIDIA in negotiations.”\\nChina data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material. Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\\nNeither thesis has resolved. Blackwell ramps while Trainium and TPU volumes rise in parallel. NVIDIA’s NVLink Fusion strategy lets hyperscalers bolt custom accelerators onto NVIDIA’s fabric so the interconnect stays sticky even when compute does not.\\nWatch hyperscaler capex mix, whether networking growth stays vertical, and any China SKU announcement over the next two quarters. Any one breaking hard could tip the call.\\nNVIDIA trades at $196 against an analyst consensus target of ~$301.62 as of this writing, implying 53% upside. Coverage skews heavily positive with 10 Strong Buy, 48 Buy, 2 Hold, 1 Sell ratings. Forward P/E sits at 22x, trailing P/E at 29x.\\nPerformance is mixed. NVDA is up 4.59% year to date and 24.06% over the trailing year, but down 12.46% over the past month. The S&P 500 delivered a smaller trailing-year gain, so NVDA outperformed with more turbulence.\\nAt $196, NVIDIA remains a buy.\\nThe numbers do not argue for selling. A company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise. The numbers also do not argue for aggressively adding. Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list, and management’s NVLink Fusion pivot is an implicit acknowledgment that fighting custom silicon head-on loses.\\nBuy conviction requires durable evidence that networking and software capture margin even when compute goes custom, plus a China resolution. Sell conviction requires a hyperscaler capex reset or a Trainium/TPU disclosure that reframes NVIDIA as a supplier rather than the platform. Prediction markets show 80.5% conviction NVDA touches $192 in July and only 7% for a week close above $210, a range consistent with the fundamentals.\\nOwning NVIDIA at this price is defensible. Buying it aggressively requires believing the customer base will keep writing checks it is openly trying to stop writing.\\nOmor Ibne Ehsan is a writer at 24/7 Wall St. He is a self-taught investor with a focus on growth, cyclical, and dividend equities that have strong fundamentals, value, and long-term potential. He also has an interest in high-risk, high-reward investments such as penny stocks.\\nNvidia is letting a rival chipmaker plug directly into its own racks, and the move only looks like a concession until you follow the money through every layer of the data center.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"About 50% of NVIDIA's data center revenue — put elsewhere in the article at roughly half coming from six companies — comes from hyperscalers, the same companies funding Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA, per 24/7 Wall St.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"About 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\",\n     \"Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list, and management’s NVLink Fusion pivot is an implicit acknowledgment that fighting custom silicon head-on loses.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA (customer concentration), as reported by 24/7 Wall St.\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": [\n     {\n      \"measure\": \"share of data center revenue from hyperscalers\",\n      \"period\": \"Q1 FY27\",\n      \"value\": \"About 50%\",\n      \"kind\": \"actual\",\n      \"basis\": \"data center revenue; described elsewhere in the article as roughly half from six companies\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Amazon has disclosed that Trainium, its in-house AI chip business, is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and the article says every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA's data center segment also funds an alternative.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Amazon\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\",\n   \"period\": \"The 12 months from the Jul 7 2026 Bloomberg Intelligence survey (roughly H2 2026 – H1 2027); set by the survey's 'next 12 months' horizon, checked against a follow-up survey.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"A follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence (or comparable) survey of Chinese enterprise AI accelerator procurement, and China customs/import data for AI accelerators over the period.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-8 tracks revenue of the US hyperscalers' in-house accelerators (Trainium, TPU, Maia, MTIA), a US competition question. China's domestic-substitution share is a separate market driven by Beijing's policies and Chinese vendors (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon); the two can move independently — Chinese substitution can accelerate while US custom-silicon revenue stays flat, or vice versa.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b\",\n   \"publisher\": \"businesstimes.com.sg\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Chinese firms leave Nvidia for local AI suppliers, survey shows\",\n   \"text\": \"[SINGAPORE] Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators in favour of domestic silicon, underscoring how tensions with the US are reshaping the artificial intelligence infrastructure buildout and propelling Beijing’s ambitions to substitute American technology.\\nExecutives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\\nThis is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7).\\nIn addition, 80 per cent of executives said their total infrastructure spending is running over-budget this year, mostly because of the high cost of AI-related projects.\\nChina’s biggest AI infrastructure builders and their key suppliers – Tencent, Alibaba and Huawei Technologies – are best-positioned to take advantage of the shift.\\nAI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\\n“China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon,” said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\\nWhile Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\nChina is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\\nAt least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\nBut a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to reap the benefits.\\nThe bottlenecks are shifting from sheer computing power to securing supply of high-bandwidth memory chips that support rapid data transfer, according to the report. BLOOMBERG\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"A Bloomberg Intelligence survey of 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies, released Jul 7 2026, found executives plan to allocate 46% of their AI accelerator budget to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\",\n     \"This is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7).\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Chinese enterprise executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The same Bloomberg Intelligence survey found that AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were being evaluated by a large pool of surveyed respondents.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence survey\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Bloomberg Intelligence survey, Nvidia's China market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips — which Beijing has urged Chinese tech firms not to use — become harder to find and local firms take over, even though Nvidia's products remain popular.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While Nvidia’s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm’s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence survey\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-07\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) over the next five years to build data centers nationwide through a government-led initiative, and at least 80% of core technologies such as chips are to be supplied by domestic companies, according to a Bloomberg News report cited in the article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\",\n     \"At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"China government (reported by Bloomberg News)\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether proposed U.S. legislation/regulation to phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States is enacted (and its scope and timing).\",\n   \"period\": \"Pending regulatory/legislative decision — checked at NVIDIA's current fiscal year-end (fiscal year ending late January 2027), per the rule that a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Congressional legislative records and votes, and FCC or other federal agency rulemaking on Chinese-made lidar; enactment (or failure) of the proposed phase-out legislation.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"All open theses track NVIDIA's data-center AI GPU franchise (credit support to cloud partners, Vera Rubin/Rubin Ultra shipments, hyperscaler capex, AI-memory prices, custom-silicon competition, and China accelerator share). None addresses U.S. policy toward Chinese-made lidar sensors or NVIDIA's automotive/robotics DRIVE platform. A U.S. lidar phase-out could shift the sensor options and cost competitiveness of NVIDIA's autonomous-vehicle platform independently of the data-center GPU cycle.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:784c556d2e58ed09dd8aa7fc\",\n   \"publisher\": \"cnbc.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Chinese lidar maker with Nvidia ties accused of being cyber risk for U.S.\",\n   \"text\": \"Hesai Technology, a Shanghai-based manufacturer of the sensors that allow robots and self-driving vehicles to see, was blacklisted as a national security threat in 2024 by the U.S. Department of Defense, which designated Hesai as a Chinese military entity.\\nIts presence on the blacklist does not prevent U.S. companies from using Hesai's technology, and the company has been expanding its reach in the U.S., including via a partnership with Nvidia.\\nDavid Li, Hesai's co-founder and CEO, says his company does not work for the Chinese military and its products are safe.\\nRobots on the factory floor. Self-driving vehicles on the Las Vegas strip. Even a substitute for man's best friend — the robotic dog.\\nThey are all part of the physical artificial intelligence buildout that depends on high-tech, low-cost lidar, the critical sensors that allow these technologies to see their surroundings.\\nAnd at the heart of this buildout is Hesai Technology , a Shanghai-based lidar manufacturer blacklisted as a national security threat in 2024 by the U.S. Department of Defense, which designated Hesai as a Chinese military entity . While the blacklist prevents Hesai and the 187 other companies and subsidiaries on the list from securing Pentagon contracts, there is nothing illegal about using these products in nonmilitary applications. Hesai's presence on the blacklist does not prevent U.S. companies from using Hesai's technology.\\nGovernment officials and security experts say the use of Chinese lidar could open this new, critical infrastructure to cyberthreats with potentially serious consequences and become a backdoor for Beijing to access sensitive data collected by the lidar technology.\\nDavid Li , Hesai's co-founder and CEO, says the narrative that his company poses a threat is fiction.\\nDavid Li, Hesai's co-founder and CEO.\\n\\\"In the DOD case, I don't feel there is sufficient evidence, and it's not logical,\\\" Li said. \\\"We are frustrated by that.\\\"\\nIn his first extended interview about the blacklist designation, Li defended the company against allegations that its technology poses national security risks or could be used by the Chinese government to collect data.\\nDavid Li, Hesai's co-founder and CEO, speaks to CNBC's Melissa Lee in his first extended interview about his company's blacklist designation by the U.S. Department of Defense.\\nDespite the federal blacklist designation, Hesai's reach is growing. Under an expanded partnership between Hesai and Nvidia , Hesai sensors will be one of the options automakers can choose to integrate into Nvidia's autonomous vehicle platforms, which the chipmaker hopes will power the self-driving vehicle revolution.\\n\\\"Our vision is that some day, every single car, every single truck will be autonomous. And we have been working towards that future,\\\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said in his keynote address at the Consumer Electronics Show in January, when the expanded partnership was announced.\\nHuang has said that robotics, which includes self-driving cars, is the company's second most important growth category after artificial intelligence. In its latest annual filing, Nvidia reported automotive revenue for fiscal year 2026 was up 39% from a year prior, driven by the adoption of its self-driving platforms.\\nHesai is one of the dominant suppliers in the global autonomous technology ecosystem. Its sensors are integrated into several autonomous systems in addition to Nvidia's, including those of Amazon's robotaxi company, Zoox; autonomous trucking companies such as Waabi and Kodiak; autonomous vehicle technology company Nuro; and agricultural automation firm Agtonomy. The sensors can also be found at New York's John F. Kennedy International Airport, where they monitor passenger and traffic flow at security checkpoints and gate entrances, and even in autonomous lawn mowers.\\nNot everyone is convinced that these data-collecting sensors should be integrated into U.S. autonomous systems.\\nCraig Singleton is a senior director for the China Program at the Foundation for Defense of Democracies, a conservative Washington-based think tank known for being critical of the Chinese government. His research has concluded that there are security risks in Chinese-made sensors operating in U.S. systems, including that lidar sensors could enable Beijing \\\"to access sensitive U.S. data or disrupt critical operations.\\\"\\nLidar — which stands for \\\"light detection and ranging\\\" — works by firing laser pulses and measuring how long it takes for them to bounce off an object and back to the sensors. The sensors then combine thousands of these measurements to create a \\\"point cloud,\\\" or three-dimensional map, which allows autonomous machines to see and navigate their surroundings.\\nSingleton told CNBC that as Chinese lidar sensors become more prolific across the U.S., they will move closer to defense nodes, utility grids and airports.\\n\\\"That data is so sensitive and it's so precise that it could be weaponized by a hostile foreign power if they ever wanted to target our infrastructure,\\\" Singleton said.\\nQuestions about how and where Chinese-made lidar sensors are being used come alongside broader concerns about Chinese government oversight and the potential for that government to access data collected by Chinese companies.\\nThe U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission requires all China-based companies to disclose \\\"the risk of Chinese government intervention or control.\\\" In its SEC filings, Hesai has disclosed that the Chinese government has \\\"significant oversight in regulating our operations and may influence or intervene in our operations at any time.\\\"\\nSingleton said that in part means Hesai can be compelled to share data collected by its lidar sensors with the Chinese government.\\n\\\"Whether they want to transmit that information or not isn't a question, it's mandated by law,\\\" Singleton said.\\nLi said the company's sensors hold no data because they lack the memory capacity to do so. He said Hesai's partners are responsible for securing the data the sensors collect and that Hesai has no control over that.\\nLi also rejected concerns that the Chinese government could access data through the company.\\nBut Singleton said the rapid deployment of autonomous systems is outpacing scrutiny over potential security risks.\\n\\\"It's a tale as old as time with Chinese tech,\\\" he said.\\nCompanies in the U.S. have previously embraced low-cost Chinese technology, even from entities that had been blacklisted, only to later spend billions replacing it after the products raised national security concerns.\\nChinese telecommunications giant Huawei, for example, was placed on the Defense Department's blacklist in 2021, but that did not stop U.S. companies from using Huawei products. The Federal Communications Commission, which had designated Huawei a \\\" national security threat \\\" in 2020, forced U.S. companies to \\\" rip and replace \\\" Huawei products from their networks beginning in July 2021. Huawei challenged both the decision to bar it from securing federal contracts and FCC's designation of the company as a national security threat in court, but lost both cases.\\nLike Huawei, other Chinese companies have been found by the Defense Department to be national security risks only after their products made their way into U.S. homes and businesses.\\nDJI, the world's largest drone maker, and Wi-Fi router maker TP-Link Technologies, both based in Shenzhen, China, sold popular consumer goods. DJI was blacklisted in 2022 by the Pentagon for its ties to the Chinese government, and TP-Link Technologies was added to the list in June 2026.\\nDJI sued the Defense Department in 2024 to be removed from the blacklist, but a federal judge ruled there was \\\"substantial evidence\\\" that the company contributes to China's defense industrial base. DJI is appealing the decision.\\nUnlike Hesai, which remains free to sell lidar sensors commercially in the United States, a separate FCC ruling in December banned DJI from selling new products to U.S. consumers due to national security risks, though existing models remain legal to use.\\nTP-Link Technologies was also barred from selling new models after the FCC in March determined foreign-made routers pose \\\"an unacceptable risk to the national security of the United States.\\\"\\nNational security concerns surrounding foreign-made lidar sensors have also spurred academic research on the potential risks posed by these devices.\\nMiroslav Pajic, a professor at Duke University who studies vulnerabilities in lidar sensors, told CNBC it's \\\"easy to physically spoof lidar.\\\"\\nMiroslav Pajic, a professor at Duke University who studies vulnerabilities in lidar sensors.\\nPajic said any lidar sensor can be compromised with malware inserted at the factory during production or through firmware updates. Malware can be difficult to detect, since automakers and other manufacturers usually cannot access a lidar maker's proprietary source code and malware can remain dormant until triggered.\\nInside his lab at Duke, Pajic demonstrated one such attack.\\nOn a computer monitor displaying a lidar sensor's point cloud image, the room the sensor was capturing appeared exactly as expected. The sensor mapped its surroundings in real time, creating a 3-D picture of the space.\\nBut after Pajic activated malware embedded in the lidar unit, a person appeared in the sensor's point cloud. In reality, nobody had entered the room.\\nThe system generated a phantom person — a false object created entirely through manipulated sensor data.\\nPajic's lab has also conducted demonstrations that have the opposite effect: manipulating lidar data to remove real objects from a sensor's view. In that scenario, an autonomous system could fail to detect a pedestrian, vehicle or obstacle that is physically present.\\nPajic said similar attacks could theoretically be used against autonomous vehicle fleets operating in cities, causing them to malfunction.\\nWhen asked about the simulation, Li said a system can be designed to be vulnerable in a lab setting.\\nBut the risk of malfunction isn't just theoretical.\\nMichael Robbins, CEO of the Association for Uncrewed Vehicle Systems International — a trade organization that represents companies in the industry, including U.S. lidar competitors — said that in 2024 Hesai pushed a firmware update to all its lidar sensors. The firmware didn't take into account that 2024 was a leap year, so on Feb. 29, all of Hesai's lidar sensors stopped working.\\nMichael Robbins, CEO of the Association for Uncrewed Vehicle Systems International.\\n\\\"In that case it was by error, but that could also be done intentionally, where every lidar in use in the United States could be turned off, or it could be used against us in a nefarious way,\\\" Robbins said.\\nWhile the potential for a software error across autonomous vehicles is widespread, an error in lidar systems is grave considering its implementation in cars across America.\\nLi said that in the case of the leap year incident there was an overlooked coding bug in the firmware — not malware. He added that Hesai publishes all its firmware as open source data, so it can be publicly analyzed. In a statement to CNBC, Hesai said the issue was fixed within 24 hours.\\n\\\"We are making ourselves transparent on what exactly this is able to do,\\\" he said.\\nLi also said autonomous systems are designed with other sensors such as cameras and radar systems that can compensate for lidar failures.\\n\\\"If any of them stop, the cars will have to reevaluate the situation to know whether it's safe enough to continue the course or we're gonna have to pull over,\\\" he said.\\nHesai's sensors have also met the standards set by Tüv Rheinland and Dekra, which are independent third-party companies that specialize in product testing, safety validation and cybersecurity assessments.\\nHesai, which is publicly listed on both the Nasdaq and the Hong Kong Stock Exchange, is one of the world's biggest lidar manufacturers. It has one-third of the global automotive lidar market, the company told CNBC in a statement.\\nAnd autonomous driving is projected to become a massive global market. McKinsey & Company estimates the market potential for autonomous driving will be roughly $300 billion to $400 billion by 2035.\\nHesai's footprint inside the U.S. autonomous ecosystem has continued to grow.\\nCNBC reached out to Hesai's U.S. partners about their relationship with the company.\\nCNBC asked Nvidia more than a dozen questions, including whether it was aware that Hesai had been blacklisted by the Pentagon and what safeguards are in place to protect the data the sensors collect.\\nNvidia did not respond to CNBC's specific questions and instead provided a statement:\\n\\\"Automakers worldwide demand an open, vendor-agnostic reference architecture, so they can select components that are best for the markets they serve to build the safest cars. Our NVIDIA DRIVE Hyperion architecture provides that flexibility, ensuring that American industry competes worldwide, consistent with all regulatory and commercial requirements.\\\"\\nIn a statement, Kodiak wrote that its technology is designed \\\"so that Hesai does not have access to the data from their sensors or any data produced by Kodiak's autonomous system.\\\"\\nA spokesperson for Waabi wrote that its \\\"autonomous trucks utilize an array of sensors\\\" and that it does not \\\"comment on or disclose specific hardware being tested or used in our autonomous vehicles.\\\" The spokesperson added, \\\"We have rigorous data security protocols in place and thoroughly vet all third-party hardware to ensure the absolute integrity and safety of our systems as well as compliance with all applicable laws and regulations.\\\"\\nAgtonomy, Nuro and Zoox did not respond to CNBC's request for comment.\\nHesai's expansion has been driven in part by pricing.\\nHesai told CNBC it has reduced the cost of its lidar units from more than $10,000 each to less than $200. By comparison, U.S. lidar manufacturer Aeva told CNBC its automotive sensors cost \\\"in the few hundreds of dollars\\\" per unit.\\nIndustry analysts say that the pricing advantage is reshaping the market. A 2025 automotive lidar report by the Yole Group, a global advisory and market analysis firm, said Chinese firms such as Hesai are \\\"dominating due to cost, scale and government support\\\" while Western players \\\"face higher costs and slower adoption.\\\"\\nIn a statement to CNBC, Hesai said it's among the first in the industry to mass produce its lidar systems due to its \\\"innovation\\\" and \\\"automotive manufacturing capability.\\\"\\nCritics say those lower prices are only possible because of Chinese government assistance.\\n\\\"Chinese lidar companies have benefited from massive unfair state subsidies that have allowed them to scale production and control the market,\\\" said Singleton, of the Foundation for Defense of Democracies.\\nLi denied his company receives support from the Chinese government.\\n\\\"That's an accusation with no evidence,\\\" he said. \\\"When you see a player being able to build sensors at a much more affordable level, you just assume that they get help.\\\"\\nAccording to Hesai's 2025 annual filing with the U.S. SEC, the company received Chinese government subsidies, preferential tax rates of 15% versus the standard 25%, preferential borrowing rates below benchmark, and a tax break that lets it deduct 200% of its research and development costs.\\nIn a statement to CNBC, Hesai said those programs are not unusual, that \\\"governments worldwide commonly offer tax incentives to technology enterprises as a standard measure to stimulate innovation\\\" and that these incentives are \\\"broadly available to all qualifying companies in China, both domestic and foreign.\\\"\\nHesai also wrote that \\\"no government organization, including the Chinese government, holds any equity stake in Hesai.\\\"\\nAt the center of Hesai's legal battle with the Pentagon is whether the company contributes to China's military-civil fusion strategy, a national initiative aimed at integrating civilian and military technological development. The Pentagon says Hesai is part of that ecosystem.\\nAfter the Defense Department blacklisted Hesai in January 2024, the company sued the department in federal court over the designation, in May 2024.\\nIn court filings, the Defense Department cited several factors in support of the designation. Among them is that Hesai's Chinese headquarters are in Shanghai's Jiading district, an area associated with military-civil fusion initiatives.\\nLi rejected that argument. He said a military-civil fusion zone has not been clearly defined and that several of the companies in the area are American companies.\\n\\\"For lack of a better analogy, just because the Pentagon is in Virginia, you think that Virginia is a place full of military,\\\" Li said. \\\"Anybody operating in the state of Virginia becomes military.\\\"\\nThe Pentagon also pointed to supplier relationships between Hesai and China Electronics Technology Group Corporation, or CETC, a state-owned defense conglomerate tied to the People's Liberation Army. In a prospectus Hesai submitted for possible listing on the Shanghai Stock Market, CETC is listed as its third-largest supplier.\\nLi said none of the parts Hesai purchased from CETC have a military application.\\nIn court, Hesai also said its sensors are \\\"solely for commercial and civilian uses.\\\"\\nLi also told CNBC that Hesai's commercial agreement strictly prohibits customers from using its devices in military applications. However, he also said that, like any piece of hardware, once sensors leave the factory, it is impossible to physically control where they end up.\\nHesai lost its lawsuit against the Defense Department in 2025 and remains on the blacklist. The company is appealing the decision.\\nBeyond the courtroom, the company is also facing scrutiny from international policy groups.\\nIn July 2025, the Prague Security Studies Institute — a nonprofit, nongovernmental public policy organization — published a white paper on Hesai. Among the findings: Hesai's lidar sensor appeared on a military vehicle during a 2023 Chinese military television program . The program is called \\\"Land Unmanned Systems Challenge,\\\" a Chinese unmanned systems competition hosted by China's Army Equipment Department and organized by a military research institute.\\nChinese defense materials describing the event say its goal is to advance China's military-civil fusion strategy. Participants included military and civilian universities, state-owned enterprises, private companies and research institutes testing unmanned vehicles.\\nLi confirmed that the sensor that appeared in the footage was Hesai lidar technology but said the company did not supply the sensor for the military competition.\\n\\\"We shipped probably hundreds of thousands of lidars like this,\\\" Li said. \\\"So, there is a possibility that they end up somewhere that we are completely unaware of in the aftermarket.\\\"\\nLi also said Hesai has no way to communicate with or trace the sensors once they are shipped out, that the sensors send the data to the company using them, not to Hesai.\\nOn Capitol Hill, lawmakers are raising alarms about the use of Chinese lidar technology in the United States, warning the sensors could create both cybersecurity and national security risks.\\n\\\"These sensors and the ability to transmit information is a huge concern,\\\" said U.S. Rep. John Moolenaar, R-Mich., chairman of the House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party. The committee's website says that it is \\\"committed to working on a bipartisan basis to build consensus on the threat posed by the Chinese Communist Party.\\\"\\nU.S. Rep. John Moolenaar, R-Mich., chairman of the House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party.\\nSome of the committee's work has focused on protecting U.S. automakers from Chinese competition.\\nThe committee has proposed legislation that would phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States, arguing that the Chinese Communist Party could otherwise exploit a fast-growing and strategically important industry.\\nIn May, Moolenaar introduced legislation that would ban Chinese vehicles from U.S. roads.\\nLi said his company's sensors are not capable of storing data and therefore could not transmit information to China. But Moolenaar said he is skeptical of those assurances, arguing that Chinese technology has been known to have back doors.\\n\\\"We've seen back doors in robots that would transmit information back to these Chinese Communist interests,\\\" he said.\\nNo proposed legislation or existing laws currently prohibit blacklisted Chinese companies from being listed on U.S. stock exchanges or prevent American investors from buying their shares.\\nMoolenaar said companies focused on growth and shareholder returns may not prioritize national security concerns in the same way policymakers do.\\n\\\"We need to consider what implications there are when we merge these Chinese technologies with American technologies,\\\" he said. \\\"Often legislation takes a while to catch up.\\\"\\nClarification: This article was updated to clarify that TP-Link Technologies, based in Shenzhen, China, was added to the Defense Department's blacklist in June 2026.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"On Capitol Hill, lawmakers led by Rep. John Moolenaar (R-Mich.), chairman of the House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party, warned that Chinese lidar sensors could create cybersecurity and national security risks; the committee has proposed legislation that would phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States, arguing the Chinese Communist Party could otherwise exploit a strategically important industry. The proposal is not yet law and no enactment deadline is given.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"On Capitol Hill, lawmakers are raising alarms about the use of Chinese lidar technology in the United States, warning the sensors could create both cybersecurity and national security risks.\",\n     \"The committee has proposed legislation that would phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States, arguing that the Chinese Communist Party could otherwise exploit a fast-growing and strategically important industry.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"U.S. House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party / Rep. John Moolenaar\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-11\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will China's government approve imports of Nvidia's H200 AI chips into China — and roughly how many — enabling Nvidia to begin generating H200 revenue in the country?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether Chinese authorities grant approval for Alibaba, ByteDance, DeepSeek and other firms to import Nvidia H200 chips (potentially a total below 200,000), as reflected in official Chinese government decisions and in Nvidia's disclosed China H200 revenue.\",\n   \"period\": \"Nvidia's current fiscal year-end (fiscal year ending late January 2027), checked at its next annual report/10-K; set by the rule that a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"A Chinese government/regulator decision or announcement on H200 import approvals, and Nvidia's disclosure (10-Q/10-K or earnings call) of H200 revenue in China.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-9 tracks a 12-month, survey-based outcome — the share of Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products versus Nvidia. This thesis tracks the discrete regulatory gate and permitted volume of H200 imports, which China decides on its own timetable: China could approve a batch of H200 imports (and Nvidia near-term China revenue) while the longer-run domestic budget share still rises toward 46%, and vice versa, so the two answers can diverge.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c\",\n   \"publisher\": \"straitstimes.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   \"title\": \"China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\",\n   \"text\": \"China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models.\\nChina will allow top AI companies like Alibaba and ByteDance to buy limited amounts of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing previous restrictions.\\nChinese firms must request approval specifying the number of chips and reasons for purchase, with a possible total below 200,000.\\nThe move responds to high AI demand but aims to protect China's chip industry and address cybersecurity concerns amid US-China tech tensions.\\nChina plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site.\\nChinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .\\nThe companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information.\\nThe chips have emerged as a geopolitical flashpoint for the world’s two largest economies.\\nThough US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell the processors to China in December, the Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country.\\nNvidia shares climbed 3.7 per cent to US$204.12 (S$264) on July 8 , part of a broader chip rally. It marked the stock’s biggest one-day rise in more than a month.\\nThough Nvidia is now up 9.4 per cent in 2026 , that pales in comparison with the gains of most major semiconductor peers.\\nIn a statement, Chinese embassy spokesperson Liu Chang said that the country’s position on US chip exports has been consistent.\\n“We advocate that China and the United States achieve mutual benefit and win-win outcomes through cooperation, and we oppose the politicisation, instrumentalisation, and weaponisation of technological and economic issues,” he said. “We stand ready to work with all parties to jointly safeguard the stability of global industrial and supply chains.”\\nA representative for Santa Clara, California-based Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment.\\nThe H200 import snags hinged in part on Chinese concerns that a flood of American-designed AI processors would hinder the government’s long-held goal of developing an indigenous chip industry. Beijing has also cited fears that allowing American-made chips into the local market could pose a cybersecurity risk.\\nBut soaring demand for AI processors helped spur the Chinese authorities to allow some H200 purchases, according to the Information, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter.\\nLabs in China have been struggling, like their US counterparts, to find enough computing power to meet their growing needs to train and run new models.\\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models. It was the most powerful AI chip on the market until the introduction of the company’s next-generation Blackwell models in late 2024.\\nNvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 . Washington has continued to restrict sales of more capable AI chips to China on national security grounds.\\nThe Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported. When it comes to AI infrastructure, that is not a large number. A single data centre can use more than 400,000 Blackwell processors.\\nIn May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.\\n“We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The Information reported on 8 July 2026, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter, that Chinese officials have told Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek they will be permitted to buy a limited quantity of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing prior restrictions. Companies must request approval stating how many chips they need and why; the Chinese government is still deciding the total, which may end up below 200,000 chips. The move is described as responding to soaring AI demand while aiming to protect China's domestic chip industry and address cybersecurity concerns.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site.\",\n     \"Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .\",\n     \"The companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information.\",\n     \"But soaring demand for AI processors helped spur the Chinese authorities to allow some H200 purchases, according to the Information, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter.\",\n     \"The Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported.\",\n     \"The move responds to high AI demand but aims to protect China's chip industry and address cybersecurity concerns amid US-China tech tensions.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"The Information\",\n    \"tier\": \"rumour\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-08\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that U.S. President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell H200 processors to China in December, but that Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Though US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell the processors to China in December, the Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"U.S. government (President Donald Trump)\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress said in May that the H200 chips had not yet generated any revenue for the company in China, and that Nvidia was uncertain whether any imports would be allowed into the country.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.\",\n     \"“We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Nvidia (Colette Kress, CFO)\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u1\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-12\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will France's competition authority (Autorité de la concurrence) conclude its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU-market practices by the check date, and will it impose remedies or fines?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The French competition authority's conclusion of its antitrust investigation into alleged anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets, and any remedies or fines imposed.\",\n   \"period\": \"Nvidia's current fiscal year-end (fiscal year ending late January 2027); set by the rule that a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"The French competition authority's published decision/conclusion of its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU practices.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-11 tracks a Chinese government decision on H200 import approvals (market access in China); this tracks a European antitrust-conduct decision with potential remedies/fines affecting Nvidia's conduct and competitive position in the EU cloud-AI/GPU market. The two decisions are made by different authorities on different questions and can move entirely independently.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86\",\n   \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Bears Add to Wagers as Competition Threatens Its Dominance: Short Seller Tracker\",\n   \"text\": \"Meta Says AI Compute Isn’t Enough: Is the Infrastructure Selloff Over?\\n5.3M Views · 140 Posts\\n$NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ faced mounting pressure after bears added to their wagers against the chip giant amid increasing competition that threatened to challenge its dominance in the market for semiconductors that power artificial intelligence workloads.\\nTrading in borrowed Nvidia shares that were sold short rose to 13.61 million shares Wednesday, from 11.69 million shares a day earlier, exchange data tracked by moomoo showed. The latest figure represented 9.2% of the shares that changed hands yesterday, when the stock advanced 3.7%, the biggest gain in five weeks. Shorting involves shares sold without the seller owning them, which is a common practice used to profit from a potential decline in a stock's price.\\n$Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)$ to manufacture the AI chips, according to the report.\\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\nStill, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\\nNVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\nRegulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\\nShare your thoughts on Nvidia in the comments section. Are retail investors losing their faith in Nvidia? Let your voice be heard by voting below.\\nComments (4)\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The French competition authority announced Thursday that its antitrust investigation into Nvidia is nearing a conclusion; the probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Regulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"French competition authority (Autorité de la concurrence)\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\",\n   \"period\": \"2027-12-31 check date, within 18 months of the 2026-07-01 item\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's annual report/10-K guarantee and credit-support disclosures, and any 8-K/10-Q reporting signed credit-support agreements.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"No open thesis exists; this question tracks NVIDIA's own contingent credit exposure from supporting AI cloud capacity, which can move independently of GPU demand or product revenue.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout\",\n   \"publisher\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑scale AI services.\\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\\nTo address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced it is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories under a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\",\n     \"To address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\",\n     \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\",\n   \"period\": \"2026-12-31 check date, based on the item's statement, published 2026-07-02, that Vera Rubin could begin shipping later this year.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"product_node\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA quarterly earnings release, 10-Q/10-K, or official product shipment announcement confirming first Vera Rubin shipments.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Vera Rubin's first-shipment timing depends on product execution and can move independently of whether NVIDIA extends guarantees or credit support to AI cloud partners, which clean-1 tracks.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-02\",\n   \"title\": \"Microsoft vs. Nvidia: Which Is the Better AI Stock to Own for the Next 3 Years?\",\n   \"text\": \"Microsoft ( MSFT +1.28% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) are two heavyweights in the technology world and key players in artificial intelligence (AI). Microsoft seems to have its hand in every tech market segment, especially anything involving enterprise customers. Meanwhile, Nvidia has become the de facto leader in GPU chips used in AI data centers.\\nBoth stocks have made investors very wealthy over the years. But which of these top AI stocks is the better buy for the next three years? This article will explain why I like Microsoft a tad more. Although, as you'll see below, it's hard to go wrong with either one.\\nVera Rubin likely means Nvidia has more growth ahead\\nNvidia has enjoyed one of the most impressive growth spurts in history over these past few years as the AI build-out juiced demand for its GPUs. This supercycle continues to rage on. Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\nWall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year. Nvidia's valuation is already well below its high at less than 19 times sales, and it might become much cheaper as these next 24 months play out.\\nMicrosoft is pivoting to capitalize on its enterprise customer base\\nMicrosoft originally tied its AI hopes to OpenAI. Although Microsoft has done well with its OpenAI investment and enjoyed significant cloud computing growth as a result of the partnership, it has still struggled to establish itself as a key AI player. Users, especially enterprises, have opted for Anthropic's Claude among other AI models. As a result, Microsoft shifted to a multi-model strategy and has reportedly considered adding open-source models to Copilot Cowork to lower token costs.\\nInvestors should applaud Microsoft's willingness to adapt. The company has deeply entrenched relationships with the enterprise world, which gives Microsoft more margin for error in fine-tuning its AI strategy than most companies. Plus, Microsoft's Azure remains a formidable growth engine, with a staggering $627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations.\\nNvidia's data center business has grown so much that the stock has become a concentrated investment in this ongoing supercycle. That means Nvidia has a sky-high ceiling with Vera Rubin shipping soon, but it's riskier in that the floor is much lower if companies stop pouring all this money into AI infrastructure. Meanwhile, Microsoft remains highly diversified across software, cloud computing, and AI. That lowers the company's ceiling, but it's also the safer stock.\\nMicrosoft's impressive track record typically earns it a premium valuation. The stock has traded at an average of almost 33 times its trailing-12-month earnings over the past decade, but trades at just 22 times its earnings today. Meanwhile, Wall Street analysts expect the company to grow earnings by 16% to 17% annually over the next three to five years.\\nWhile some may opt for Nvidia's immense growth potential , Microsoft rarely offers a valuation compelling enough to buy into.\\nJustin Pope is a contributing Motley Fool stock market analyst covering information technology, consumer discretionary, consumer staples, and industrials. Prior to The Motley Fool, Justin was a business manager for an industrial company.\\nBy Justin Pope – Jul 2, 2026 at 12:43PM EST\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year, according to the Motley Fool article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027, according to the Motley Fool article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO)\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": [\n     {\n      \"measure\": \"orders\",\n      \"period\": \"through 2027\",\n      \"value\": \"$1 trillion\",\n      \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n      \"basis\": \"for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips\"\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal year-end (FY2027, late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision; the item (published 2026-07-02) gives no date for Beijing's decision.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"An official Chinese regulatory/government decision on H200 imports (e.g. CAC/MOFCOM announcement or licensing) and/or NVIDIA's quarterly filing or earnings disclosure stating whether H200 sales into China have resumed; also Chinese customs/import data.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Vera Rubin's first shipments (clean-2) depend on NVIDIA's own execution and its foundry/packaging supply and can happen regardless of China; China access depends on a decision by Beijing and can stay closed even with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule. Each independently moves data-center revenue, so an analyst would hold separate cards.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02\",\n   \"publisher\": \"thewirechina.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-02\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Uses The Specter of Huawei to Make Its Chip Exports Case\",\n   \"text\": \"Before Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S. government that Huawei could have enough chips to satisfy global demand.\\nBy Noah Berman\\nWhen Nvidia pitched the Trump administration last year to let it sell advanced AI chips to China, the world’s biggest chipmaker issued a clear warning: Huawei, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company warned that Huawei’s stockpile of chips, along with growing capacity at its manufacturing partner Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. (SMIC), give it “the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.”\\n“If Nvidia is kept out of the China market, Huawei production and sales will soar,” the report said, although it did not specify a timeframe or refer to the differences in performance between Nvidia and Huawei chips.\\nThe pitch — alongside Nvidia Chief Executive Jensen Huang’s personal diplomacy — worked. President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\\nNvidia’s analysis sheds light on how the world’s most valuable company has presented Huawei’s growing capabilities as justification for it to be able to sell more to China, the world’s second-largest market for chips after the U.S.\\nNvidia is attempting to “scare U.S. policymakers that Huawei is this great big threat,” says Gregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls. “They are, but not in the way Nvidia is claiming.”\\n“[But] there is no apples-to-apples comparison between Nvidia chips and Huawei chips,” says Allen, who supports export controls. “Having a lot of chips is not a synonym for being competitive in the marketplace.”\\nSince the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022, Nvidia’s market share in China has fallen to zero, Huang has said .\\nIn the report it presented to the government, Nvidia estimated that Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and that it would sell 1 million in 2025. It also estimated that by the end of this year SMIC — which manufactures chips based on Huawei’s design and technology — will have made more than 700,000 Ascend 910C chips, one of Huawei’s most advanced, with production reaching 50,000 of these chips per month. By 2027, the report said, SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei’s less advanced 910B chips.\\nHuawei simply does not pose a competitive threat to Nvidia globally right now. Huawei’s chips are nowhere near as good, and it can’t make nearly enough of them.\\nChris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations\\nThose numbers far exceed Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick’s estimate in congressional testimony last June that China could make just 200,000 advanced chips in 2025.\\nA Nvidia spokesperson declined to comment on the report. “The ability of China’s domestic ecosystem to yield and scale competitive AI systems is a fact, evidenced by leading Chinese models that are optimized to perform on domestic hardware,” the spokesperson said.\\nBut even on Nvidia’s numbers, Huawei would remain way short of the American company’s chip output, let alone being able to “satisfy global demand.”\\nIn a report published in December, Chris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations, estimated that if Huawei made 800,000 Ascend 910C chips in 2025 — a figure at the high end of several estimates — that would amount to just 5.3 percent of the total processing power of Nvidia’s chip production that year.\\nMcGuire also estimates that Nvidia’s lead by performance will only grow, even if Huawei were to make 2 million AI chips this year and 4 million in 2027. Industry analysts measure performance by how many calculations a chip can do per second and how fast it can move data around, with Nvidia’s best chips capable of doing far more than those of Huawei.\\n“Huawei simply does not pose a competitive threat to Nvidia globally right now,” says McGuire, formerly a national security official who helped design U.S. chip controls. “Huawei’s chips are nowhere near as good, and it can’t make nearly enough of them.”\\nIronically, he adds, Nvidia has a lead over Huawei largely because of U.S. export controls on semiconductor manufacturing equipment, the lack of which has degraded China’s ability to make AI chips.\\nNvidia’s analysis did not contain any of its own new modeling of Huawei’s capabilities. Instead its report cited estimates from outside research groups, including think tank the Center for Strategic and International Studies, the consultancy Semianalysis, and a handful of Wall Street firms including Bernstein and Morgan Stanley.\\nThe report also cited Reuters reporting that Huawei had built a stockpile of between 2 and 3 million of its own AI chips made by Taiwanese chipmaker TSMC, which it had obtained via illegal diversion by the Chinese company Sophgo. Companies such as TSMC that make chips using American technology must follow U.S. export controls, which effectively prevent sales to Huawei.\\nBy choosing to highlight that stockpile in its report, Nvidia is in fact underscoring how export controls are succeeding in holding Huawei back, says Lennart Heim , an independent researcher who studies chip and AI policy.\\n“If they were able to produce that many chips themselves, why would they go to TSMC?” he asks. Even though figures such as 2 to 3 million AI chips sound significant, “this number is tiny” relative to the more than 60 million 910C-equivalent chips that the U.S. will have produced between January 2025 and the end of this year, Heim adds.\\nHuawei made just 6 percent of the total AI chips sold worldwide during the first quarter of this year, and half that amount after adjusting for the fact that the company’s chips are not as powerful as Nvidia’s, according to Epoch AI, a research institute.\\nWhile Nvidia’s report framed its argument on AI chip export controls around its competition with Huawei, others argue that a more important issue is that the restrictions will incentivize Chinese companies to try to catch up with their American rivals.\\n“There are good arguments against export controls,” Heim says. But instead of articulating those, Nvidia is “trying to use technical terms to confuse people.”\\nNvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe. I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.\\nGregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls\\n“Huawei has no way right now to satisfy global demand,” he adds.\\nHuawei has certainly been trying to improve its offering. In March, it introduced a new chip, the 950PR, and is preparing to launch another later this year. Yet even those chips fail to match the quality of the H200 because they have slower memory , according to Heim. They lag even further behind Nvidia’s most advanced Blackwell chips .\\nHuawei and SMIC did not respond to requests for comment.\\nThe Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\\n“We sell the first, best and most of our technology to America and offer competitive products in China to commercial users vetted and approved by the U.S. government,” says the Nvidia spokesperson. “National security is first and foremost. Where commercial opportunity conflicts with U.S. national security, national security comes first.”\\nBut Nvidia is losing sympathy in Washington, says Allen, the analyst.\\n“Nvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe,” he says. “I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.”\\nNoah Berman is a staff writer for The Wire based in New York. He previously wrote about economics and technology at the Council on Foreign Relations. His work has appeared in the Boston Globe and PBS News . He graduated from Georgetown University.\\nBy Michael Kovrig\\nA new book provides searing testimonials of how the Chinese Communist Party’s thought remolding and coercive interrogations continue under the legal obfuscation of “residential surveillance at a designated location”.\\nBy Savannah Billman\\nBy Eliot Chen\\nThe former State Department official in the Biden administration talks about leading the first talks with China on AI, and what the realistic goals should be of any dialogue between the two superpowers about the...\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"President Trump announced that his administration would allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips to China in December 2025, after the Biden administration had restricted sales of the chip (launched in 2023) under export controls.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"President Trump / Trump administration\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said that NVIDIA's market share in China has fallen to zero since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022, Nvidia’s market share in China has fallen to zero, Huang has said .\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Jensen Huang, CEO of NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"As of publication (2026-07-02), the Chinese government has locked NVIDIA out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared the path for its H200 exports.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Chinese government\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026, settled by the hyperscalers' Q4 2026 earnings and capex disclosures (reported early 2027).\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' quarterly earnings releases and capital-expenditure disclosures for calendar Q2-Q4 2026 (full-year 2026 capex reported in their Q4 2026 results).\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Hyperscaler capex budgets are set by the buyers' AI buildout plans and can be raised or cut regardless of whether NVIDIA's Vera Rubin ships on schedule; equally, confirmation of Vera Rubin shipments would not by itself settle the aggregate 2026 spend, so the two questions can move apart.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-04\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) investors have had it good for several years. In 2023, 2024, and 2025, it produced market-crushing returns, making it one of the best stocks to own during each of those years. However, 2026 hasn't been that way. The stock is up 5% so far in 2026, which isn't nothing, but compared to the returns that Nvidia investors have enjoyed in recent years, it feels like an absolute failure. On another note, its market-crushing status is in jeopardy for 2026, as the S&P 500 is up nearly 10% for the year.\\nWith Nvidia stock looking like a dud this year, many investors are wondering whether now is the time to cash out and look for other investment opportunities, or to load up on Nvidia shares. Let's take a look, as not everything is as it appears with Nvidia.\\nNvidia was the top AI stock to own over the past three years, due to its market position. Nvidia designs graphics processing units (GPUs) and the hardware that supports them. Although it doesn't own any of the fabrication process itself, it works with several companies that manufacture its GPUs, which it then sells to a wide range of customers.\\nBefore 2023, Nvidia's business was more balanced. However, the massive demand for AI data centers has driven this lineup of GPUs into high demand, and Nvidia's product line has become the go-to for several AI hyperscalers and cloud providers. Nvidia has a complete ecosystem of products that gives its clients everything they need to run an Nvidia-powered data center, locking in clients once they've chosen to use its products.\\nThis bodes well for the future, as AI hyperscalers are spending a massive amount of money this year and beyond to continue building out their AI computing footprints. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion. That means more growth is coming to Nvidia, and the investing story is far from complete. Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\\nHowever, the stock price doesn't reflect that.\\nNvidia trades at 21.7 times forward earnings, essentially the same price tag as the S&P 500. This conveys that the market believes Nvidia will be a market-average stock after 2026's growth is accounted for. However, Wall Street analysts don't believe that, as they're guiding for major growth next year. That's why the stock looks so cheap when next year's earnings are used to value the stock.\\nAs a result, I think Nvidia is an excellent stock to buy now. None of next year's growth has been priced in, which could lead to strong returns toward the end of this year.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The fool.com article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, as they continue building out their AI computing footprints. No individual hyperscaler or source is named for the figure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This bodes well for the future, as AI hyperscalers are spending a massive amount of money this year and beyond to continue building out their AI computing footprints. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers (not individually named)\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-04\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion next year (2027), according to the fool.com article. This is NVIDIA's forecast, not a reported outcome.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\",\n   \"period\": \"2027-12-31 check date — the item (published 2026-07-05) says Kyber was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 and has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, so end-2027 discriminates the original schedule from the reported delay.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"product_node\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's own shipment and roadmap disclosures — GTC keynote/product briefings, quarterly earnings commentary on Rubin Ultra revenue timing, and cloud-partner (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud) deployment confirmations — checked at year-end 2027.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-2 asks only whether the current Vera Rubin platform begins shipping by end-2026; this asks whether the following-generation Kyber NVL144/Rubin Ultra rack ships in 2027 or 2028. Vera Rubin can ship on time while the next-generation rack slips (as SemiAnalysis reports), and the reverse is also possible, so the two answers move independently and an analyst would bet on them separately.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587\",\n   \"publisher\": \"cnbc.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n   \"text\": \"Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\\nNvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\nShares of the artificial intelligence chip giant climbed about 1% on Monday.\\nKyber is a server cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips into a single unit so they can work together as one giant computer, providing the horsepower AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\\nThe design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\\n\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\\nThe reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits.\\nA backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\\nThat leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\nThe delays \\\"should not be over analyzed as affecting the long-term criticality of Nvidia to AI data infrastructure buildouts,\\\" said Paul Triolo, a partner at consultancy DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group, noting the company \\\"has faced these kinds of challenges before, and has worked with vendors to overcome technical issues.\\\"\\nWith power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \\\"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\\\"\\nNvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud. SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\nWhile Huawei and other domestic manufacturers \\\"will potentially gain some time,\\\" China's hardware ecosystem is increasingly diverging from — rather than chasing — the Nvidia-led model, Triolo said. \\\"The issue is no longer really catching up, but how good will China's alternative AI stack be by 2030,\\\" he said.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan to bolt two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to reach similar power has been cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its odd design and heavy operational burden.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027); the item gives no date, so the date is set by the reason — an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K, in its contractual obligations / purchase commitments disclosure.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 asks how much contingent credit support NVIDIA extends to AI cloud partners; this asks how much NVIDIA itself has committed to buy in its own name. The two move independently: NVIDIA can carry heavy supply purchase commitments while backing no partner, or guarantee partner capacity while its own purchase commitments stay flat, and they hit cash risk through different channels.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32\",\n   \"publisher\": \"247wallst.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n   \"text\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\\nBy Danielle Liverance\\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in.\\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\\nBut the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money, what executives are worried about, and what any of it means for the person checking their 401(k) on a Sunday afternoon. I write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\\nSK Hynix surged 14% Tuesday without a single company-specific headline, and the real test comes July 29 when its earnings either confirm or shatter the bullish memory narrative Micron just set.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. reports that NVIDIA carries $119 billion in supply commitments, which it says depend on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": [\n     {\n      \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n      \"period\": null,\n      \"value\": \"$119B\",\n      \"kind\": \"actual\",\n      \"basis\": \"amount the item says hinges on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools\"\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026; settled by the hyperscalers' Q4 2026 earnings and capex/custom-silicon disclosures reported in early 2027.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Hyperscaler quarterly earnings and capex disclosures, including any custom-silicon program spending or segment revenue for Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA, plus their Q4 2026 reports.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Clean-4 tracks the big four's total data-center capital expenditures; this thesis tracks the custom-accelerator share of that spending. Total capex can rise while the custom share also rises, so NVIDIA's data-center revenue can weaken even if clean-4's capex total beats expectations—or vice versa. An analyst would bet on the mix separately because it determines whether hyperscaler spending accrues to NVIDIA.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3\",\n   \"publisher\": \"247wallst.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\\nAmazon, Google, Microsoft, and Meta are all building proprietary silicon to cut costs and gain negotiating leverage over NVIDIA.\\nNVIDIA's NVLink Fusion pivot keeps its interconnect sticky even as hyperscalers shift compute to custom chips, but the strategy implicitly concedes the battle over GPUs.\\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells.\\nNVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18 and a high of $236.26. Q1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\\nAbout 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\nGrowth accelerates at NVIDIA’s scale. Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%. Networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\\nBlackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\\nAll of this says NVDA stock is set to keep delivering, as long as the broader market remains bullish.\\nThe customer list is the threat. Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative. Custom silicon “not only gives you a differentiation factor where you can be cheaper than competitors, but it also allows you to have some leverage over NVIDIA in negotiations.”\\nChina data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia’s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at “close to about $50 billion in the future,” would be material. Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\\nNeither thesis has resolved. Blackwell ramps while Trainium and TPU volumes rise in parallel. NVIDIA’s NVLink Fusion strategy lets hyperscalers bolt custom accelerators onto NVIDIA’s fabric so the interconnect stays sticky even when compute does not.\\nWatch hyperscaler capex mix, whether networking growth stays vertical, and any China SKU announcement over the next two quarters. Any one breaking hard could tip the call.\\nNVIDIA trades at $196 against an analyst consensus target of ~$301.62 as of this writing, implying 53% upside. Coverage skews heavily positive with 10 Strong Buy, 48 Buy, 2 Hold, 1 Sell ratings. Forward P/E sits at 22x, trailing P/E at 29x.\\nPerformance is mixed. NVDA is up 4.59% year to date and 24.06% over the trailing year, but down 12.46% over the past month. The S&P 500 delivered a smaller trailing-year gain, so NVDA outperformed with more turbulence.\\nAt $196, NVIDIA remains a buy.\\nThe numbers do not argue for selling. A company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise. The numbers also do not argue for aggressively adding. Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list, and management’s NVLink Fusion pivot is an implicit acknowledgment that fighting custom silicon head-on loses.\\nBuy conviction requires durable evidence that networking and software capture margin even when compute goes custom, plus a China resolution. Sell conviction requires a hyperscaler capex reset or a Trainium/TPU disclosure that reframes NVIDIA as a supplier rather than the platform. Prediction markets show 80.5% conviction NVDA touches $192 in July and only 7% for a week close above $210, a range consistent with the fundamentals.\\nOwning NVIDIA at this price is defensible. Buying it aggressively requires believing the customer base will keep writing checks it is openly trying to stop writing.\\nOmor Ibne Ehsan is a writer at 24/7 Wall St. He is a self-taught investor with a focus on growth, cyclical, and dividend equities that have strong fundamentals, value, and long-term potential. He also has an interest in high-risk, high-reward investments such as penny stocks.\\nNvidia is letting a rival chipmaker plug directly into its own racks, and the move only looks like a concession until you follow the money through every layer of the data center.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The article estimates that about 50% of NVIDIA's Q1 FY27 data center revenue comes from hyperscalers—the same companies funding Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"About 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"247wallst.com (Omor Ibne Ehsan)\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Amazon has disclosed that Trainium, its custom AI chip, is now a multi-billion-dollar business; the article adds that every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA's data center segment also funds an alternative.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business, and every hyperscaler funding NVIDIA’s data center segment also funds an alternative.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Amazon\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"What share of Chinese companies' AI-accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers (e.g. Huawei Ascend and DeepSeek's in-house chips) over the next 12 months, and how much will that displace NVIDIA's China demand?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The share of Chinese AI-accelerator budgets allocated to domestic suppliers, versus roughly 30% today, as reported in industry surveys/market research and reflected in NVIDIA's disclosed China revenue.\",\n   \"period\": \"12 months from the item (by ~mid-2027), per the item's statement that Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Industry surveys/market-research estimates of Chinese AI-accelerator spending shares and NVIDIA's disclosed China revenue over the next 12 months (to mid-2027).\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Beijing's decision to permit (or bar) H200 sales and Chinese buyers' allocation of budgets to domestic suppliers are separate outcomes: Beijing could clear H200 while budgets keep shifting to Huawei/DeepSeek (China revenue stays low), or domestic chips could underdeliver while Beijing continues to bar H200, leaving pent-up demand. Their answers can move apart, so an analyst would track them separately.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a\",\n   \"publisher\": \"mlq.ai\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence\",\n   \"text\": \"DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\\nThe project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\\nNvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]\\nDeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion, its first outside capital raise [4]\\nU.S. export controls bar Chinese designers from accessing the most advanced overseas foundries and high-bandwidth memory, posing a major manufacturing hurdle [2]\\nDeepSeek, the Hangzhou-based AI startup behind some of China's most advanced large language models, is developing its own custom AI chip, Reuters reported on Monday, citing three people with knowledge of the matter [1] . The chip is designed for inference — the phase where trained models generate responses to user queries — rather than for training new models.\\nThe project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with DeepSeek quietly expanding its chip engineering team without public job postings [2] . The company is in discussions with chip design firms, semiconductor foundries, and memory suppliers, though no specific partners have been named.\\nThe move marks a significant strategic expansion for DeepSeek, which burst onto the global AI stage in January 2025 with models that rivaled U.S. frontrunners at a fraction of the compute cost. It comes weeks after the company closed a record $7.5 billion funding round — its first outside capital — at a valuation exceeding $50 billion, with backing from Tencent, CATL, and founder Liang Wenfeng himself [4] .\\nDeepSeek's chip targets the fastest-growing segment of AI computing. Inference workloads — running trained models for end users — now account for the majority of AI chip demand as companies deploy models at scale. Custom inference chips are typically cheaper and more power-efficient than the general-purpose GPUs sold by Nvidia, making them attractive for companies seeking to compress costs [2] .\\nThe strategy aligns with DeepSeek's broader cost-efficiency playbook. In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5] . Controlling its own silicon would give DeepSeek further leverage to undercut rivals on inference pricing.\\nDesigning a competitive AI chip typically takes years and billions of dollars in investment. For a Chinese company, the challenge is compounded by U.S. export controls that bar access to the most advanced overseas foundries and restrict shipments of high-bandwidth memory — a component critical to inference chip performance [2] .\\nAnalyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3] .\\nChinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5] . DeepSeek's chip effort fits squarely within that trend.\\nDeepSeek is not the only AI lab pursuing proprietary silicon. OpenAI and Anthropic have both disclosed custom chip programs, while Google has long run its TPU chips for both training and inference. Amazon designs its Trainium and Inferentia chips for AWS customers [2] .\\nWithin China, Huawei's Ascend line of AI accelerators has emerged as the primary domestic alternative to Nvidia. DeepSeek's in-house effort positions the startup as a competitor to Huawei in inference hardware — not just in AI models [5] .\\nNo timeline for tape-out or production has been disclosed. The project's early-stage status means a finished chip is likely years away, and manufacturing constraints under current U.S. export rules represent a structural bottleneck [2] .\\nDeepSeek's $7.5 billion war chest gives it the capital to sustain a multi-year hardware development effort. Founder Liang Wenfeng personally contributed roughly $2.9 billion to the round, and the deal's unusual structure — a limited partnership with a five-year lock-up for most investors — suggests the company is planning on a long horizon [4] .\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"DeepSeek is developing a custom AI inference chip (for generating model responses, not training), Reuters reported citing three unnamed sources. The project has been underway for about a year and remains in an early stage — DeepSeek is quietly recruiting chip engineers and in talks with foundries and memory suppliers, with no partners named. No tape-out or production timeline was disclosed; a finished chip is likely years away, and U.S. export controls barring the most advanced overseas foundries and high-bandwidth memory are a structural manufacturing bottleneck.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\",\n     \"The project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\",\n     \"No timeline for tape-out or production has been disclosed. The project's early-stage status means a finished chip is likely years away, and manufacturing constraints under current U.S. export rules represent a structural bottleneck [2]\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"DeepSeek\",\n    \"tier\": \"rumour\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"DeepSeek closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion — its first outside capital raise — backed by Tencent, CATL and founder Liang Wenfeng; Wenfeng personally contributed roughly $2.9 billion, and the deal uses a limited-partnership structure with a five-year lock-up for most investors. The item presents this war chest as capital backing DeepSeek's multi-year hardware (chip) development effort.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"DeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion, its first outside capital raise [4]\",\n     \"It comes weeks after the company closed a record $7.5 billion funding round — its first outside capital — at a valuation exceeding $50 billion, with backing from Tencent, CATL, and founder Liang Wenfeng himself [4] .\",\n     \"Founder Liang Wenfeng personally contributed roughly $2.9 billion to the round, and the deal's unusual structure — a limited partnership with a five-year lock-up for most investors — suggests the company is planning on a long horizon [4]\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"DeepSeek\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Analyst Richard Windey said the U.S. export rules that have effectively zeroed out NVIDIA's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Analyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3].\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Richard Windey\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5].\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": null,\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will France's competition authority reach a conclusion/decision — a fine, behavioral remedies or closure — in its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA's alleged anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets by NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027)?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The French competition authority's decision concluding its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA, including any fine or behavioral remedies imposed.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision; the item gives no date for the conclusion, only that it is 'nearing a conclusion'.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"The French competition authority's formal decision/announcement concluding its investigation, and NVIDIA's related disclosure of any fine or remedies.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 tracks Beijing's decision on whether to allow H200 sales into mainland China; this tracks a European antitrust decision on NVIDIA's cloud-AI/GPU conduct. They are different regulators, jurisdictions and subject matter and can move apart — China can grant (or keep barring) H200 access while France imposes remedies, or the reverse.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86\",\n   \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Bears Add to Wagers as Competition Threatens Its Dominance: Short Seller Tracker\",\n   \"text\": \"Meta Says AI Compute Isn’t Enough: Is the Infrastructure Selloff Over?\\n5.3M Views · 140 Posts\\n$NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ faced mounting pressure after bears added to their wagers against the chip giant amid increasing competition that threatened to challenge its dominance in the market for semiconductors that power artificial intelligence workloads.\\nTrading in borrowed Nvidia shares that were sold short rose to 13.61 million shares Wednesday, from 11.69 million shares a day earlier, exchange data tracked by moomoo showed. The latest figure represented 9.2% of the shares that changed hands yesterday, when the stock advanced 3.7%, the biggest gain in five weeks. Shorting involves shares sold without the seller owning them, which is a common practice used to profit from a potential decline in a stock's price.\\n$Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)$ to manufacture the AI chips, according to the report.\\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\nStill, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\\nNVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\nRegulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\\nShare your thoughts on Nvidia in the comments section. Are retail investors losing their faith in Nvidia? Let your voice be heard by voting below.\\nComments (4)\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The French competition authority announced on Thursday that its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA is nearing a conclusion; the probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Regulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"French competition authority\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u2\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How much new high-bandwidth memory (HBM) capacity will the three main producers (SK Hynix, Micron, Samsung) bring online, and will the HBM shortage keep memory prices at record highs through late 2027 — a supply and input-cost constraint on NVIDIA's GPU shipments?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The state of HBM supply and pricing through late 2027: whether any meaningful new HBM capacity comes online before then, and whether record-high HBM/memory prices persist.\",\n   \"period\": \"Late 2027 (the item, published 2026-07-10, says no new HBM supply arrives before late 2027, keeping the shortage and record prices into 2027); set by the item's forecast, settled by memory-market research and producers' capacity disclosures for 2027.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Memory-market research releases (e.g., TrendForce/Counterpoint HBM capacity and contract-price reports) and the HBM producers' (SK Hynix, Micron, Samsung) capacity and ramp disclosures covering 2027.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-6 tracks the dollar amount of NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments disclosed at its annual report (a company-specific disclosure); this thesis tracks the external aggregate HBM capacity/price environment that determines whether those purchases can be filled and at what input cost. They can move apart: NVIDIA's commitments could rise while external HBM supply loosens and prices fall, or fall while supply stays tight.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f\",\n   \"publisher\": \"247wallst.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n   \"title\": \"Memory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut\",\n   \"text\": \"Memory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut - 24/7 Wall St.\\nMemory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut\\nSK Hynix just pulled off one of the largest share sales in history, and one memory market expert says U.S. investors have been missing the single most critical bottleneck in the entire AI supply chain. Here is why that changes…\\nBy Thomas Richmond\\nNVIDIA's $82B quarterly revenue and Micron's 346% year-over-year sales surge confirm AI chips are driving an unprecedented memory demand cycle.\\nSK Hynix controls 58% of the HBM market, and its $26.5B Nasdaq debut finally gives U.S. investors direct access to NVIDIA's most critical chip supplier.\\nPartsinevelos warned that no new HBM supply arrives before late 2027, keeping record prices intact while $14B in passive index buying looms.\\nCNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos reports on SK Hynix's significant $26.5 billion Nasdaq debut, highlighting its crucial role in the AI memory market alongside NVIDIA. © 24/7 Wall St.\\nDuring a July 10 CNBC segment , Kristina Partsinevelos framed SK Hynix’s roughly $26.5 billion capital raise at $149 per American depositary share as a defining moment for the AI supply chain. The Korean memory giant controls approximately 58% of the high-bandwidth memory market, giving it a commanding position in the technology required to make NVIDIA’s most advanced chips work. The offering ranks as the second-largest share sale ever, trailing only SpaceX’s NASDAQ debut last month.\\nFor U.S. investors, the debut closes a long-standing accessibility gap. SK Hynix has been the essential HBM supplier to NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) throughout the AI buildout, yet trading in the Korean parent required navigating Seoul-listed shares. It also arrives as Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang’s “largest infrastructure expansion in human history” creates extraordinary demand for a product that remains in short supply.\\nSK Hynix Controls the Memory Powering the AI Revolution\\nThe core setup for SK Hynix, according to Partsinevelos: “SK Hynix is the world leader in high bandwidth memory . The memory that feeds AI chips with roughly 58% of that market. Micron and Samsung competitors split roughly 21% each … as per Counterpoint Research.” She added a blunter framing of the competitive stack: “SK Hynix is bigger, cheaper and closer to NVIDIA.”\\nPartsinevelos noted revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year, with the company committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea. A separate roughly $458 million from the U.S. CHIPS Act is earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\\nNew Supply Will Likely Not Arrive Until 2027\\nMemory prices have been incredibly strong due to tight supply. “None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now,” Partsinevelos said. Passive flows may amplify the near-term move: “Berkeley estimates the stock could see up to roughly $14 billion in passive buying alone as it enters the major indices just over the next few months.”\\nReaders looking for the broader thesis on companies riding this wave can dig into our Free Report: 7 Stocks Powering the AI Boom (That Aren’t Chipmakers) .\\nMicron Shows How Valuable AI Memory Has Become\\nSK Hynix’s U.S.-listed challenger, Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ), shows why memory investors are watching HBM share so closely. Micron posted Q3 FY2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year, with non-GAAP diluted EPS of $25.11 and GAAP gross margin of 84.6%. Guidance for the following quarter calls for revenue of $50.0 billion plus or minus $1.0 billion. CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in the Q3 release that results “reflect the strategic value of memory in the AI era.”\\nMicron’s HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and HBM4E is in development, with volume production expected in calendar 2027. Shares closed at $991.64 on July 9, up 247.66% year to date. Analysts maintain a consensus price target of $1,486, with the stock currently sporting a forward P/E of about 6.\\nNVIDIA’s Growth Is Creating an Unprecedented Demand Signal\\nOn the demand side, NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion. Shares closed at $202.78 on July 9. Every one of Nvidia’s Blackwell and Vera Rubin systems requires HBM stacks supplied at scale by SK Hynix today.\\nSK Hynix’s U.S. debut gives investors direct access to the company holding the strongest position in one of the AI buildout’s tightest bottlenecks. It controls roughly 58% of the HBM market, and new capacity will not materially alleviate the shortage before late 2027.\\nPartsinevelos closed with the industry’s most durable caveat: “The longer term risk, though, is that memory has never really met a supercycle that didn’t eventually crash .” History is unambiguous on that point. For now, the memory shortage is likely to persist into 2027, index inclusion should act as a mechanical bid for the stock, and the three players controlling roughly 95% of memory production remain in a pricing environment they have not enjoyed in over a decade.\\nThomas Richmond is a financial writer and content strategist with 5+ years of experience covering stocks and financial markets. He has published over 500 articles focused on individual stock analysis, helping investors better understand business fundamentals, stock valuations, and long-term opportunities.\\nSK Hynix crashed into bear-market territory in Seoul just days after a blockbuster U.S. debut, and Jim Cramer thinks Korean investors are missing something fundamental about where AI memory demand is actually headed.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"SK Hynix raised roughly $26.5 billion at $149 per American depositary share in a Nasdaq listing described as the second-largest share sale ever (trailing SpaceX), and controls approximately 58% of the high-bandwidth memory (HBM) market that supplies NVIDIA's most advanced chips, per CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos in a July 10, 2026 segment; Counterpoint Research puts Micron and Samsung at roughly 21% each.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"During a July 10 CNBC segment , Kristina Partsinevelos framed SK Hynix’s roughly $26.5 billion capital raise at $149 per American depositary share as a defining moment for the AI supply chain. The Korean memory giant controls approximately 58% of the high-bandwidth memory market, giving it a commanding position in the technology required to make NVIDIA’s most advanced chips work. The offering ranks as the second-largest share sale ever, trailing only SpaceX’s NASDAQ debut last month.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SK Hynix\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos said none of the new HBM supply arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high memory prices intact for now (July 10, 2026).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory prices have been incredibly strong due to tight supply. “None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now,” Partsinevelos said.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"CNBC (Kristina Partsinevelos)\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"SK Hynix's revenue is expected to roughly triple to $235 billion this year, and the company is committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea, with a separate roughly $458 million U.S. CHIPS Act award earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility, per CNBC's July 10, 2026 segment.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Partsinevelos noted revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year, with the company committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea. A separate roughly $458 million from the U.S. CHIPS Act is earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SK Hynix\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year, non-GAAP diluted EPS of $25.11 and GAAP gross margin of 84.6%, and guided the following quarter to revenue of $50.0 billion ± $1.0 billion; its HBM4 (1-beta DRAM) is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform and HBM4E volume production is expected in calendar 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron posted Q3 FY2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year, with non-GAAP diluted EPS of $25.11 and GAAP gross margin of 84.6%. Guidance for the following quarter calls for revenue of $50.0 billion plus or minus $1.0 billion.\",\n     \"Micron’s HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and HBM4E is in development, with volume production expected in calendar 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Micron Technology\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K), expected early 2027 — set by the reason: a credit-support obligation being undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report / Form 10-K, including the guarantees and commitments note and MD&A disclosing credit-support and revenue-share arrangements.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"No open thesis exists; the size and credit-risk impact of NVIDIA's credit-support backstops for AI-cloud customers can move independently of GPU demand and product cycles, so it warrants its own tracking card.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout\",\n   \"publisher\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑scale AI services.\\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\\nTo address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced a new business model enabling AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through a revenue-sharing and credit-support arrangement, under which NVIDIA earns standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, providing a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\",\n     \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\",\n     \"Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity.\",\n     \"This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\",\n   \"period\": \"2026 calendar year — set because the item reports the shift is beginning as of mid-2026 and gives no specific release date.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"2026 industry server/accelerator shipment and attach-rate data (e.g., IDC, Dell'Oro, hyperscaler or OEM disclosures) plus NVIDIA and Intel product disclosures.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks NVIDIA's credit support and contingent obligations to AI cloud partners; this tracks an industry CPU:GPU mix that can change NVIDIA's GPU demand and system design independently of any credit-support decision.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a\",\n   \"publisher\": \"tradingkey.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\",\n   \"text\": \"Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix. While currently trading above consensus analyst targets, the valuation is supported by evolving commercial fundamentals. Technically, the stock remains in a neutral-bullish trend, with a potential move toward $159.90 if it clears $150.40 resistance.\\nTradingKey - Intel Corporation (NASDAQ: INTC ) sits at $140.05, holding firm on the ascending trendline within the 2H timeframe. The RSI indicator is currently reading 55.21, positioning it as neutral-bullish, while green candles continue to soak up sell orders in close proximity to the trendline-resistance alignment. This rally translates into a ~465% gain for the company's stock over the prior 12 months, climbing from a $18.97 low, a climb so massive that the 12-month average analyst price target of $93.93 now sits 33% behind the actual price of shares, creating an enduring wedge between where analysts have modelled the stock to end and where they find it today.\\nIntel’s July investment thesis does not hinge on the Apple deal or Trump’s social media post, topics I’ve written about in the past few issues; rather, it turns on the fact that NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel as well as opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems. Put another way, it’s the most important GPU company of our day endorsing Intel’s CPU.\\nJensen Huang Picked Intel — What the Xeon 6 Selection for DGX Rubin Means\\nNVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. CEO Lip-Bu Tan recently said Xeon 6 now plays the role of “the orchestration layer and critical control plane for the entire AI stack” and highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the ratio of CPUs needed to support the GPU is actually increasing, not decreasing (the prior narrative was that GPUs would ultimately take over the CPU role).\\nJensen Huang’s decision to build the DGX Rubin NVL8 on Xeon 6 coupled with their $5B equity investment in Intel represents the strongest signal yet from a third party that Intel’s CPU is competitive. This is not an Apple rumor or an announcement on social media: This is a CPU architecture decision from the world’s largest infrastructure company in AI.\\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges). Meanwhile, Intel Foundry saw revenue of $5.4B (up 16% year-over-year), which translates to a Foundry segment that is now over $20B in annualised revenue (still negative earnings but not quite the rounding error it was). CFO David Zinsner characterized the quarter as having “unprecedented demand for silicon” and Q2 guidance is $13.8 to $14.8B, indicating continued sequential revenue strength. About 60% of this quarter's revenue mix now comes from AI-linked sources, compared with a little less than 25% a year ago.\\nThe Google Deal — Multiyear, Locked Pricing, Custom ASIC Co-Development\\nGoogle's Q1 2026 announcement is the second major confirmed hyperscaler commitment that brings contractual revenue certainty to the Intel thesis. Google inked a multiyear deal for continued use of Intel Xeon processors in Google's workload-optimized instances, including the newest Xeon 6 processors that power C4 and N4 instances. The companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks.\\nCEO Tan said the agreements span three to five years, with locked volume and pricing. That represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term. The structure directly addresses the main criticism of Intel's revenue run-rate so far this year, which is that demand is not real but order-driven (front-loaded).\\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\\nThis deal stack is where the current price stands relative to the average price target of $93.93 on the stock from analyst coverage. This gap represents how fast the new Intel story is unfolding versus the rate at which sell-side analysts are updating their models: Cantor Fitzgerald raised its price target to $150 from $90 in early July 1 and still held a Neutral rating noting its price target remains a mere 13.9% above current levels.\\nCurrent valuation checks (per Simply Wall St) show Intel trades at a price-to-sales multiple of 13.1x vs. a suggested fair price multiple of 15.1x given its combination of foundry + AI exposure, and scores 2 of 6 on its broader valuation checks. While INTC is not cheap on an absolute basis, it's not obviously expensive when compared to the group of peers and the confirmed customer stack.\\nINTC Technical Setup — Trendline Defense at $140, RSI 55.21, Target $159.90\\nOn the 2H chart, INTC is holding the ascending multi-touch black trendline after a series of rejections of the descending red trendlines from the $169 plus highs and is holding above the EMAs200 at $106.07 with higher lows. RSI at 55.21 is neutral-bullish with room to rise above before becoming overbought and does not have any divergence, as buyer absorption of sell orders (seen on green candles) near the trendline-resistance confluence.\\nResistance overhead sits at $150.43 to $159.97. If INTC holds the trendline, and confirms a move above $150.40, the next price target is $159.90. If it breaks down below $126.20, the next price target is $117.07.\\nEntry: Long above $150.40 — trendline and resistance cleared\\nTarget: $159.90 — trendline bounce extension\\nStop Loss: Close below $126.20 — ascending trendline fails\\nNVIDIA selection: Xeon 6 chosen as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8; $5B equity investment\\nGoogle deal: Multiyear, locked pricing 3–5 years, custom ASIC IPU co-development\\nNext earnings: July 23, 2026\\nWhy Did NVIDIA Invest $5 Billion in Intel and Select Xeon 6 for Its AI Systems?\\nNVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next product cycle? Here's the explanation from Lip-Bu Tan: \\\"The CPU has become the orchestration layer, the control plane of the entire AI infrastructure. In inference workloads, we're starting to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs towards 1 CPU to 1 GPU.\\\" Jensen Huang's choice of Xeon 6 is a product architecture decision, by the company that's the benchmark for defining AI infrastructure, not an investment, or a promotion, from NVIDIA: Jensen's selection of Xeon 6 is the best possible validation of Intel's CPU competitive position.\\nWhat Did Intel Report in Q1 2026 and What Is Q2 Guidance?\\nIn Q1 2026, Data Center and AI (DC&A) was $5.1 billion (+22% YoY) and Foundry was $5.4 billion (+16% YoY). Non-GAAP earnings per share was $0.29 per share, compared with $0.73 (GAAP) because of foundry capital investment charges for 18A. Intel expects Q2 to continue expanding sequentially with revenues of $13.8 billion to $14.8 billion. \\\"AI-linked revenue is now approximately 60% of the quarterly mix, as compared with a year ago when it was under 25%,\\\" said CFO David Zinsner. The quarter was defined by unprecedented demand for silicon and Intel's determination to use the entire factory network as a means to maximize output. \\\"Our long-term deals span three to five years, with locked volumes and pricing,\\\" said CFO Zinsner.\\nWhy Is the Average Analyst Target at $93.93 When Intel Trades at $140?\\nThat's a 33% difference, which shows how rapidly the Intel story has evolved relative to the time required for sell-side equity research analysts to update their models. Cantor Fitzgerald increased its 12-month price target to $150 on July 1 while keeping a Neutral rating, stating that the new price target was 13.9% above the current stock price. The majority of Wall Street firms haven't yet updated their financial models to account for the NVIDIA $5B equity investment, the Google multiyear deal, and a full risk production announcement for 18A. The Simple Wall St analysis places \\\"fair value\\\" Price/Sales of 15.1 vs. 13.1, which suggests that Intel is not significantly overvalued vs. peer group P/S at current trading levels.\\nIntel is up 465% over the past year and now trades 33% above the consensus analyst 12-month price target of $93.93. That gap exists because the story, now anchored around NVIDIA's investment and product choice, the Google and Microsoft signed deals, has moved a long distance relative to the ability of sell-side research analysts to update models. Those commercial agreements are no longer a rumour.\\nThe Intel story now includes a $5 billion equity investment from NVIDIA for Xeon 6 as the host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8, a Google multiyear agreement with locked volume and pricing (3-to-5-year deployment and co-development of Intel custom AI accelerators (ASIC)), and Microsoft has signed a deal for wafers using 18A technology.\\nQ1 2026 was Data Center and AI $5.1 billion (+22%), with an average of 60% AI-linked of the mix. Technicals are a defense at $140, with the average ascending trendline, plus RSI at 55.21, neutral-bullish. Confirmation above $150.40 will send prices to a $159.90 price target. A stop at $126.20 would protect against a failure. Q2 2026 guidance to be issued at July 23 earnings is the next checkpoint, where we'll hopefully see a large component of the stack materialize in guidance.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA selected Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, a product architecture decision for an AI system the item says will power data centres over the next two to three years. The item does not give a shipment date.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan said the CPU has become the orchestration layer and control plane of AI infrastructure and that, in inference workloads, the CPU:GPU ratio is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU, meaning more CPUs per GPU. The item says he spoke 'recently' and gives no full date.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"CEO Lip-Bu Tan recently said Xeon 6 now plays the role of “the orchestration layer and critical control plane for the entire AI stack” and highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the ratio of CPUs needed to support the GPU is actually increasing, not decreasing (the prior narrative was that GPUs would ultimately take over the CPU role).\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Lip-Bu Tan (Intel CEO)\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\",\n   \"period\": \"First shipments by end of calendar 2026 — set by the item: the platform 'could begin shipping later this year' in an article dated 2 July 2026.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"product_node\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's quarterly earnings release and Form 10-Q disclosures on the data center product ramp, plus first-shipment confirmation from NVIDIA and its server/system partners.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks how much credit support NVIDIA extends to AI cloud partners and the resulting contingent credit exposure; the Vera Rubin shipping milestone can be reached or missed on its own (production, packaging/HBM supply, customer qualification) regardless of how much backstop NVIDIA provides, so an analyst would bet on the two separately.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-02\",\n   \"title\": \"Microsoft vs. Nvidia: Which Is the Better AI Stock to Own for the Next 3 Years?\",\n   \"text\": \"Microsoft ( MSFT +1.28% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) are two heavyweights in the technology world and key players in artificial intelligence (AI). Microsoft seems to have its hand in every tech market segment, especially anything involving enterprise customers. Meanwhile, Nvidia has become the de facto leader in GPU chips used in AI data centers.\\nBoth stocks have made investors very wealthy over the years. But which of these top AI stocks is the better buy for the next three years? This article will explain why I like Microsoft a tad more. Although, as you'll see below, it's hard to go wrong with either one.\\nVera Rubin likely means Nvidia has more growth ahead\\nNvidia has enjoyed one of the most impressive growth spurts in history over these past few years as the AI build-out juiced demand for its GPUs. This supercycle continues to rage on. Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\nWall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year. Nvidia's valuation is already well below its high at less than 19 times sales, and it might become much cheaper as these next 24 months play out.\\nMicrosoft is pivoting to capitalize on its enterprise customer base\\nMicrosoft originally tied its AI hopes to OpenAI. Although Microsoft has done well with its OpenAI investment and enjoyed significant cloud computing growth as a result of the partnership, it has still struggled to establish itself as a key AI player. Users, especially enterprises, have opted for Anthropic's Claude among other AI models. As a result, Microsoft shifted to a multi-model strategy and has reportedly considered adding open-source models to Copilot Cowork to lower token costs.\\nInvestors should applaud Microsoft's willingness to adapt. The company has deeply entrenched relationships with the enterprise world, which gives Microsoft more margin for error in fine-tuning its AI strategy than most companies. Plus, Microsoft's Azure remains a formidable growth engine, with a staggering $627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations.\\nNvidia's data center business has grown so much that the stock has become a concentrated investment in this ongoing supercycle. That means Nvidia has a sky-high ceiling with Vera Rubin shipping soon, but it's riskier in that the floor is much lower if companies stop pouring all this money into AI infrastructure. Meanwhile, Microsoft remains highly diversified across software, cloud computing, and AI. That lowers the company's ceiling, but it's also the safer stock.\\nMicrosoft's impressive track record typically earns it a premium valuation. The stock has traded at an average of almost 33 times its trailing-12-month earnings over the past decade, but trades at just 22 times its earnings today. Meanwhile, Wall Street analysts expect the company to grow earnings by 16% to 17% annually over the next three to five years.\\nWhile some may opt for Nvidia's immense growth potential , Microsoft rarely offers a valuation compelling enough to buy into.\\nJustin Pope is a contributing Motley Fool stock market analyst covering information technology, consumer discretionary, consumer staples, and industrials. Prior to The Motley Fool, Justin was a business manager for an industrial company.\\nBy Justin Pope – Jul 2, 2026 at 12:43PM EST\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's next-generation AI chip platform, Vera Rubin, is described as being in full production and as possibly beginning shipments later this year (2026).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027, per the article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang)\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": [\n     {\n      \"measure\": \"orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips\",\n      \"period\": \"through 2027\",\n      \"value\": \"$1 trillion\",\n      \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n      \"basis\": \"CEO Jensen Huang's stated expectation, as reported by the article\"\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar 2026 — set by the item's SMIC capacity figures ('by the end of this year ... 50,000 of these chips per month').\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Huawei and SMIC public disclosures and filings, independent chip-output and teardown estimates (e.g., SemiAnalysis, TechInsights, Epoch AI) covering 2026, and China customs/production statistics published through early 2027.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 asks whether Nvidia's own Vera Rubin platform starts shipping by end-2026; this asks how much substitute AI compute a Chinese rival can make in the same year. The two can move apart in both directions: Rubin can ship on schedule while SMIC output stalls (or vice versa), and Huawei output can scale on Nvidia's estimates without any implication for Rubin's schedule.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02\",\n   \"publisher\": \"thewirechina.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-02\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Uses The Specter of Huawei to Make Its Chip Exports Case\",\n   \"text\": \"Before Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S. government that Huawei could have enough chips to satisfy global demand.\\nBy Noah Berman\\nWhen Nvidia pitched the Trump administration last year to let it sell advanced AI chips to China, the world’s biggest chipmaker issued a clear warning: Huawei, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company warned that Huawei’s stockpile of chips, along with growing capacity at its manufacturing partner Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. (SMIC), give it “the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.”\\n“If Nvidia is kept out of the China market, Huawei production and sales will soar,” the report said, although it did not specify a timeframe or refer to the differences in performance between Nvidia and Huawei chips.\\nThe pitch — alongside Nvidia Chief Executive Jensen Huang’s personal diplomacy — worked. President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\\nNvidia’s analysis sheds light on how the world’s most valuable company has presented Huawei’s growing capabilities as justification for it to be able to sell more to China, the world’s second-largest market for chips after the U.S.\\nNvidia is attempting to “scare U.S. policymakers that Huawei is this great big threat,” says Gregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls. “They are, but not in the way Nvidia is claiming.”\\n“[But] there is no apples-to-apples comparison between Nvidia chips and Huawei chips,” says Allen, who supports export controls. “Having a lot of chips is not a synonym for being competitive in the marketplace.”\\nSince the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022, Nvidia’s market share in China has fallen to zero, Huang has said .\\nIn the report it presented to the government, Nvidia estimated that Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and that it would sell 1 million in 2025. It also estimated that by the end of this year SMIC — which manufactures chips based on Huawei’s design and technology — will have made more than 700,000 Ascend 910C chips, one of Huawei’s most advanced, with production reaching 50,000 of these chips per month. By 2027, the report said, SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei’s less advanced 910B chips.\\nHuawei simply does not pose a competitive threat to Nvidia globally right now. Huawei’s chips are nowhere near as good, and it can’t make nearly enough of them.\\nChris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations\\nThose numbers far exceed Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick’s estimate in congressional testimony last June that China could make just 200,000 advanced chips in 2025.\\nA Nvidia spokesperson declined to comment on the report. “The ability of China’s domestic ecosystem to yield and scale competitive AI systems is a fact, evidenced by leading Chinese models that are optimized to perform on domestic hardware,” the spokesperson said.\\nBut even on Nvidia’s numbers, Huawei would remain way short of the American company’s chip output, let alone being able to “satisfy global demand.”\\nIn a report published in December, Chris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations, estimated that if Huawei made 800,000 Ascend 910C chips in 2025 — a figure at the high end of several estimates — that would amount to just 5.3 percent of the total processing power of Nvidia’s chip production that year.\\nMcGuire also estimates that Nvidia’s lead by performance will only grow, even if Huawei were to make 2 million AI chips this year and 4 million in 2027. Industry analysts measure performance by how many calculations a chip can do per second and how fast it can move data around, with Nvidia’s best chips capable of doing far more than those of Huawei.\\n“Huawei simply does not pose a competitive threat to Nvidia globally right now,” says McGuire, formerly a national security official who helped design U.S. chip controls. “Huawei’s chips are nowhere near as good, and it can’t make nearly enough of them.”\\nIronically, he adds, Nvidia has a lead over Huawei largely because of U.S. export controls on semiconductor manufacturing equipment, the lack of which has degraded China’s ability to make AI chips.\\nNvidia’s analysis did not contain any of its own new modeling of Huawei’s capabilities. Instead its report cited estimates from outside research groups, including think tank the Center for Strategic and International Studies, the consultancy Semianalysis, and a handful of Wall Street firms including Bernstein and Morgan Stanley.\\nThe report also cited Reuters reporting that Huawei had built a stockpile of between 2 and 3 million of its own AI chips made by Taiwanese chipmaker TSMC, which it had obtained via illegal diversion by the Chinese company Sophgo. Companies such as TSMC that make chips using American technology must follow U.S. export controls, which effectively prevent sales to Huawei.\\nBy choosing to highlight that stockpile in its report, Nvidia is in fact underscoring how export controls are succeeding in holding Huawei back, says Lennart Heim , an independent researcher who studies chip and AI policy.\\n“If they were able to produce that many chips themselves, why would they go to TSMC?” he asks. Even though figures such as 2 to 3 million AI chips sound significant, “this number is tiny” relative to the more than 60 million 910C-equivalent chips that the U.S. will have produced between January 2025 and the end of this year, Heim adds.\\nHuawei made just 6 percent of the total AI chips sold worldwide during the first quarter of this year, and half that amount after adjusting for the fact that the company’s chips are not as powerful as Nvidia’s, according to Epoch AI, a research institute.\\nWhile Nvidia’s report framed its argument on AI chip export controls around its competition with Huawei, others argue that a more important issue is that the restrictions will incentivize Chinese companies to try to catch up with their American rivals.\\n“There are good arguments against export controls,” Heim says. But instead of articulating those, Nvidia is “trying to use technical terms to confuse people.”\\nNvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe. I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.\\nGregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls\\n“Huawei has no way right now to satisfy global demand,” he adds.\\nHuawei has certainly been trying to improve its offering. In March, it introduced a new chip, the 950PR, and is preparing to launch another later this year. Yet even those chips fail to match the quality of the H200 because they have slower memory , according to Heim. They lag even further behind Nvidia’s most advanced Blackwell chips .\\nHuawei and SMIC did not respond to requests for comment.\\nThe Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\\n“We sell the first, best and most of our technology to America and offer competitive products in China to commercial users vetted and approved by the U.S. government,” says the Nvidia spokesperson. “National security is first and foremost. Where commercial opportunity conflicts with U.S. national security, national security comes first.”\\nBut Nvidia is losing sympathy in Washington, says Allen, the analyst.\\n“Nvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe,” he says. “I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.”\\nNoah Berman is a staff writer for The Wire based in New York. He previously wrote about economics and technology at the Council on Foreign Relations. His work has appeared in the Boston Globe and PBS News . He graduated from Georgetown University.\\nBy Michael Kovrig\\nA new book provides searing testimonials of how the Chinese Communist Party’s thought remolding and coercive interrogations continue under the legal obfuscation of “residential surveillance at a designated location”.\\nBy Savannah Billman\\nBy Eliot Chen\\nThe former State Department official in the Biden administration talks about leading the first talks with China on AI, and what the realistic goals should be of any dialogue between the two superpowers about the...\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia presented a report to the U.S. government, reviewed by The Wire, warning that Huawei's chip stockpile plus growing capacity at its manufacturing partner SMIC give Huawei 'the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.' In the report Nvidia estimated Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and would sell 1 million in 2025; that SMIC, which makes chips based on Huawei's design and technology, will have produced more than 700,000 Ascend 910C chips by the end of 2026, with output reaching 50,000 of those chips per month; and that by 2027 SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei's less advanced 910B chips. A Nvidia spokesperson declined to comment on the report.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company warned that Huawei’s stockpile of chips, along with growing capacity at its manufacturing partner Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. (SMIC), give it “the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.”\",\n     \"In the report it presented to the government, Nvidia estimated that Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and that it would sell 1 million in 2025. It also estimated that by the end of this year SMIC — which manufactures chips based on Huawei’s design and technology — will have made more than 700,000 Ascend 910C chips, one of Huawei’s most advanced, with production reaching 50,000 of these chips per month. By 2027, the report said, SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei’s less advanced 910B chips.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"U.S. Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick estimated in congressional testimony last June that China could make just 200,000 advanced chips in 2025, a figure the article says is far below the Huawei/SMIC output Nvidia's report projected.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Those numbers far exceed Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick’s estimate in congressional testimony last June that China could make just 200,000 advanced chips in 2025.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Howard Lutnick (U.S. Commerce Secretary)\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Chris McGuire, a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations, estimated in a report published in December that if Huawei made 800,000 Ascend 910C chips in 2025 — the high end of several estimates — that would amount to just 5.3% of the total processing power of Nvidia's chip production that year. McGuire also estimates Nvidia's performance lead will only grow even if Huawei makes 2 million AI chips this year (2026) and 4 million in 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In a report published in December, Chris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations, estimated that if Huawei made 800,000 Ascend 910C chips in 2025 — a figure at the high end of several estimates — that would amount to just 5.3 percent of the total processing power of Nvidia’s chip production that year.\",\n     \"McGuire also estimates that Nvidia’s lead by performance will only grow, even if Huawei were to make 2 million AI chips this year and 4 million in 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Chris McGuire (Council on Foreign Relations)\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Lennart Heim, an independent researcher, said Huawei's stockpile of 2 to 3 million of its own AI chips — which Nvidia's report cited Reuters as reporting were made by TSMC and obtained through illegal diversion by Chinese company Sophgo — is 'tiny' relative to the more than 60 million 910C-equivalent chips the U.S. will have produced between January 2025 and the end of 2026.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The report also cited Reuters reporting that Huawei had built a stockpile of between 2 and 3 million of its own AI chips made by Taiwanese chipmaker TSMC, which it had obtained via illegal diversion by the Chinese company Sophgo.\",\n     \"Even though figures such as 2 to 3 million AI chips sound significant, “this number is tiny” relative to the more than 60 million 910C-equivalent chips that the U.S. will have produced between January 2025 and the end of this year, Heim adds.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Lennart Heim (independent researcher); stockpile figure via Reuters, cited by Nvidia\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"According to research institute Epoch AI, Huawei made just 6 percent of the total AI chips sold worldwide during the first quarter of this year (2026), and half that amount after adjusting for the fact that its chips are not as powerful as Nvidia's.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Huawei made just 6 percent of the total AI chips sold worldwide during the first quarter of this year, and half that amount after adjusting for the fact that the company’s chips are not as powerful as Nvidia’s, according to Epoch AI, a research institute.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Epoch AI\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Huawei introduced a new AI chip, the 950PR, in March 2026 and is preparing to launch another chip later this year, according to the article; Lennart Heim says those chips still fail to match the quality of Nvidia's H200 because they have slower memory, and lag even further behind Nvidia's most advanced Blackwell chips.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Huawei has certainly been trying to improve its offering. In March, it introduced a new chip, the 950PR, and is preparing to launch another later this year.\",\n     \"Yet even those chips fail to match the quality of the H200 because they have slower memory , according to Heim. They lag even further behind Nvidia’s most advanced Blackwell chips .\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Huawei (with assessment by Lennart Heim)\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\",\n   \"period\": \"Nvidia's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027) — set by the reason: a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Chinese government (State Council / MOFCOM / CAC) licensing decisions and announcements, and Nvidia's quarterly Form 10-Q and FY2027 Form 10-K disclosures of China data-center revenue.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 is about Nvidia's next-generation product reaching first shipments; this is about market access for an existing product (H200) that has already been cleared by Washington but not by Beijing. They move independently: Rubin can begin shipping while China stays closed, and Beijing can reopen H200 purchases while Rubin slips.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02\",\n   \"publisher\": \"thewirechina.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-02\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Uses The Specter of Huawei to Make Its Chip Exports Case\",\n   \"text\": \"Before Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S. government that Huawei could have enough chips to satisfy global demand.\\nBy Noah Berman\\nWhen Nvidia pitched the Trump administration last year to let it sell advanced AI chips to China, the world’s biggest chipmaker issued a clear warning: Huawei, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company warned that Huawei’s stockpile of chips, along with growing capacity at its manufacturing partner Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. (SMIC), give it “the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.”\\n“If Nvidia is kept out of the China market, Huawei production and sales will soar,” the report said, although it did not specify a timeframe or refer to the differences in performance between Nvidia and Huawei chips.\\nThe pitch — alongside Nvidia Chief Executive Jensen Huang’s personal diplomacy — worked. President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\\nNvidia’s analysis sheds light on how the world’s most valuable company has presented Huawei’s growing capabilities as justification for it to be able to sell more to China, the world’s second-largest market for chips after the U.S.\\nNvidia is attempting to “scare U.S. policymakers that Huawei is this great big threat,” says Gregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls. “They are, but not in the way Nvidia is claiming.”\\n“[But] there is no apples-to-apples comparison between Nvidia chips and Huawei chips,” says Allen, who supports export controls. “Having a lot of chips is not a synonym for being competitive in the marketplace.”\\nSince the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022, Nvidia’s market share in China has fallen to zero, Huang has said .\\nIn the report it presented to the government, Nvidia estimated that Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and that it would sell 1 million in 2025. It also estimated that by the end of this year SMIC — which manufactures chips based on Huawei’s design and technology — will have made more than 700,000 Ascend 910C chips, one of Huawei’s most advanced, with production reaching 50,000 of these chips per month. By 2027, the report said, SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei’s less advanced 910B chips.\\nHuawei simply does not pose a competitive threat to Nvidia globally right now. Huawei’s chips are nowhere near as good, and it can’t make nearly enough of them.\\nChris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations\\nThose numbers far exceed Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick’s estimate in congressional testimony last June that China could make just 200,000 advanced chips in 2025.\\nA Nvidia spokesperson declined to comment on the report. “The ability of China’s domestic ecosystem to yield and scale competitive AI systems is a fact, evidenced by leading Chinese models that are optimized to perform on domestic hardware,” the spokesperson said.\\nBut even on Nvidia’s numbers, Huawei would remain way short of the American company’s chip output, let alone being able to “satisfy global demand.”\\nIn a report published in December, Chris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations, estimated that if Huawei made 800,000 Ascend 910C chips in 2025 — a figure at the high end of several estimates — that would amount to just 5.3 percent of the total processing power of Nvidia’s chip production that year.\\nMcGuire also estimates that Nvidia’s lead by performance will only grow, even if Huawei were to make 2 million AI chips this year and 4 million in 2027. Industry analysts measure performance by how many calculations a chip can do per second and how fast it can move data around, with Nvidia’s best chips capable of doing far more than those of Huawei.\\n“Huawei simply does not pose a competitive threat to Nvidia globally right now,” says McGuire, formerly a national security official who helped design U.S. chip controls. “Huawei’s chips are nowhere near as good, and it can’t make nearly enough of them.”\\nIronically, he adds, Nvidia has a lead over Huawei largely because of U.S. export controls on semiconductor manufacturing equipment, the lack of which has degraded China’s ability to make AI chips.\\nNvidia’s analysis did not contain any of its own new modeling of Huawei’s capabilities. Instead its report cited estimates from outside research groups, including think tank the Center for Strategic and International Studies, the consultancy Semianalysis, and a handful of Wall Street firms including Bernstein and Morgan Stanley.\\nThe report also cited Reuters reporting that Huawei had built a stockpile of between 2 and 3 million of its own AI chips made by Taiwanese chipmaker TSMC, which it had obtained via illegal diversion by the Chinese company Sophgo. Companies such as TSMC that make chips using American technology must follow U.S. export controls, which effectively prevent sales to Huawei.\\nBy choosing to highlight that stockpile in its report, Nvidia is in fact underscoring how export controls are succeeding in holding Huawei back, says Lennart Heim , an independent researcher who studies chip and AI policy.\\n“If they were able to produce that many chips themselves, why would they go to TSMC?” he asks. Even though figures such as 2 to 3 million AI chips sound significant, “this number is tiny” relative to the more than 60 million 910C-equivalent chips that the U.S. will have produced between January 2025 and the end of this year, Heim adds.\\nHuawei made just 6 percent of the total AI chips sold worldwide during the first quarter of this year, and half that amount after adjusting for the fact that the company’s chips are not as powerful as Nvidia’s, according to Epoch AI, a research institute.\\nWhile Nvidia’s report framed its argument on AI chip export controls around its competition with Huawei, others argue that a more important issue is that the restrictions will incentivize Chinese companies to try to catch up with their American rivals.\\n“There are good arguments against export controls,” Heim says. But instead of articulating those, Nvidia is “trying to use technical terms to confuse people.”\\nNvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe. I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.\\nGregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls\\n“Huawei has no way right now to satisfy global demand,” he adds.\\nHuawei has certainly been trying to improve its offering. In March, it introduced a new chip, the 950PR, and is preparing to launch another later this year. Yet even those chips fail to match the quality of the H200 because they have slower memory , according to Heim. They lag even further behind Nvidia’s most advanced Blackwell chips .\\nHuawei and SMIC did not respond to requests for comment.\\nThe Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\\n“We sell the first, best and most of our technology to America and offer competitive products in China to commercial users vetted and approved by the U.S. government,” says the Nvidia spokesperson. “National security is first and foremost. Where commercial opportunity conflicts with U.S. national security, national security comes first.”\\nBut Nvidia is losing sympathy in Washington, says Allen, the analyst.\\n“Nvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe,” he says. “I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.”\\nNoah Berman is a staff writer for The Wire based in New York. He previously wrote about economics and technology at the Council on Foreign Relations. His work has appeared in the Boston Globe and PBS News . He graduated from Georgetown University.\\nBy Michael Kovrig\\nA new book provides searing testimonials of how the Chinese Communist Party’s thought remolding and coercive interrogations continue under the legal obfuscation of “residential surveillance at a designated location”.\\nBy Savannah Billman\\nBy Eliot Chen\\nThe former State Department official in the Biden administration talks about leading the first talks with China on AI, and what the realistic goals should be of any dialogue between the two superpowers about the...\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia presented a report to the U.S. government, reviewed by The Wire, warning that Huawei's chip stockpile plus growing capacity at its manufacturing partner SMIC give Huawei 'the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.' In the report Nvidia estimated Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and would sell 1 million in 2025; that SMIC, which makes chips based on Huawei's design and technology, will have produced more than 700,000 Ascend 910C chips by the end of 2026, with output reaching 50,000 of those chips per month; and that by 2027 SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei's less advanced 910B chips. A Nvidia spokesperson declined to comment on the report.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company warned that Huawei’s stockpile of chips, along with growing capacity at its manufacturing partner Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. (SMIC), give it “the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.”\",\n     \"In the report it presented to the government, Nvidia estimated that Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and that it would sell 1 million in 2025. It also estimated that by the end of this year SMIC — which manufactures chips based on Huawei’s design and technology — will have made more than 700,000 Ascend 910C chips, one of Huawei’s most advanced, with production reaching 50,000 of these chips per month. By 2027, the report said, SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei’s less advanced 910B chips.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"President Trump announced last December that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 AI chips to China, after Nvidia's pitch and CEO Jensen Huang's personal diplomacy. The U.S. government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling the chip, which it launched in 2023, to China under export controls put in place by the Biden administration.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The pitch — alongside Nvidia Chief Executive Jensen Huang’s personal diplomacy — worked. President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"U.S. government (President Trump)\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"As of the article's publication, the Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared the path for exports of its H200 chips.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Chinese government\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-02\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has said Nvidia's market share in China has fallen to zero since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Since the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022, Nvidia’s market share in China has fallen to zero, Huang has said .\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Nvidia (CEO Jensen Huang)\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item: Nvidia projects hyperscaler capex could reach $1 trillion 'next year' in an article dated 4 July 2026.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Full-year 2027 capital expenditure disclosures and forward guidance in the big four hyperscalers' quarterly earnings reports and 10-K/10-Q filings.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 asks whether Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform ships by end-2026, a supply/product timing question; this asks how much the customers will actually spend in 2027, a demand-volume question. The two can move apart: hyperscaler capex could exceed $1 trillion even if Vera Rubin slips, or capex could stall even if Vera Rubin ships on schedule.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-04\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) investors have had it good for several years. In 2023, 2024, and 2025, it produced market-crushing returns, making it one of the best stocks to own during each of those years. However, 2026 hasn't been that way. The stock is up 5% so far in 2026, which isn't nothing, but compared to the returns that Nvidia investors have enjoyed in recent years, it feels like an absolute failure. On another note, its market-crushing status is in jeopardy for 2026, as the S&P 500 is up nearly 10% for the year.\\nWith Nvidia stock looking like a dud this year, many investors are wondering whether now is the time to cash out and look for other investment opportunities, or to load up on Nvidia shares. Let's take a look, as not everything is as it appears with Nvidia.\\nNvidia was the top AI stock to own over the past three years, due to its market position. Nvidia designs graphics processing units (GPUs) and the hardware that supports them. Although it doesn't own any of the fabrication process itself, it works with several companies that manufacture its GPUs, which it then sells to a wide range of customers.\\nBefore 2023, Nvidia's business was more balanced. However, the massive demand for AI data centers has driven this lineup of GPUs into high demand, and Nvidia's product line has become the go-to for several AI hyperscalers and cloud providers. Nvidia has a complete ecosystem of products that gives its clients everything they need to run an Nvidia-powered data center, locking in clients once they've chosen to use its products.\\nThis bodes well for the future, as AI hyperscalers are spending a massive amount of money this year and beyond to continue building out their AI computing footprints. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion. That means more growth is coming to Nvidia, and the investing story is far from complete. Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\\nHowever, the stock price doesn't reflect that.\\nNvidia trades at 21.7 times forward earnings, essentially the same price tag as the S&P 500. This conveys that the market believes Nvidia will be a market-average stock after 2026's growth is accounted for. However, Wall Street analysts don't believe that, as they're guiding for major growth next year. That's why the stock looks so cheap when next year's earnings are used to value the stock.\\nAs a result, I think Nvidia is an excellent stock to buy now. None of next year's growth has been priced in, which could lead to strong returns toward the end of this year.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, a demand backdrop for Nvidia's AI GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"big four AI hyperscalers\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in the next year (2027).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\",\n   \"period\": \"First shipments by end of calendar 2028 — set by the item: SemiAnalysis says the Kyber NVL144 architecture 'has been delayed to 2028' from a planned 2027 debut alongside Rubin Ultra.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"product_node\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Nvidia's own shipment confirmations and data-center product commentary on its quarterly earnings calls and in its Form 10-Q/10-K, plus SemiAnalysis follow-up reporting on Kyber NVL144 manufacturability.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 asks whether Vera Rubin begins shipping by end-2026 — a schedule this item says is on track for this fall; the Kyber rack for the following Rubin Ultra generation can slip to 2028 independently, so the two shipment dates, and the revenue each carries, can move apart.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587\",\n   \"publisher\": \"cnbc.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n   \"text\": \"Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\\nNvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\nShares of the artificial intelligence chip giant climbed about 1% on Monday.\\nKyber is a server cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips into a single unit so they can work together as one giant computer, providing the horsepower AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\\nThe design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\\n\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\\nThe reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits.\\nA backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\\nThat leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\nThe delays \\\"should not be over analyzed as affecting the long-term criticality of Nvidia to AI data infrastructure buildouts,\\\" said Paul Triolo, a partner at consultancy DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group, noting the company \\\"has faced these kinds of challenges before, and has worked with vendors to overcome technical issues.\\\"\\nWith power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \\\"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\\\"\\nNvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud. SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\nWhile Huawei and other domestic manufacturers \\\"will potentially gain some time,\\\" China's hardware ecosystem is increasingly diverging from — rather than chasing — the Nvidia-led model, Triolo said. \\\"The issue is no longer really catching up, but how good will China's alternative AI stack be by 2030,\\\" he said.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's backup plan to bolt two of its current-generation racks together to obtain similar scale-up power for Rubin Ultra was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's operational burden, leaving Nvidia, in the firm's words, with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra' and, it predicted, a technical opening for AMD and Google at the high end.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\",\n     \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n    \"tier\": \"party_statement\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K), expected early 2027 — set by the reason: an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's FY2027 Form 10-K (and the interim 10-Q purchase-commitments note), which disclose the dollar amount of supply/purchase commitments.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks credit support NVIDIA extends to AI cloud partners — contingent, customer-financing exposure it may have to fund if partners' revenue disappoints. This thesis tracks NVIDIA's own committed supply purchases — on-balance-sheet input-cost and obsolescence exposure. The two can rise or fall independently: strong AI demand could pull both up, but a demand air-pocket would expose them differently, so an analyst would bet on them separately.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32\",\n   \"publisher\": \"247wallst.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n   \"text\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\\nBy Danielle Liverance\\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewarding investors who bet on chips with room to reprice rather than perfection already baked in.\\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\\nBut the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from the data center buildout, they’re just not piling into NVIDIA.\\nMicron is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money, what executives are worried about, and what any of it means for the person checking their 401(k) on a Sunday afternoon. I write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\\nSK Hynix surged 14% Tuesday without a single company-specific headline, and the real test comes July 29 when its earnings either confirm or shatter the bullish memory narrative Micron just set.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"247wallst.com states that NVIDIA carries about $119 billion in 'supply commitments,' which the article says depend on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": [\n     {\n      \"measure\": \"Supply commitments\",\n      \"period\": null,\n      \"value\": \"$119B\",\n      \"kind\": \"actual\",\n      \"basis\": \"supply commitments\"\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will the French competition authority conclude its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU practices with an adverse decision, fine or remedy, and by when?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The French competition authority's conclusion of its antitrust probe into Nvidia's alleged anti-competitive conduct in cloud-AI and GPU markets, including the decision date, finding and any fine or remedy imposed.\",\n   \"period\": \"Nvidia's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027) — set because a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end; the item reports the probe is 'nearing a conclusion.'\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"The French competition authority's (Autorité de la concurrence) published decision or conclusion statement on its investigation into cloud-AI and GPU markets.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-5 tracks whether Beijing permits Chinese buyers to purchase H200 chips (China export access). This thesis tracks a separate EU/French antitrust conduct finding and any fine or remedy, whose outcome can move independently: Beijing could open H200 access while the French authority rules adversely, or vice versa.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86\",\n   \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Bears Add to Wagers as Competition Threatens Its Dominance: Short Seller Tracker\",\n   \"text\": \"Meta Says AI Compute Isn’t Enough: Is the Infrastructure Selloff Over?\\n5.3M Views · 140 Posts\\n$NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ faced mounting pressure after bears added to their wagers against the chip giant amid increasing competition that threatened to challenge its dominance in the market for semiconductors that power artificial intelligence workloads.\\nTrading in borrowed Nvidia shares that were sold short rose to 13.61 million shares Wednesday, from 11.69 million shares a day earlier, exchange data tracked by moomoo showed. The latest figure represented 9.2% of the shares that changed hands yesterday, when the stock advanced 3.7%, the biggest gain in five weeks. Shorting involves shares sold without the seller owning them, which is a common practice used to profit from a potential decline in a stock's price.\\n$Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)$ to manufacture the AI chips, according to the report.\\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\nStill, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\\nNVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\nRegulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\\nShare your thoughts on Nvidia in the comments section. Are retail investors losing their faith in Nvidia? Let your voice be heard by voting below.\\nComments (4)\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The French competition authority announced on Thursday (2026-07-09) that its antitrust investigation into Nvidia is nearing a conclusion; the probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Regulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"French competition authority (Autorité de la concurrence)\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How much new high-bandwidth memory (HBM) production capacity will SK Hynix, Micron and Samsung bring online in calendar 2027, and will it arrive early enough to ease the current HBM shortage and record prices that gate NVIDIA's GPU output?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The combined new HBM capacity the three producers add in 2027 and whether any of it arrives before late 2027, easing the shortage; measured against the item's forecast that no new HBM supply arrives before late 2027.\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item's forecast that 'None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027,' which places the supply-easing question in 2027.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"HBM capacity disclosures and HBM contract/spot-price reporting from SK Hynix, Micron and Samsung (company results, TrendForce/Counterpoint industry data) covering 2027.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-8 tracks the dollar amount of NVIDIA's own supply/purchase commitments on its balance sheet; this thesis tracks the physical availability and prices of HBM from its suppliers. They can move independently — NVIDIA could raise commitments (paying more) while HBM stays tight, or HBM supply could ease while commitments remain high — so an analyst would bet on the two separately.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:9ae96ca750b8e3962c47795f\",\n   \"publisher\": \"247wallst.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n   \"title\": \"Memory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut\",\n   \"text\": \"Memory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut - 24/7 Wall St.\\nMemory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut\\nSK Hynix just pulled off one of the largest share sales in history, and one memory market expert says U.S. investors have been missing the single most critical bottleneck in the entire AI supply chain. Here is why that changes…\\nBy Thomas Richmond\\nNVIDIA's $82B quarterly revenue and Micron's 346% year-over-year sales surge confirm AI chips are driving an unprecedented memory demand cycle.\\nSK Hynix controls 58% of the HBM market, and its $26.5B Nasdaq debut finally gives U.S. investors direct access to NVIDIA's most critical chip supplier.\\nPartsinevelos warned that no new HBM supply arrives before late 2027, keeping record prices intact while $14B in passive index buying looms.\\nCNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos reports on SK Hynix's significant $26.5 billion Nasdaq debut, highlighting its crucial role in the AI memory market alongside NVIDIA. © 24/7 Wall St.\\nDuring a July 10 CNBC segment , Kristina Partsinevelos framed SK Hynix’s roughly $26.5 billion capital raise at $149 per American depositary share as a defining moment for the AI supply chain. The Korean memory giant controls approximately 58% of the high-bandwidth memory market, giving it a commanding position in the technology required to make NVIDIA’s most advanced chips work. The offering ranks as the second-largest share sale ever, trailing only SpaceX’s NASDAQ debut last month.\\nFor U.S. investors, the debut closes a long-standing accessibility gap. SK Hynix has been the essential HBM supplier to NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) throughout the AI buildout, yet trading in the Korean parent required navigating Seoul-listed shares. It also arrives as Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang’s “largest infrastructure expansion in human history” creates extraordinary demand for a product that remains in short supply.\\nSK Hynix Controls the Memory Powering the AI Revolution\\nThe core setup for SK Hynix, according to Partsinevelos: “SK Hynix is the world leader in high bandwidth memory . The memory that feeds AI chips with roughly 58% of that market. Micron and Samsung competitors split roughly 21% each … as per Counterpoint Research.” She added a blunter framing of the competitive stack: “SK Hynix is bigger, cheaper and closer to NVIDIA.”\\nPartsinevelos noted revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year, with the company committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea. A separate roughly $458 million from the U.S. CHIPS Act is earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\\nNew Supply Will Likely Not Arrive Until 2027\\nMemory prices have been incredibly strong due to tight supply. “None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now,” Partsinevelos said. Passive flows may amplify the near-term move: “Berkeley estimates the stock could see up to roughly $14 billion in passive buying alone as it enters the major indices just over the next few months.”\\nReaders looking for the broader thesis on companies riding this wave can dig into our Free Report: 7 Stocks Powering the AI Boom (That Aren’t Chipmakers) .\\nMicron Shows How Valuable AI Memory Has Become\\nSK Hynix’s U.S.-listed challenger, Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ), shows why memory investors are watching HBM share so closely. Micron posted Q3 FY2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year, with non-GAAP diluted EPS of $25.11 and GAAP gross margin of 84.6%. Guidance for the following quarter calls for revenue of $50.0 billion plus or minus $1.0 billion. CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in the Q3 release that results “reflect the strategic value of memory in the AI era.”\\nMicron’s HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and HBM4E is in development, with volume production expected in calendar 2027. Shares closed at $991.64 on July 9, up 247.66% year to date. Analysts maintain a consensus price target of $1,486, with the stock currently sporting a forward P/E of about 6.\\nNVIDIA’s Growth Is Creating an Unprecedented Demand Signal\\nOn the demand side, NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion. Shares closed at $202.78 on July 9. Every one of Nvidia’s Blackwell and Vera Rubin systems requires HBM stacks supplied at scale by SK Hynix today.\\nSK Hynix’s U.S. debut gives investors direct access to the company holding the strongest position in one of the AI buildout’s tightest bottlenecks. It controls roughly 58% of the HBM market, and new capacity will not materially alleviate the shortage before late 2027.\\nPartsinevelos closed with the industry’s most durable caveat: “The longer term risk, though, is that memory has never really met a supercycle that didn’t eventually crash .” History is unambiguous on that point. For now, the memory shortage is likely to persist into 2027, index inclusion should act as a mechanical bid for the stock, and the three players controlling roughly 95% of memory production remain in a pricing environment they have not enjoyed in over a decade.\\nThomas Richmond is a financial writer and content strategist with 5+ years of experience covering stocks and financial markets. He has published over 500 articles focused on individual stock analysis, helping investors better understand business fundamentals, stock valuations, and long-term opportunities.\\nSK Hynix crashed into bear-market territory in Seoul just days after a blockbuster U.S. debut, and Jim Cramer thinks Korean investors are missing something fundamental about where AI memory demand is actually headed.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"SK Hynix, described as a top seller of the high-bandwidth memory required for NVIDIA's most advanced chips, completed a roughly $26.5 billion Nasdaq capital raise at $149 per American depositary share, the second-largest share sale ever; CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos noted SK Hynix holds approximately 58% of the HBM market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"During a July 10 CNBC segment , Kristina Partsinevelos framed SK Hynix’s roughly $26.5 billion capital raise at $149 per American depositary share as a defining moment for the AI supply chain. The Korean memory giant controls approximately 58% of the high-bandwidth memory market, giving it a commanding position in the technology required to make NVIDIA’s most advanced chips work. The offering ranks as the second-largest share sale ever, trailing only SpaceX’s NASDAQ debut last month.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SK Hynix\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos reported SK Hynix revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year, with the company committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years (primarily in South Korea), and roughly $458 million of U.S. CHIPS Act funding earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Partsinevelos noted revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year, with the company committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea. A separate roughly $458 million from the U.S. CHIPS Act is earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"SK Hynix\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"CNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos said none of the new memory supply arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"“None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now,” Partsinevelos said.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Kristina Partsinevelos (CNBC)\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-10\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Micron said its HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and that HBM4E is in development with volume production expected in calendar 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and HBM4E is in development, with volume production expected in calendar 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Micron Technology\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-11\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will custom ASIC AI accelerators designed by Broadcom and Marvell take a growing share of the big four hyperscalers' AI accelerator spending in calendar 2027, at Nvidia's expense, given their lower cost per gigawatt?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The split of big-four hyperscaler 2027 AI accelerator spending (and of the 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity projected for 2027) between custom ASIC racks and Nvidia GPU racks, measured against the item's cost-per-gigawatt estimates of $6–11 billion for Broadcom/Marvell ASIC racks versus roughly $19 billion for Nvidia GB300-based racks and $25 billion under Vera Rubin.\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item: Morgan Stanley forecasts the big four will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure 'next year' and add 19.5 GW of capacity, the period in which the ASIC-versus-GPU mix plays out.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"Broadcom's and Marvell's disclosed custom AI accelerator (XPU) revenue in their quarterly results and annual reports covering calendar 2027, alongside the hyperscalers' own capex disclosures for 2027.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-6 tracks only the total size of big-four hyperscaler capex. This thesis tracks the mix within that spending: total AI capex can reach $1 trillion while Nvidia's merchant-GPU share holds (hyperscalers buy both ASICs and GPUs), or ASIC share can rise sharply even if total capex disappoints — the two answers can move in opposite directions.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5\",\n   \"publisher\": \"247wallst.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-13\",\n   \"title\": \"Broadcom and Marvell Are Bigger Threats to Nvidia Than You Know\",\n   \"text\": \"By Rich Duprey\\nBroadcom (AVGO) and Marvell (MRVL) design custom AI chips costing between $6 billion and $11 billion per gigawatt, compared to $19 billion for Nvidia GPU racks.\\nHyperscalers will spend over $1 trillion on AI next year, with Google alone adding more compute than all four combined did in 2025.\\nNvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) remains the undisputed leader of the AI chip market, but leadership and dominance are not the same thing. The next phase of the AI buildout is shifting from buying the fastest hardware available to lowering the cost of operating AI at hyperscale.\\nAlphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ), and Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ) are all pursuing custom silicon alongside Nvidia’s GPUs because every percentage point of efficiency matters when spending tens of billions of dollars annually on AI infrastructure.\\nThat trend doesn’t eliminate Nvidia’s advantage, but it does create a growing opportunity for the companies designing those custom chips—most notably Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) and Marvell Technology ( NASDAQ:MRVL ).\\nThe Battle Is Moving From Training to Cost Efficiency\\nNvidia still owns AI training. Its GPUs remain the gold standard for developing frontier models, and its CUDA software ecosystem continues to give it a competitive moat. But this is where the market is changing.\\nOnce models are trained, they spend years handling inference — answering prompts, generating images, and powering AI applications. Those workloads don’t always require Nvidia’s most powerful processors. They often reward lower costs and higher efficiency instead. That is where application-specific integrated circuits, or ASICs, enter the picture.\\nBroadcom has become the leading partner helping hyperscalers build custom AI accelerators, while Marvell has carved out a similar niche with customers including Amazon. Rather than selling chips under their own brands, they help cloud providers design silicon optimized for their own software and infrastructure.\\nMorgan Stanley says hyperscalers continue to expand their investments in proprietary silicon to improve total cost of ownership and reduce dependence on merchant GPU suppliers.\\nThe opportunity for Broadcom and Marvell is enormous. The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\\nThe $1 trillion AI infrastructure race is pivoting: training is out, and custom-built efficiency is in. See why the industry's 'Second Act' is making room for new leaders beyond Nvidia.\\nThe Economics Are Becoming Harder to Ignore\\nThe appeal of custom silicon comes down to dollars. Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion. It illustrates why hyperscalers are increasingly willing to invest years designing their own chips.\\nSurprisingly, this isn’t an either-or decision for hyperscalers. Google continues developing Tensor Processing Units with Broadcom while still purchasing enormous numbers of Nvidia GPUs. Amazon follows a similar strategy with Trainium alongside Nvidia deployments.\\nThat diversification gives cloud providers leverage in negotiations while matching the right chip to the right workload.\\nNvidia Is Still Winning — But Watch the Suppliers\\nGranted, Nvidia is hardly standing still. The company continues expanding beyond GPUs into networking, rack-scale systems, and software while opening technologies such as NVLink Fusion to custom silicon partners — including Marvell itself.\\nThat said, investors sometimes underestimate where the fastest incremental growth may occur. Every new custom AI accelerator designed by Google, Meta, Amazon, or another hyperscaler creates another opportunity for Broadcom or Marvell. They benefit whether customers build proprietary chips instead of buying more off-the-shelf processors, and they are standing directly in the path of a massive $1 trillion spending tsunami.\\nIn other words, Nvidia remains the king of AI compute, but Broadcom and Marvell are increasingly becoming the architects behind the industry’s second act.\\nIn short, investors shouldn’t mistake Nvidia’s dominance for exclusivity. The AI infrastructure market is expanding so rapidly that multiple winners can emerge. Nvidia still commands the premium end of AI computing, particularly for training, but custom silicon is becoming an essential part of every hyperscaler’s long-term strategy. Broadcom appears best positioned today thanks to its deep relationships with Google and Meta, while Marvell continues strengthening its foothold with Amazon and other large customers.\\nUltimately, investors looking beyond today’s GPU boom may find that the companies quietly designing tomorrow’s AI chips offer just as compelling a long-term opportunity.\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"Milk Road AI estimates, as cited by 247wallst.com's Rich Duprey, that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure with Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks costs between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based GPU racks, and that rack costs rise to $25 billion per gigawatt under Nvidia's Vera Rubin generation.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Broadcom (AVGO) and Marvell (MRVL) design custom AI chips costing between $6 billion and $11 billion per gigawatt, compared to $19 billion for Nvidia GPU racks.\",\n     \"Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Milk Road AI (cost estimate), cited by 247wallst.com\",\n    \"tier\": \"third_party_view\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"run\": \"u3\",\n  \"id\": \"clean-12\",\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How far will the US crackdown on advanced AI-chip exports to third countries escalate — will the US Commerce Department go beyond its May 2026 guidance and impose formal licensing requirements or other escalated restrictions on AI-chip shipments to third countries such as Malaysia, Singapore and Japan by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether the US Commerce Department / Bureau of Industry and Security moves beyond its May 2026 guidance to a binding export-licensing rule or other escalated restriction on AI-chip shipments to third countries, and the resulting constraint on Nvidia's sales to those markets.\",\n   \"period\": \"Nvidia's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027) — set by the reason: a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"A US Commerce Department / Bureau of Industry and Security Federal Register rule, licence requirement or formal export-control action.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-5 turns on Beijing's decision whether to allow Chinese commercial buyers to purchase H200 chips the US cleared in December 2025; this thesis turns on Washington's decision whether to tighten third-country diversion controls. The two can move independently — Beijing could reopen H200 purchases while Washington hardens third-country rules, or Washington could act while Beijing stays closed.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"id\": \"gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd\",\n   \"publisher\": \"businesstimes.com.sg\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia halves Asia buyer list in China chip crackdown, steps up due diligence: report\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new “white list” of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14).\\nOver the past few months, the chipmaker has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan, the report added, citing three people familiar with the matter.\\nReuters could not immediately verify the report. Nvidia did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment outside regular business hours.\\nThe tighter scrutiny comes as the Trump administration seeks to prevent advanced US chips from reaching Chinese entities through third countries.\\nThe US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia’s cutting-edge Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\\nNvidia has tightened compliance procedures following pressure from Washington, the FT said, adding that staff now visit customers’ data centres, verify contracts and interview end users as part of the checks.\\nThe US Department of Commerce is also involved, providing oversight and political backing, the report said.\\nThe department did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment outside regular business hours. REUTERS\"\n  },\n  \"linked_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new 'white list' of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported (citing three people familiar); the chipmaker has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan over the past few months and, following pressure from Washington, has tightened compliance procedures so that staff visit customers' data centres, verify contracts and interview end users.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new “white list” of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14).\",\n     \"Over the past few months, the chipmaker has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan, the report added, citing three people familiar with the matter.\",\n     \"Nvidia has tightened compliance procedures following pressure from Washington, the FT said, adding that staff now visit customers’ data centres, verify contracts and interview end users as part of the checks.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"Financial Times (unnamed sources)\",\n    \"tier\": \"rumour\",\n    \"statement_date\": \"2026-07-14\",\n    \"figures\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"summary\": \"The US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, amid concerns that Nvidia's cutting-edge Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions; the department is also involved in the effort, providing oversight and political backing, the Financial Times reported.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia’s cutting-edge Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\",\n     \"The US Department of Commerce is also involved, providing oversight and political backing, the report said.\"\n    ],\n    \"original_party\": \"US Commerce Department\",\n    \"tier\": \"fact\",\n    \"statement_date\": null,\n    \"figures\": []\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nCASE\\\", j+1, \\\"\\\\nTHESIS\\\", json.dumps(x[\\\"thesis\\\"],ensure_ascii=False), \\\"\\\\nARTICLE\\\", x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"text\\\"])) for j,x in enumerate(p) if 4*5 <= j < (4+1)*5]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nCASE\\\", j+1, \\\"\\\\nTHESIS\\\", json.dumps(x[\\\"thesis\\\"],ensure_ascii=False), \\\"\\\\nARTICLE\\\", x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"text\\\"])) for j,x in enumerate(p) if 2*5 <= j < (2+1)*5]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nCASE\\\", j+1, \\\"\\\\nTHESIS\\\", json.dumps(x[\\\"thesis\\\"],ensure_ascii=False), \\\"\\\\nARTICLE\\\", x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"text\\\"])) for j,x in enumerate(p) if 2*5 <= j < (2+1)*5]'\"","aggregated_output":"\nCASE 11 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will China's government approve imports of Nvidia's H200 AI chips into China — and roughly how many — enabling Nvidia to begin generating H200 revenue in the country?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Chinese authorities grant approval for Alibaba, ByteDance, DeepSeek and other firms to import Nvidia H200 chips (potentially a total below 200,000), as reflected in official Chinese government decisions and in Nvidia's disclosed China H200 revenue.\", \"period\": \"Nvidia's current fiscal year-end (fiscal year ending late January 2027), checked at its next annual report/10-K; set by the rule that a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\", \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\", \"settled_by\": \"A Chinese government/regulator decision or announcement on H200 import approvals, and Nvidia's disclosure (10-Q/10-K or earnings call) of H200 revenue in China.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-9 tracks a 12-month, survey-based outcome — the share of Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products versus Nvidia. This thesis tracks the discrete regulatory gate and permitted volume of H200 imports, which China decides on its own timetable: China could approve a batch of H200 imports (and Nvidia near-term China revenue) while the longer-run domestic budget share still rises toward 46%, and vice versa, so the two answers can diverge.\"} \nARTICLE China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models.\nChina will allow top AI companies like Alibaba and ByteDance to buy limited amounts of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing previous restrictions.\nChinese firms must request approval specifying the number of chips and reasons for purchase, with a possible total below 200,000.\nThe move responds to high AI demand but aims to protect China's chip industry and address cybersecurity concerns amid US-China tech tensions.\nChina plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site.\nChinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .\nThe companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information.\nThe chips have emerged as a geopolitical flashpoint for the world’s two largest economies.\nThough US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell the processors to China in December, the Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country.\nNvidia shares climbed 3.7 per cent to US$204.12 (S$264) on July 8 , part of a broader chip rally. It marked the stock’s biggest one-day rise in more than a month.\nThough Nvidia is now up 9.4 per cent in 2026 , that pales in comparison with the gains of most major semiconductor peers.\nIn a statement, Chinese embassy spokesperson Liu Chang said that the country’s position on US chip exports has been consistent.\n“We advocate that China and the United States achieve mutual benefit and win-win outcomes through cooperation, and we oppose the politicisation, instrumentalisation, and weaponisation of technological and economic issues,” he said. “We stand ready to work with all parties to jointly safeguard the stability of global industrial and supply chains.”\nA representative for Santa Clara, California-based Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment.\nThe H200 import snags hinged in part on Chinese concerns that a flood of American-designed AI processors would hinder the government’s long-held goal of developing an indigenous chip industry. Beijing has also cited fears that allowing American-made chips into the local market could pose a cybersecurity risk.\nBut soaring demand for AI processors helped spur the Chinese authorities to allow some H200 purchases, according to the Information, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter.\nLabs in China have been struggling, like their US counterparts, to find enough computing power to meet their growing needs to train and run new models.\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models. It was the most powerful AI chip on the market until the introduction of the company’s next-generation Blackwell models in late 2024.\nNvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 . Washington has continued to restrict sales of more capable AI chips to China on national security grounds.\nThe Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported. When it comes to AI infrastructure, that is not a large number. A single data centre can use more than 400,000 Blackwell processors.\nIn May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.\n“We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.\n\nCASE 12 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will France's competition authority (Autorité de la concurrence) conclude its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU-market practices by the check date, and will it impose remedies or fines?\", \"outcome\": \"The French competition authority's conclusion of its antitrust investigation into alleged anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets, and any remedies or fines imposed.\", \"period\": \"Nvidia's current fiscal year-end (fiscal year ending late January 2027); set by the rule that a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\", \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\", \"settled_by\": \"The French competition authority's published decision/conclusion of its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU practices.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-11 tracks a Chinese government decision on H200 import approvals (market access in China); this tracks a European antitrust-conduct decision with potential remedies/fines affecting Nvidia's conduct and competitive position in the EU cloud-AI/GPU market. The two decisions are made by different authorities on different questions and can move entirely independently.\"} \nARTICLE Meta Says AI Compute Isn’t Enough: Is the Infrastructure Selloff Over?\n5.3M Views · 140 Posts\n$NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ faced mounting pressure after bears added to their wagers against the chip giant amid increasing competition that threatened to challenge its dominance in the market for semiconductors that power artificial intelligence workloads.\nTrading in borrowed Nvidia shares that were sold short rose to 13.61 million shares Wednesday, from 11.69 million shares a day earlier, exchange data tracked by moomoo showed. The latest figure represented 9.2% of the shares that changed hands yesterday, when the stock advanced 3.7%, the biggest gain in five weeks. Shorting involves shares sold without the seller owning them, which is a common practice used to profit from a potential decline in a stock's price.\n$Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)$ to manufacture the AI chips, according to the report.\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\nStill, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\nNVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\nRegulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\nShare your thoughts on Nvidia in the comments section. Are retail investors losing their faith in Nvidia? Let your voice be heard by voting below.\nComments (4)\n\nCASE 13 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\", \"period\": \"2027-12-31 check date, within 18 months of the 2026-07-01 item\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's annual report/10-K guarantee and credit-support disclosures, and any 8-K/10-Q reporting signed credit-support agreements.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"No open thesis exists; this question tracks NVIDIA's own contingent credit exposure from supporting AI cloud capacity, which can move independently of GPU demand or product revenue.\"} \nARTICLE NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑scale AI services.\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\nTo address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\n\nCASE 14 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\", \"period\": \"2026-12-31 check date, based on the item's statement, published 2026-07-02, that Vera Rubin could begin shipping later this year.\", \"opens_as\": \"product_node\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA quarterly earnings release, 10-Q/10-K, or official product shipment announcement confirming first Vera Rubin shipments.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Vera Rubin's first-shipment timing depends on product execution and can move independently of whether NVIDIA extends guarantees or credit support to AI cloud partners, which clean-1 tracks.\"} \nARTICLE Microsoft ( MSFT +1.28% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) are two heavyweights in the technology world and key players in artificial intelligence (AI). Microsoft seems to have its hand in every tech market segment, especially anything involving enterprise customers. Meanwhile, Nvidia has become the de facto leader in GPU chips used in AI data centers.\nBoth stocks have made investors very wealthy over the years. But which of these top AI stocks is the better buy for the next three years? This article will explain why I like Microsoft a tad more. Although, as you'll see below, it's hard to go wrong with either one.\nVera Rubin likely means Nvidia has more growth ahead\nNvidia has enjoyed one of the most impressive growth spurts in history over these past few years as the AI build-out juiced demand for its GPUs. This supercycle continues to rage on. Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\nWall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year. Nvidia's valuation is already well below its high at less than 19 times sales, and it might become much cheaper as these next 24 months play out.\nMicrosoft is pivoting to capitalize on its enterprise customer base\nMicrosoft originally tied its AI hopes to OpenAI. Although Microsoft has done well with its OpenAI investment and enjoyed significant cloud computing growth as a result of the partnership, it has still struggled to establish itself as a key AI player. Users, especially enterprises, have opted for Anthropic's Claude among other AI models. As a result, Microsoft shifted to a multi-model strategy and has reportedly considered adding open-source models to Copilot Cowork to lower token costs.\nInvestors should applaud Microsoft's willingness to adapt. The company has deeply entrenched relationships with the enterprise world, which gives Microsoft more margin for error in fine-tuning its AI strategy than most companies. Plus, Microsoft's Azure remains a formidable growth engine, with a staggering $627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations.\nNvidia's data center business has grown so much that the stock has become a concentrated investment in this ongoing supercycle. That means Nvidia has a sky-high ceiling with Vera Rubin shipping soon, but it's riskier in that the floor is much lower if companies stop pouring all this money into AI infrastructure. Meanwhile, Microsoft remains highly diversified across software, cloud computing, and AI. That lowers the company's ceiling, but it's also the safer stock.\nMicrosoft's impressive track record typically earns it a premium valuation. The stock has traded at an average of almost 33 times its trailing-12-month earnings over the past decade, but trades at just 22 times its earnings today. Meanwhile, Wall Street analysts expect the company to grow earnings by 16% to 17% annually over the next three to five years.\nWhile some may opt for Nvidia's immense growth potential , Microsoft rarely offers a valuation compelling enough to buy into.\nJustin Pope is a contributing Motley Fool stock market analyst covering information technology, consumer discretionary, consumer staples, and industrials. Prior to The Motley Fool, Justin was a business manager for an industrial company.\nBy Justin Pope – Jul 2, 2026 at 12:43PM EST\n\nCASE 15 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\", \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal year-end (FY2027, late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision; the item (published 2026-07-02) gives no date for Beijing's decision.\", \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\", \"settled_by\": \"An official Chinese regulatory/government decision on H200 imports (e.g. CAC/MOFCOM announcement or licensing) and/or NVIDIA's quarterly filing or earnings disclosure stating whether H200 sales into China have resumed; also Chinese customs/import data.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Vera Rubin's first shipments (clean-2) depend on NVIDIA's own execution and its foundry/packaging supply and can happen regardless of China; China access depends on a decision by Beijing and can stay closed even with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule. Each independently moves data-center revenue, so an analyst would hold separate cards.\"} \nARTICLE Before Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S. government that Huawei could have enough chips to satisfy global demand.\nBy Noah Berman\nWhen Nvidia pitched the Trump administration last year to let it sell advanced AI chips to China, the world’s biggest chipmaker issued a clear warning: Huawei, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company warned that Huawei’s stockpile of chips, along with growing capacity at its manufacturing partner Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. (SMIC), give it “the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.”\n“If Nvidia is kept out of the China market, Huawei production and sales will soar,” the report said, although it did not specify a timeframe or refer to the differences in performance between Nvidia and Huawei chips.\nThe pitch — alongside Nvidia Chief Executive Jensen Huang’s personal diplomacy — worked. President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\nNvidia’s analysis sheds light on how the world’s most valuable company has presented Huawei’s growing capabilities as justification for it to be able to sell more to China, the world’s second-largest market for chips after the U.S.\nNvidia is attempting to “scare U.S. policymakers that Huawei is this great big threat,” says Gregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls. “They are, but not in the way Nvidia is claiming.”\n“[But] there is no apples-to-apples comparison between Nvidia chips and Huawei chips,” says Allen, who supports export controls. “Having a lot of chips is not a synonym for being competitive in the marketplace.”\nSince the Biden administration first imposed export controls on AI chips in 2022, Nvidia’s market share in China has fallen to zero, Huang has said .\nIn the report it presented to the government, Nvidia estimated that Huawei sold between 200,000 and 450,000 AI chips in 2024 and that it would sell 1 million in 2025. It also estimated that by the end of this year SMIC — which manufactures chips based on Huawei’s design and technology — will have made more than 700,000 Ascend 910C chips, one of Huawei’s most advanced, with production reaching 50,000 of these chips per month. By 2027, the report said, SMIC would be producing several million of Huawei’s less advanced 910B chips.\nHuawei simply does not pose a competitive threat to Nvidia globally right now. Huawei’s chips are nowhere near as good, and it can’t make nearly enough of them.\nChris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations\nThose numbers far exceed Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick’s estimate in congressional testimony last June that China could make just 200,000 advanced chips in 2025.\nA Nvidia spokesperson declined to comment on the report. “The ability of China’s domestic ecosystem to yield and scale competitive AI systems is a fact, evidenced by leading Chinese models that are optimized to perform on domestic hardware,” the spokesperson said.\nBut even on Nvidia’s numbers, Huawei would remain way short of the American company’s chip output, let alone being able to “satisfy global demand.”\nIn a report published in December, Chris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations, estimated that if Huawei made 800,000 Ascend 910C chips in 2025 — a figure at the high end of several estimates — that would amount to just 5.3 percent of the total processing power of Nvidia’s chip production that year.\nMcGuire also estimates that Nvidia’s lead by performance will only grow, even if Huawei were to make 2 million AI chips this year and 4 million in 2027. Industry analysts measure performance by how many calculations a chip can do per second and how fast it can move data around, with Nvidia’s best chips capable of doing far more than those of Huawei.\n“Huawei simply does not pose a competitive threat to Nvidia globally right now,” says McGuire, formerly a national security official who helped design U.S. chip controls. “Huawei’s chips are nowhere near as good, and it can’t make nearly enough of them.”\nIronically, he adds, Nvidia has a lead over Huawei largely because of U.S. export controls on semiconductor manufacturing equipment, the lack of which has degraded China’s ability to make AI chips.\nNvidia’s analysis did not contain any of its own new modeling of Huawei’s capabilities. Instead its report cited estimates from outside research groups, including think tank the Center for Strategic and International Studies, the consultancy Semianalysis, and a handful of Wall Street firms including Bernstein and Morgan Stanley.\nThe report also cited Reuters reporting that Huawei had built a stockpile of between 2 and 3 million of its own AI chips made by Taiwanese chipmaker TSMC, which it had obtained via illegal diversion by the Chinese company Sophgo. Companies such as TSMC that make chips using American technology must follow U.S. export controls, which effectively prevent sales to Huawei.\nBy choosing to highlight that stockpile in its report, Nvidia is in fact underscoring how export controls are succeeding in holding Huawei back, says Lennart Heim , an independent researcher who studies chip and AI policy.\n“If they were able to produce that many chips themselves, why would they go to TSMC?” he asks. Even though figures such as 2 to 3 million AI chips sound significant, “this number is tiny” relative to the more than 60 million 910C-equivalent chips that the U.S. will have produced between January 2025 and the end of this year, Heim adds.\nHuawei made just 6 percent of the total AI chips sold worldwide during the first quarter of this year, and half that amount after adjusting for the fact that the company’s chips are not as powerful as Nvidia’s, according to Epoch AI, a research institute.\nWhile Nvidia’s report framed its argument on AI chip export controls around its competition with Huawei, others argue that a more important issue is that the restrictions will incentivize Chinese companies to try to catch up with their American rivals.\n“There are good arguments against export controls,” Heim says. But instead of articulating those, Nvidia is “trying to use technical terms to confuse people.”\nNvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe. I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.\nGregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls\n“Huawei has no way right now to satisfy global demand,” he adds.\nHuawei has certainly been trying to improve its offering. In March, it introduced a new chip, the 950PR, and is preparing to launch another later this year. Yet even those chips fail to match the quality of the H200 because they have slower memory , according to Heim. They lag even further behind Nvidia’s most advanced Blackwell chips .\nHuawei and SMIC did not respond to requests for comment.\nThe Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\n“We sell the first, best and most of our technology to America and offer competitive products in China to commercial users vetted and approved by the U.S. government,” says the Nvidia spokesperson. “National security is first and foremost. Where commercial opportunity conflicts with U.S. national security, national security comes first.”\nBut Nvidia is losing sympathy in Washington, says Allen, the analyst.\n“Nvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe,” he says. “I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.”\nNoah Berman is a staff writer for The Wire based in New York. He previously wrote about economics and technology at the Council on Foreign Relations. His work has appeared in the Boston Globe and PBS News . He graduated from Georgetown University.\nBy Michael Kovrig\nA new book provides searing testimonials of how the Chinese Communist Party’s thought remolding and coercive interrogations continue under the legal obfuscation of “residential surveillance at a designated location”.\nBy Savannah Billman\nBy Eliot Chen\nThe former State Department official in the Biden administration talks about leading the first talks with China on AI, and what the realistic goals should be of any dialogue between the two superpowers about the...\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nCASE\\\", j+1, \\\"\\\\nTHESIS\\\", json.dumps(x[\\\"thesis\\\"],ensure_ascii=False), \\\"\\\\nARTICLE\\\", x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"text\\\"])) for j,x in enumerate(p) if 4*5 <= j < (4+1)*5]'\"","aggregated_output":"\nCASE 21 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will France's competition authority reach a conclusion/decision — a fine, behavioral remedies or closure — in its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA's alleged anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets by NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027)?\", \"outcome\": \"The French competition authority's decision concluding its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA, including any fine or behavioral remedies imposed.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision; the item gives no date for the conclusion, only that it is 'nearing a conclusion'.\", \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\", \"settled_by\": \"The French competition authority's formal decision/announcement concluding its investigation, and NVIDIA's related disclosure of any fine or remedies.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 tracks Beijing's decision on whether to allow H200 sales into mainland China; this tracks a European antitrust decision on NVIDIA's cloud-AI/GPU conduct. They are different regulators, jurisdictions and subject matter and can move apart — China can grant (or keep barring) H200 access while France imposes remedies, or the reverse.\"} \nARTICLE Meta Says AI Compute Isn’t Enough: Is the Infrastructure Selloff Over?\n5.3M Views · 140 Posts\n$NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ faced mounting pressure after bears added to their wagers against the chip giant amid increasing competition that threatened to challenge its dominance in the market for semiconductors that power artificial intelligence workloads.\nTrading in borrowed Nvidia shares that were sold short rose to 13.61 million shares Wednesday, from 11.69 million shares a day earlier, exchange data tracked by moomoo showed. The latest figure represented 9.2% of the shares that changed hands yesterday, when the stock advanced 3.7%, the biggest gain in five weeks. Shorting involves shares sold without the seller owning them, which is a common practice used to profit from a potential decline in a stock's price.\n$Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)$ to manufacture the AI chips, according to the report.\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\nStill, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\nNVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\nRegulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\nShare your thoughts on Nvidia in the comments section. Are retail investors losing their faith in Nvidia? Let your voice be heard by voting below.\nComments (4)\n\nCASE 22 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much new high-bandwidth memory (HBM) capacity will the three main producers (SK Hynix, Micron, Samsung) bring online, and will the HBM shortage keep memory prices at record highs through late 2027 — a supply and input-cost constraint on NVIDIA's GPU shipments?\", \"outcome\": \"The state of HBM supply and pricing through late 2027: whether any meaningful new HBM capacity comes online before then, and whether record-high HBM/memory prices persist.\", \"period\": \"Late 2027 (the item, published 2026-07-10, says no new HBM supply arrives before late 2027, keeping the shortage and record prices into 2027); set by the item's forecast, settled by memory-market research and producers' capacity disclosures for 2027.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"Memory-market research releases (e.g., TrendForce/Counterpoint HBM capacity and contract-price reports) and the HBM producers' (SK Hynix, Micron, Samsung) capacity and ramp disclosures covering 2027.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-6 tracks the dollar amount of NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments disclosed at its annual report (a company-specific disclosure); this thesis tracks the external aggregate HBM capacity/price environment that determines whether those purchases can be filled and at what input cost. They can move apart: NVIDIA's commitments could rise while external HBM supply loosens and prices fall, or fall while supply stays tight.\"} \nARTICLE Memory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut - 24/7 Wall St.\nMemory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut\nSK Hynix just pulled off one of the largest share sales in history, and one memory market expert says U.S. investors have been missing the single most critical bottleneck in the entire AI supply chain. Here is why that changes…\nBy Thomas Richmond\nNVIDIA's $82B quarterly revenue and Micron's 346% year-over-year sales surge confirm AI chips are driving an unprecedented memory demand cycle.\nSK Hynix controls 58% of the HBM market, and its $26.5B Nasdaq debut finally gives U.S. investors direct access to NVIDIA's most critical chip supplier.\nPartsinevelos warned that no new HBM supply arrives before late 2027, keeping record prices intact while $14B in passive index buying looms.\nCNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos reports on SK Hynix's significant $26.5 billion Nasdaq debut, highlighting its crucial role in the AI memory market alongside NVIDIA. © 24/7 Wall St.\nDuring a July 10 CNBC segment , Kristina Partsinevelos framed SK Hynix’s roughly $26.5 billion capital raise at $149 per American depositary share as a defining moment for the AI supply chain. The Korean memory giant controls approximately 58% of the high-bandwidth memory market, giving it a commanding position in the technology required to make NVIDIA’s most advanced chips work. The offering ranks as the second-largest share sale ever, trailing only SpaceX’s NASDAQ debut last month.\nFor U.S. investors, the debut closes a long-standing accessibility gap. SK Hynix has been the essential HBM supplier to NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) throughout the AI buildout, yet trading in the Korean parent required navigating Seoul-listed shares. It also arrives as Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang’s “largest infrastructure expansion in human history” creates extraordinary demand for a product that remains in short supply.\nSK Hynix Controls the Memory Powering the AI Revolution\nThe core setup for SK Hynix, according to Partsinevelos: “SK Hynix is the world leader in high bandwidth memory . The memory that feeds AI chips with roughly 58% of that market. Micron and Samsung competitors split roughly 21% each … as per Counterpoint Research.” She added a blunter framing of the competitive stack: “SK Hynix is bigger, cheaper and closer to NVIDIA.”\nPartsinevelos noted revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year, with the company committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea. A separate roughly $458 million from the U.S. CHIPS Act is earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\nNew Supply Will Likely Not Arrive Until 2027\nMemory prices have been incredibly strong due to tight supply. “None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now,” Partsinevelos said. Passive flows may amplify the near-term move: “Berkeley estimates the stock could see up to roughly $14 billion in passive buying alone as it enters the major indices just over the next few months.”\nReaders looking for the broader thesis on companies riding this wave can dig into our Free Report: 7 Stocks Powering the AI Boom (That Aren’t Chipmakers) .\nMicron Shows How Valuable AI Memory Has Become\nSK Hynix’s U.S.-listed challenger, Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ), shows why memory investors are watching HBM share so closely. Micron posted Q3 FY2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year, with non-GAAP diluted EPS of $25.11 and GAAP gross margin of 84.6%. Guidance for the following quarter calls for revenue of $50.0 billion plus or minus $1.0 billion. CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in the Q3 release that results “reflect the strategic value of memory in the AI era.”\nMicron’s HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and HBM4E is in development, with volume production expected in calendar 2027. Shares closed at $991.64 on July 9, up 247.66% year to date. Analysts maintain a consensus price target of $1,486, with the stock currently sporting a forward P/E of about 6.\nNVIDIA’s Growth Is Creating an Unprecedented Demand Signal\nOn the demand side, NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion. Shares closed at $202.78 on July 9. Every one of Nvidia’s Blackwell and Vera Rubin systems requires HBM stacks supplied at scale by SK Hynix today.\nSK Hynix’s U.S. debut gives investors direct access to the company holding the strongest position in one of the AI buildout’s tightest bottlenecks. It controls roughly 58% of the HBM market, and new capacity will not materially alleviate the shortage before late 2027.\nPartsinevelos closed with the industry’s most durable caveat: “The longer term risk, though, is that memory has never really met a supercycle that didn’t eventually crash .” History is unambiguous on that point. For now, the memory shortage is likely to persist into 2027, index inclusion should act as a mechanical bid for the stock, and the three players controlling roughly 95% of memory production remain in a pricing environment they have not enjoyed in over a decade.\nThomas Richmond is a financial writer and content strategist with 5+ years of experience covering stocks and financial markets. He has published over 500 articles focused on individual stock analysis, helping investors better understand business fundamentals, stock valuations, and long-term opportunities.\nSK Hynix crashed into bear-market territory in Seoul just days after a blockbuster U.S. debut, and Jim Cramer thinks Korean investors are missing something fundamental about where AI memory demand is actually headed.\n\nCASE 23 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K), expected early 2027 — set by the reason: a credit-support obligation being undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report / Form 10-K, including the guarantees and commitments note and MD&A disclosing credit-support and revenue-share arrangements.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"No open thesis exists; the size and credit-risk impact of NVIDIA's credit-support backstops for AI-cloud customers can move independently of GPU demand and product cycles, so it warrants its own tracking card.\"} \nARTICLE NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑scale AI services.\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\nTo address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\n\nCASE 24 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\", \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\", \"period\": \"2026 calendar year — set because the item reports the shift is beginning as of mid-2026 and gives no specific release date.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"2026 industry server/accelerator shipment and attach-rate data (e.g., IDC, Dell'Oro, hyperscaler or OEM disclosures) plus NVIDIA and Intel product disclosures.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks NVIDIA's credit support and contingent obligations to AI cloud partners; this tracks an industry CPU:GPU mix that can change NVIDIA's GPU demand and system design independently of any credit-support decision.\"} \nARTICLE Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix. While currently trading above consensus analyst targets, the valuation is supported by evolving commercial fundamentals. Technically, the stock remains in a neutral-bullish trend, with a potential move toward $159.90 if it clears $150.40 resistance.\nTradingKey - Intel Corporation (NASDAQ: INTC ) sits at $140.05, holding firm on the ascending trendline within the 2H timeframe. The RSI indicator is currently reading 55.21, positioning it as neutral-bullish, while green candles continue to soak up sell orders in close proximity to the trendline-resistance alignment. This rally translates into a ~465% gain for the company's stock over the prior 12 months, climbing from a $18.97 low, a climb so massive that the 12-month average analyst price target of $93.93 now sits 33% behind the actual price of shares, creating an enduring wedge between where analysts have modelled the stock to end and where they find it today.\nIntel’s July investment thesis does not hinge on the Apple deal or Trump’s social media post, topics I’ve written about in the past few issues; rather, it turns on the fact that NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel as well as opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems. Put another way, it’s the most important GPU company of our day endorsing Intel’s CPU.\nJensen Huang Picked Intel — What the Xeon 6 Selection for DGX Rubin Means\nNVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. CEO Lip-Bu Tan recently said Xeon 6 now plays the role of “the orchestration layer and critical control plane for the entire AI stack” and highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the ratio of CPUs needed to support the GPU is actually increasing, not decreasing (the prior narrative was that GPUs would ultimately take over the CPU role).\nJensen Huang’s decision to build the DGX Rubin NVL8 on Xeon 6 coupled with their $5B equity investment in Intel represents the strongest signal yet from a third party that Intel’s CPU is competitive. This is not an Apple rumor or an announcement on social media: This is a CPU architecture decision from the world’s largest infrastructure company in AI.\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges). Meanwhile, Intel Foundry saw revenue of $5.4B (up 16% year-over-year), which translates to a Foundry segment that is now over $20B in annualised revenue (still negative earnings but not quite the rounding error it was). CFO David Zinsner characterized the quarter as having “unprecedented demand for silicon” and Q2 guidance is $13.8 to $14.8B, indicating continued sequential revenue strength. About 60% of this quarter's revenue mix now comes from AI-linked sources, compared with a little less than 25% a year ago.\nThe Google Deal — Multiyear, Locked Pricing, Custom ASIC Co-Development\nGoogle's Q1 2026 announcement is the second major confirmed hyperscaler commitment that brings contractual revenue certainty to the Intel thesis. Google inked a multiyear deal for continued use of Intel Xeon processors in Google's workload-optimized instances, including the newest Xeon 6 processors that power C4 and N4 instances. The companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks.\nCEO Tan said the agreements span three to five years, with locked volume and pricing. That represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term. The structure directly addresses the main criticism of Intel's revenue run-rate so far this year, which is that demand is not real but order-driven (front-loaded).\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\nThis deal stack is where the current price stands relative to the average price target of $93.93 on the stock from analyst coverage. This gap represents how fast the new Intel story is unfolding versus the rate at which sell-side analysts are updating their models: Cantor Fitzgerald raised its price target to $150 from $90 in early July 1 and still held a Neutral rating noting its price target remains a mere 13.9% above current levels.\nCurrent valuation checks (per Simply Wall St) show Intel trades at a price-to-sales multiple of 13.1x vs. a suggested fair price multiple of 15.1x given its combination of foundry + AI exposure, and scores 2 of 6 on its broader valuation checks. While INTC is not cheap on an absolute basis, it's not obviously expensive when compared to the group of peers and the confirmed customer stack.\nINTC Technical Setup — Trendline Defense at $140, RSI 55.21, Target $159.90\nOn the 2H chart, INTC is holding the ascending multi-touch black trendline after a series of rejections of the descending red trendlines from the $169 plus highs and is holding above the EMAs200 at $106.07 with higher lows. RSI at 55.21 is neutral-bullish with room to rise above before becoming overbought and does not have any divergence, as buyer absorption of sell orders (seen on green candles) near the trendline-resistance confluence.\nResistance overhead sits at $150.43 to $159.97. If INTC holds the trendline, and confirms a move above $150.40, the next price target is $159.90. If it breaks down below $126.20, the next price target is $117.07.\nEntry: Long above $150.40 — trendline and resistance cleared\nTarget: $159.90 — trendline bounce extension\nStop Loss: Close below $126.20 — ascending trendline fails\nNVIDIA selection: Xeon 6 chosen as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8; $5B equity investment\nGoogle deal: Multiyear, locked pricing 3–5 years, custom ASIC IPU co-development\nNext earnings: July 23, 2026\nWhy Did NVIDIA Invest $5 Billion in Intel and Select Xeon 6 for Its AI Systems?\nNVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next product cycle? Here's the explanation from Lip-Bu Tan: \"The CPU has become the orchestration layer, the control plane of the entire AI infrastructure. In inference workloads, we're starting to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs towards 1 CPU to 1 GPU.\" Jensen Huang's choice of Xeon 6 is a product architecture decision, by the company that's the benchmark for defining AI infrastructure, not an investment, or a promotion, from NVIDIA: Jensen's selection of Xeon 6 is the best possible validation of Intel's CPU competitive position.\nWhat Did Intel Report in Q1 2026 and What Is Q2 Guidance?\nIn Q1 2026, Data Center and AI (DC&A) was $5.1 billion (+22% YoY) and Foundry was $5.4 billion (+16% YoY). Non-GAAP earnings per share was $0.29 per share, compared with $0.73 (GAAP) because of foundry capital investment charges for 18A. Intel expects Q2 to continue expanding sequentially with revenues of $13.8 billion to $14.8 billion. \"AI-linked revenue is now approximately 60% of the quarterly mix, as compared with a year ago when it was under 25%,\" said CFO David Zinsner. The quarter was defined by unprecedented demand for silicon and Intel's determination to use the entire factory network as a means to maximize output. \"Our long-term deals span three to five years, with locked volumes and pricing,\" said CFO Zinsner.\nWhy Is the Average Analyst Target at $93.93 When Intel Trades at $140?\nThat's a 33% difference, which shows how rapidly the Intel story has evolved relative to the time required for sell-side equity research analysts to update their models. Cantor Fitzgerald increased its 12-month price target to $150 on July 1 while keeping a Neutral rating, stating that the new price target was 13.9% above the current stock price. The majority of Wall Street firms haven't yet updated their financial models to account for the NVIDIA $5B equity investment, the Google multiyear deal, and a full risk production announcement for 18A. The Simple Wall St analysis places \"fair value\" Price/Sales of 15.1 vs. 13.1, which suggests that Intel is not significantly overvalued vs. peer group P/S at current trading levels.\nIntel is up 465% over the past year and now trades 33% above the consensus analyst 12-month price target of $93.93. That gap exists because the story, now anchored around NVIDIA's investment and product choice, the Google and Microsoft signed deals, has moved a long distance relative to the ability of sell-side research analysts to update models. Those commercial agreements are no longer a rumour.\nThe Intel story now includes a $5 billion equity investment from NVIDIA for Xeon 6 as the host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8, a Google multiyear agreement with locked volume and pricing (3-to-5-year deployment and co-development of Intel custom AI accelerators (ASIC)), and Microsoft has signed a deal for wafers using 18A technology.\nQ1 2026 was Data Center and AI $5.1 billion (+22%), with an average of 60% AI-linked of the mix. Technicals are a defense at $140, with the average ascending trendline, plus RSI at 55.21, neutral-bullish. Confirmation above $150.40 will send prices to a $159.90 price target. A stop at $126.20 would protect against a failure. Q2 2026 guidance to be issued at July 23 earnings is the next checkpoint, where we'll hopefully see a large component of the stack materialize in guidance.\n\nCASE 25 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\", \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\", \"period\": \"First shipments by end of calendar 2026 — set by the item: the platform 'could begin shipping later this year' in an article dated 2 July 2026.\", \"opens_as\": \"product_node\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's quarterly earnings release and Form 10-Q disclosures on the data center product ramp, plus first-shipment confirmation from NVIDIA and its server/system partners.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks how much credit support NVIDIA extends to AI cloud partners and the resulting contingent credit exposure; the Vera Rubin shipping milestone can be reached or missed on its own (production, packaging/HBM supply, customer qualification) regardless of how much backstop NVIDIA provides, so an analyst would bet on the two separately.\"} \nARTICLE Microsoft ( MSFT +1.28% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) are two heavyweights in the technology world and key players in artificial intelligence (AI). Microsoft seems to have its hand in every tech market segment, especially anything involving enterprise customers. Meanwhile, Nvidia has become the de facto leader in GPU chips used in AI data centers.\nBoth stocks have made investors very wealthy over the years. But which of these top AI stocks is the better buy for the next three years? This article will explain why I like Microsoft a tad more. Although, as you'll see below, it's hard to go wrong with either one.\nVera Rubin likely means Nvidia has more growth ahead\nNvidia has enjoyed one of the most impressive growth spurts in history over these past few years as the AI build-out juiced demand for its GPUs. This supercycle continues to rage on. Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\nWall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year. Nvidia's valuation is already well below its high at less than 19 times sales, and it might become much cheaper as these next 24 months play out.\nMicrosoft is pivoting to capitalize on its enterprise customer base\nMicrosoft originally tied its AI hopes to OpenAI. Although Microsoft has done well with its OpenAI investment and enjoyed significant cloud computing growth as a result of the partnership, it has still struggled to establish itself as a key AI player. Users, especially enterprises, have opted for Anthropic's Claude among other AI models. As a result, Microsoft shifted to a multi-model strategy and has reportedly considered adding open-source models to Copilot Cowork to lower token costs.\nInvestors should applaud Microsoft's willingness to adapt. The company has deeply entrenched relationships with the enterprise world, which gives Microsoft more margin for error in fine-tuning its AI strategy than most companies. Plus, Microsoft's Azure remains a formidable growth engine, with a staggering $627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations.\nNvidia's data center business has grown so much that the stock has become a concentrated investment in this ongoing supercycle. That means Nvidia has a sky-high ceiling with Vera Rubin shipping soon, but it's riskier in that the floor is much lower if companies stop pouring all this money into AI infrastructure. Meanwhile, Microsoft remains highly diversified across software, cloud computing, and AI. That lowers the company's ceiling, but it's also the safer stock.\nMicrosoft's impressive track record typically earns it a premium valuation. The stock has traded at an average of almost 33 times its trailing-12-month earnings over the past decade, but trades at just 22 times its earnings today. Meanwhile, Wall Street analysts expect the company to grow earnings by 16% to 17% annually over the next three to five years.\nWhile some may opt for Nvidia's immense growth potential , Microsoft rarely offers a valuation compelling enough to buy into.\nJustin Pope is a contributing Motley Fool stock market analyst covering information technology, consumer discretionary, consumer staples, and industrials. Prior to The Motley Fool, Justin was a business manager for an industrial company.\nBy Justin Pope – Jul 2, 2026 at 12:43PM EST\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nCASE\\\", j+1, \\\"\\\\nTHESIS\\\", json.dumps(x[\\\"thesis\\\"],ensure_ascii=False), \\\"\\\\nARTICLE\\\", x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"text\\\"])) for j,x in enumerate(p) if 6*5 <= j < (6+1)*5]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nCASE\\\", j+1, \\\"\\\\nTHESIS\\\", json.dumps(x[\\\"thesis\\\"],ensure_ascii=False), \\\"\\\\nARTICLE\\\", x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"text\\\"])) for j,x in enumerate(p) if 0*5 <= j < (0+1)*5]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nCASE\\\", j+1, \\\"\\\\nTHESIS\\\", json.dumps(x[\\\"thesis\\\"],ensure_ascii=False), \\\"\\\\nARTICLE\\\", x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"text\\\"])) for j,x in enumerate(p) if 6*5 <= j < (6+1)*5]'\"","aggregated_output":"\nCASE 31 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will the French competition authority conclude its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU practices with an adverse decision, fine or remedy, and by when?\", \"outcome\": \"The French competition authority's conclusion of its antitrust probe into Nvidia's alleged anti-competitive conduct in cloud-AI and GPU markets, including the decision date, finding and any fine or remedy imposed.\", \"period\": \"Nvidia's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027) — set because a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end; the item reports the probe is 'nearing a conclusion.'\", \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\", \"settled_by\": \"The French competition authority's (Autorité de la concurrence) published decision or conclusion statement on its investigation into cloud-AI and GPU markets.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-5 tracks whether Beijing permits Chinese buyers to purchase H200 chips (China export access). This thesis tracks a separate EU/French antitrust conduct finding and any fine or remedy, whose outcome can move independently: Beijing could open H200 access while the French authority rules adversely, or vice versa.\"} \nARTICLE Meta Says AI Compute Isn’t Enough: Is the Infrastructure Selloff Over?\n5.3M Views · 140 Posts\n$NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ faced mounting pressure after bears added to their wagers against the chip giant amid increasing competition that threatened to challenge its dominance in the market for semiconductors that power artificial intelligence workloads.\nTrading in borrowed Nvidia shares that were sold short rose to 13.61 million shares Wednesday, from 11.69 million shares a day earlier, exchange data tracked by moomoo showed. The latest figure represented 9.2% of the shares that changed hands yesterday, when the stock advanced 3.7%, the biggest gain in five weeks. Shorting involves shares sold without the seller owning them, which is a common practice used to profit from a potential decline in a stock's price.\n$Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)$ to manufacture the AI chips, according to the report.\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\nStill, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\nNVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\nRegulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\nShare your thoughts on Nvidia in the comments section. Are retail investors losing their faith in Nvidia? Let your voice be heard by voting below.\nComments (4)\n\nCASE 32 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much new high-bandwidth memory (HBM) production capacity will SK Hynix, Micron and Samsung bring online in calendar 2027, and will it arrive early enough to ease the current HBM shortage and record prices that gate NVIDIA's GPU output?\", \"outcome\": \"The combined new HBM capacity the three producers add in 2027 and whether any of it arrives before late 2027, easing the shortage; measured against the item's forecast that no new HBM supply arrives before late 2027.\", \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item's forecast that 'None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027,' which places the supply-easing question in 2027.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"HBM capacity disclosures and HBM contract/spot-price reporting from SK Hynix, Micron and Samsung (company results, TrendForce/Counterpoint industry data) covering 2027.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-8 tracks the dollar amount of NVIDIA's own supply/purchase commitments on its balance sheet; this thesis tracks the physical availability and prices of HBM from its suppliers. They can move independently — NVIDIA could raise commitments (paying more) while HBM stays tight, or HBM supply could ease while commitments remain high — so an analyst would bet on the two separately.\"} \nARTICLE Memory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut - 24/7 Wall St.\nMemory Market Expert: “SK Hynix Is Bigger, Cheaper and Closer to NVIDIA.” Inside Its $26.5 Billion Nasdaq Debut\nSK Hynix just pulled off one of the largest share sales in history, and one memory market expert says U.S. investors have been missing the single most critical bottleneck in the entire AI supply chain. Here is why that changes…\nBy Thomas Richmond\nNVIDIA's $82B quarterly revenue and Micron's 346% year-over-year sales surge confirm AI chips are driving an unprecedented memory demand cycle.\nSK Hynix controls 58% of the HBM market, and its $26.5B Nasdaq debut finally gives U.S. investors direct access to NVIDIA's most critical chip supplier.\nPartsinevelos warned that no new HBM supply arrives before late 2027, keeping record prices intact while $14B in passive index buying looms.\nCNBC's Kristina Partsinevelos reports on SK Hynix's significant $26.5 billion Nasdaq debut, highlighting its crucial role in the AI memory market alongside NVIDIA. © 24/7 Wall St.\nDuring a July 10 CNBC segment , Kristina Partsinevelos framed SK Hynix’s roughly $26.5 billion capital raise at $149 per American depositary share as a defining moment for the AI supply chain. The Korean memory giant controls approximately 58% of the high-bandwidth memory market, giving it a commanding position in the technology required to make NVIDIA’s most advanced chips work. The offering ranks as the second-largest share sale ever, trailing only SpaceX’s NASDAQ debut last month.\nFor U.S. investors, the debut closes a long-standing accessibility gap. SK Hynix has been the essential HBM supplier to NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) throughout the AI buildout, yet trading in the Korean parent required navigating Seoul-listed shares. It also arrives as Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang’s “largest infrastructure expansion in human history” creates extraordinary demand for a product that remains in short supply.\nSK Hynix Controls the Memory Powering the AI Revolution\nThe core setup for SK Hynix, according to Partsinevelos: “SK Hynix is the world leader in high bandwidth memory . The memory that feeds AI chips with roughly 58% of that market. Micron and Samsung competitors split roughly 21% each … as per Counterpoint Research.” She added a blunter framing of the competitive stack: “SK Hynix is bigger, cheaper and closer to NVIDIA.”\nPartsinevelos noted revenue is expected to triple to $235 billion this year, with the company committing over $720 billion in capital investment over the coming years, primarily in South Korea. A separate roughly $458 million from the U.S. CHIPS Act is earmarked for an Indiana advanced packaging facility.\nNew Supply Will Likely Not Arrive Until 2027\nMemory prices have been incredibly strong due to tight supply. “None of that supply, though, arrives before late 2027, which keeps the memory shortage going and record-high prices intact for now,” Partsinevelos said. Passive flows may amplify the near-term move: “Berkeley estimates the stock could see up to roughly $14 billion in passive buying alone as it enters the major indices just over the next few months.”\nReaders looking for the broader thesis on companies riding this wave can dig into our Free Report: 7 Stocks Powering the AI Boom (That Aren’t Chipmakers) .\nMicron Shows How Valuable AI Memory Has Become\nSK Hynix’s U.S.-listed challenger, Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ), shows why memory investors are watching HBM share so closely. Micron posted Q3 FY2026 revenue of $41.456 billion, up 345.7% year over year, with non-GAAP diluted EPS of $25.11 and GAAP gross margin of 84.6%. Guidance for the following quarter calls for revenue of $50.0 billion plus or minus $1.0 billion. CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in the Q3 release that results “reflect the strategic value of memory in the AI era.”\nMicron’s HBM4, built on 1-beta DRAM, is in high-volume shipments for its lead customer platform, and HBM4E is in development, with volume production expected in calendar 2027. Shares closed at $991.64 on July 9, up 247.66% year to date. Analysts maintain a consensus price target of $1,486, with the stock currently sporting a forward P/E of about 6.\nNVIDIA’s Growth Is Creating an Unprecedented Demand Signal\nOn the demand side, NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and total supply-related commitments of $119.0 billion. Shares closed at $202.78 on July 9. Every one of Nvidia’s Blackwell and Vera Rubin systems requires HBM stacks supplied at scale by SK Hynix today.\nSK Hynix’s U.S. debut gives investors direct access to the company holding the strongest position in one of the AI buildout’s tightest bottlenecks. It controls roughly 58% of the HBM market, and new capacity will not materially alleviate the shortage before late 2027.\nPartsinevelos closed with the industry’s most durable caveat: “The longer term risk, though, is that memory has never really met a supercycle that didn’t eventually crash .” History is unambiguous on that point. For now, the memory shortage is likely to persist into 2027, index inclusion should act as a mechanical bid for the stock, and the three players controlling roughly 95% of memory production remain in a pricing environment they have not enjoyed in over a decade.\nThomas Richmond is a financial writer and content strategist with 5+ years of experience covering stocks and financial markets. He has published over 500 articles focused on individual stock analysis, helping investors better understand business fundamentals, stock valuations, and long-term opportunities.\nSK Hynix crashed into bear-market territory in Seoul just days after a blockbuster U.S. debut, and Jim Cramer thinks Korean investors are missing something fundamental about where AI memory demand is actually headed.\n\nCASE 33 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will custom ASIC AI accelerators designed by Broadcom and Marvell take a growing share of the big four hyperscalers' AI accelerator spending in calendar 2027, at Nvidia's expense, given their lower cost per gigawatt?\", \"outcome\": \"The split of big-four hyperscaler 2027 AI accelerator spending (and of the 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity projected for 2027) between custom ASIC racks and Nvidia GPU racks, measured against the item's cost-per-gigawatt estimates of $6–11 billion for Broadcom/Marvell ASIC racks versus roughly $19 billion for Nvidia GB300-based racks and $25 billion under Vera Rubin.\", \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item: Morgan Stanley forecasts the big four will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure 'next year' and add 19.5 GW of capacity, the period in which the ASIC-versus-GPU mix plays out.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"Broadcom's and Marvell's disclosed custom AI accelerator (XPU) revenue in their quarterly results and annual reports covering calendar 2027, alongside the hyperscalers' own capex disclosures for 2027.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-6 tracks only the total size of big-four hyperscaler capex. This thesis tracks the mix within that spending: total AI capex can reach $1 trillion while Nvidia's merchant-GPU share holds (hyperscalers buy both ASICs and GPUs), or ASIC share can rise sharply even if total capex disappoints — the two answers can move in opposite directions.\"} \nARTICLE By Rich Duprey\nBroadcom (AVGO) and Marvell (MRVL) design custom AI chips costing between $6 billion and $11 billion per gigawatt, compared to $19 billion for Nvidia GPU racks.\nHyperscalers will spend over $1 trillion on AI next year, with Google alone adding more compute than all four combined did in 2025.\nNvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) remains the undisputed leader of the AI chip market, but leadership and dominance are not the same thing. The next phase of the AI buildout is shifting from buying the fastest hardware available to lowering the cost of operating AI at hyperscale.\nAlphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ), and Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ) are all pursuing custom silicon alongside Nvidia’s GPUs because every percentage point of efficiency matters when spending tens of billions of dollars annually on AI infrastructure.\nThat trend doesn’t eliminate Nvidia’s advantage, but it does create a growing opportunity for the companies designing those custom chips—most notably Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) and Marvell Technology ( NASDAQ:MRVL ).\nThe Battle Is Moving From Training to Cost Efficiency\nNvidia still owns AI training. Its GPUs remain the gold standard for developing frontier models, and its CUDA software ecosystem continues to give it a competitive moat. But this is where the market is changing.\nOnce models are trained, they spend years handling inference — answering prompts, generating images, and powering AI applications. Those workloads don’t always require Nvidia’s most powerful processors. They often reward lower costs and higher efficiency instead. That is where application-specific integrated circuits, or ASICs, enter the picture.\nBroadcom has become the leading partner helping hyperscalers build custom AI accelerators, while Marvell has carved out a similar niche with customers including Amazon. Rather than selling chips under their own brands, they help cloud providers design silicon optimized for their own software and infrastructure.\nMorgan Stanley says hyperscalers continue to expand their investments in proprietary silicon to improve total cost of ownership and reduce dependence on merchant GPU suppliers.\nThe opportunity for Broadcom and Marvell is enormous. The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\nThe $1 trillion AI infrastructure race is pivoting: training is out, and custom-built efficiency is in. See why the industry's 'Second Act' is making room for new leaders beyond Nvidia.\nThe Economics Are Becoming Harder to Ignore\nThe appeal of custom silicon comes down to dollars. Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion. It illustrates why hyperscalers are increasingly willing to invest years designing their own chips.\nSurprisingly, this isn’t an either-or decision for hyperscalers. Google continues developing Tensor Processing Units with Broadcom while still purchasing enormous numbers of Nvidia GPUs. Amazon follows a similar strategy with Trainium alongside Nvidia deployments.\nThat diversification gives cloud providers leverage in negotiations while matching the right chip to the right workload.\nNvidia Is Still Winning — But Watch the Suppliers\nGranted, Nvidia is hardly standing still. The company continues expanding beyond GPUs into networking, rack-scale systems, and software while opening technologies such as NVLink Fusion to custom silicon partners — including Marvell itself.\nThat said, investors sometimes underestimate where the fastest incremental growth may occur. Every new custom AI accelerator designed by Google, Meta, Amazon, or another hyperscaler creates another opportunity for Broadcom or Marvell. They benefit whether customers build proprietary chips instead of buying more off-the-shelf processors, and they are standing directly in the path of a massive $1 trillion spending tsunami.\nIn other words, Nvidia remains the king of AI compute, but Broadcom and Marvell are increasingly becoming the architects behind the industry’s second act.\nIn short, investors shouldn’t mistake Nvidia’s dominance for exclusivity. The AI infrastructure market is expanding so rapidly that multiple winners can emerge. Nvidia still commands the premium end of AI computing, particularly for training, but custom silicon is becoming an essential part of every hyperscaler’s long-term strategy. Broadcom appears best positioned today thanks to its deep relationships with Google and Meta, while Marvell continues strengthening its foothold with Amazon and other large customers.\nUltimately, investors looking beyond today’s GPU boom may find that the companies quietly designing tomorrow’s AI chips offer just as compelling a long-term opportunity.\n\nCASE 34 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How far will the US crackdown on advanced AI-chip exports to third countries escalate — will the US Commerce Department go beyond its May 2026 guidance and impose formal licensing requirements or other escalated restrictions on AI-chip shipments to third countries such as Malaysia, Singapore and Japan by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether the US Commerce Department / Bureau of Industry and Security moves beyond its May 2026 guidance to a binding export-licensing rule or other escalated restriction on AI-chip shipments to third countries, and the resulting constraint on Nvidia's sales to those markets.\", \"period\": \"Nvidia's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027) — set by the reason: a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\", \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\", \"settled_by\": \"A US Commerce Department / Bureau of Industry and Security Federal Register rule, licence requirement or formal export-control action.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-5 turns on Beijing's decision whether to allow Chinese commercial buyers to purchase H200 chips the US cleared in December 2025; this thesis turns on Washington's decision whether to tighten third-country diversion controls. The two can move independently — Beijing could reopen H200 purchases while Washington hardens third-country rules, or Washington could act while Beijing stays closed.\"} \nARTICLE NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new “white list” of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14).\nOver the past few months, the chipmaker has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan, the report added, citing three people familiar with the matter.\nReuters could not immediately verify the report. Nvidia did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment outside regular business hours.\nThe tighter scrutiny comes as the Trump administration seeks to prevent advanced US chips from reaching Chinese entities through third countries.\nThe US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia’s cutting-edge Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\nNvidia has tightened compliance procedures following pressure from Washington, the FT said, adding that staff now visit customers’ data centres, verify contracts and interview end users as part of the checks.\nThe US Department of Commerce is also involved, providing oversight and political backing, the report said.\nThe department did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment outside regular business hours. REUTERS\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/census/packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nCASE\\\", j+1, \\\"\\\\nTHESIS\\\", json.dumps(x[\\\"thesis\\\"],ensure_ascii=False), \\\"\\\\nARTICLE\\\", x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"text\\\"])) for j,x in enumerate(p) if 0*5 <= j < (0+1)*5]'\"","aggregated_output":"\nCASE 1 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\", \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Next annual report (Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027, likely filed early 2027): the outstanding amount of credit support/guarantees; set by the reason (obligation already undertaken, tracked at the next annual report).\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K), including its guarantees and credit-support commitment disclosures.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"There is no open thesis; this asks specifically how much credit support/guarantee exposure NVIDIA is committing to backstop third parties' compute infrastructure, a balance-sheet and credit-risk question whose answer can move independently of NVIDIA's product revenue.\"} \nARTICLE NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑scale AI services.\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\nTo address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\n\nCASE 2 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\", \"period\": \"Check date of 31 December 2027 (18 months from the 1 July 2026 item date): whether the guarantee is signed by then; set by the rule for an unsigned obligation (a check date within 18 months of the item).\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's 8-K/10-Q/10-K filings disclosing guarantees and commitments, or an official NVIDIA confirmation / the definitive agreement being announced as executed.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks the aggregate outstanding credit support extended under the revenue-sharing 'AI Compute Partnership' model as disclosed at the next annual report; the OpenAI Ohio lease guarantee is a distinct, much larger, specific obligation still under negotiation that may never be signed and may sit outside that revenue-sharing program. NVIDIA could report a large clean-1 aggregate from other cloud partners without ever signing the OpenAI guarantee, or sign the OpenAI guarantee while accounting for it separately, so the two answers can move apart.\"} \nARTICLE It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balance sheet to gain market leverage, quietly evolving from a chip vendor into the 'central bank' of the AI computing ecosystem by offering financial backing to emerging cloud service providers in exchange for a share of their revenue.\nOn July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\nIn return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\nOn July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\nAccording to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program. This move marks a significant strategic shift for NVIDIA:\nOn one hand, it lowers financing barriers for emerging cloud service providers to expand its customer base; on the other, it directly participates in profit distribution within the downstream computing market through revenue sharing, thereby extending its control further down the AI value chain.\nBusiness Model Shift: From Selling Chips to Sharing Cloud Revenue\nAccording to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream. The core objective of this model is to overcome the long-standing financing barrier that has constrained startups from accessing large-scale computing power.\nNVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. These deployments directly reflect NVIDIA’s latest progress in transforming computing demand into deployable, financeable infrastructure.\nNVIDIA noted that emerging AI companies have historically faced significant constraints in accessing capital-intensive infrastructure—even long-term contractual commitments have often been insufficient to secure financing for compute capacity procurement. This means that numerous AI-native firms, model developers, and inference service providers face lengthy delays when scaling their computing capabilities: site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware commissioning—each step can take several months or even longer.\nThe promise of this new model is to realign economic structures so these groups can rapidly access full-stack accelerated computing capabilities without waiting for the traditional infrastructure build-out cycle to complete.\nUnderlying Rationale: Addressing the Core Challenge of GPU Financing\nReportedly, GPUs typically represent the most expensive component in AI data centers. For chip buyers with lower credit ratings, securing sufficient loans is itself a major hurdle.\nA data center executive commented on such NVIDIA deals as “killing two birds with one stone.” He explained that if NVIDIA merely provided a guarantee for a data center facility lease, “you’d still face the question of ‘how to finance the GPUs’”; but if NVIDIA commits to purchasing any unsold compute capacity within the facility, “the GPU financing issue is resolved—and so is the data center’s financing problem.”\nIn other words, NVIDIA’s backstop commitment effectively serves as a credit enhancement tool, enabling emerging cloud service providers—who would otherwise struggle to obtain bank loans—to mobilize significantly larger amounts of capital and accelerate data center construction.\nStrategic Intent: Breaking the Dominance of Large Customers\nNVIDIA’s launch of these initiatives is grounded in a clear strategic context. Currently, a small number of large cloud service providers—including Amazon, Microsoft, SpaceX, Oracle, Meta, and Google—purchase the majority of NVIDIA’s chip output. However, several of these companies are developing their own competitive AI chips, posing a potential threat to NVIDIA.\nTo reduce its reliance on these giant clients, NVIDIA has spent the past few years actively supporting a cohort of emerging GPU cloud service providers, such as CoreWeave. The newly announced 'AI Compute Partnership Program' represents a continuation and deepening of this strategy.\nAccording to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\nCapital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees\nNVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial.\nTo date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA’s own researchers have also used GPU servers leased back from Lambda.\nOn the capacity guarantee front, NVIDIA began advancing related deals in the autumn of last year. In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. This move effectively alleviated investor concerns about CoreWeave’s highly leveraged business model and drove its stock price up nearly 30% within the following week.\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\nTaken together, NVIDIA is constructing a multi-layered mechanism to align interests: equity investments, capacity leasebacks, lease guarantees, and now revenue-sharing arrangements. Each layer deepens the financial ties between NVIDIA and downstream cloud service providers, enabling NVIDIA to directly share in the incremental returns from the commercialization of AI computing power beyond chip sales alone.\n\nCASE 3 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\", \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\", \"period\": \"End of calendar 2026, the date the item supports ('could begin shipping later this year' relative to its 2 July 2026 date).\", \"opens_as\": \"product_node\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA quarterly earnings releases and 10-Q/10-K disclosures confirming Vera Rubin first shipments/revenue, and shipping confirmations.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Vera Rubin first shipments are a manufacturing/ramp milestone settled by NVIDIA's own delivery and revenue disclosures; the credit-support theses (clean-1, clean-2) track the dollar amount of guarantees/backstops NVIDIA extends to cloud partners. NVIDIA could ship Vera Rubin on schedule while extending little credit support, or delay shipments while guaranteeing partners' capacity, so the two answers move independently.\"} \nARTICLE Microsoft ( MSFT +1.28% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) are two heavyweights in the technology world and key players in artificial intelligence (AI). Microsoft seems to have its hand in every tech market segment, especially anything involving enterprise customers. Meanwhile, Nvidia has become the de facto leader in GPU chips used in AI data centers.\nBoth stocks have made investors very wealthy over the years. But which of these top AI stocks is the better buy for the next three years? This article will explain why I like Microsoft a tad more. Although, as you'll see below, it's hard to go wrong with either one.\nVera Rubin likely means Nvidia has more growth ahead\nNvidia has enjoyed one of the most impressive growth spurts in history over these past few years as the AI build-out juiced demand for its GPUs. This supercycle continues to rage on. Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\nWall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year. Nvidia's valuation is already well below its high at less than 19 times sales, and it might become much cheaper as these next 24 months play out.\nMicrosoft is pivoting to capitalize on its enterprise customer base\nMicrosoft originally tied its AI hopes to OpenAI. Although Microsoft has done well with its OpenAI investment and enjoyed significant cloud computing growth as a result of the partnership, it has still struggled to establish itself as a key AI player. Users, especially enterprises, have opted for Anthropic's Claude among other AI models. As a result, Microsoft shifted to a multi-model strategy and has reportedly considered adding open-source models to Copilot Cowork to lower token costs.\nInvestors should applaud Microsoft's willingness to adapt. The company has deeply entrenched relationships with the enterprise world, which gives Microsoft more margin for error in fine-tuning its AI strategy than most companies. Plus, Microsoft's Azure remains a formidable growth engine, with a staggering $627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations.\nNvidia's data center business has grown so much that the stock has become a concentrated investment in this ongoing supercycle. That means Nvidia has a sky-high ceiling with Vera Rubin shipping soon, but it's riskier in that the floor is much lower if companies stop pouring all this money into AI infrastructure. Meanwhile, Microsoft remains highly diversified across software, cloud computing, and AI. That lowers the company's ceiling, but it's also the safer stock.\nMicrosoft's impressive track record typically earns it a premium valuation. The stock has traded at an average of almost 33 times its trailing-12-month earnings over the past decade, but trades at just 22 times its earnings today. Meanwhile, Wall Street analysts expect the company to grow earnings by 16% to 17% annually over the next three to five years.\nWhile some may opt for Nvidia's immense growth potential , Microsoft rarely offers a valuation compelling enough to buy into.\nJustin Pope is a contributing Motley Fool stock market analyst covering information technology, consumer discretionary, consumer staples, and industrials. Prior to The Motley Fool, Justin was a business manager for an industrial company.\nBy Justin Pope – Jul 2, 2026 at 12:43PM EST\n\nCASE 4 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\", \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\", \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 (the item's 'next year' from its 2026 date), when the four hyperscalers report full-year capex; set by the item, which projects the figure could reach $1 trillion in 2027 up from around $650 billion in 2026.\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"The four leading AI hyperscalers' quarterly and full-year capital expenditure disclosures in their earnings releases and 10-K/10-Q filings for FY2027, aggregated.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 tracks whether Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform reaches first shipments by end-2026, a product-timing question; this thesis tracks the industry-level capex quantity that drives total GPU demand. They can move independently: Rubin could ship on schedule while hyperscaler capex is cut back, or Rubin could slip while aggregate capex stays high on spending for existing platforms.\"} \nARTICLE Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) investors have had it good for several years. In 2023, 2024, and 2025, it produced market-crushing returns, making it one of the best stocks to own during each of those years. However, 2026 hasn't been that way. The stock is up 5% so far in 2026, which isn't nothing, but compared to the returns that Nvidia investors have enjoyed in recent years, it feels like an absolute failure. On another note, its market-crushing status is in jeopardy for 2026, as the S&P 500 is up nearly 10% for the year.\nWith Nvidia stock looking like a dud this year, many investors are wondering whether now is the time to cash out and look for other investment opportunities, or to load up on Nvidia shares. Let's take a look, as not everything is as it appears with Nvidia.\nNvidia was the top AI stock to own over the past three years, due to its market position. Nvidia designs graphics processing units (GPUs) and the hardware that supports them. Although it doesn't own any of the fabrication process itself, it works with several companies that manufacture its GPUs, which it then sells to a wide range of customers.\nBefore 2023, Nvidia's business was more balanced. However, the massive demand for AI data centers has driven this lineup of GPUs into high demand, and Nvidia's product line has become the go-to for several AI hyperscalers and cloud providers. Nvidia has a complete ecosystem of products that gives its clients everything they need to run an Nvidia-powered data center, locking in clients once they've chosen to use its products.\nThis bodes well for the future, as AI hyperscalers are spending a massive amount of money this year and beyond to continue building out their AI computing footprints. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion. That means more growth is coming to Nvidia, and the investing story is far from complete. Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\nHowever, the stock price doesn't reflect that.\nNvidia trades at 21.7 times forward earnings, essentially the same price tag as the S&P 500. This conveys that the market believes Nvidia will be a market-average stock after 2026's growth is accounted for. However, Wall Street analysts don't believe that, as they're guiding for major growth next year. That's why the stock looks so cheap when next year's earnings are used to value the stock.\nAs a result, I think Nvidia is an excellent stock to buy now. None of next year's growth has been priced in, which could lead to strong returns toward the end of this year.\n\nCASE 5 \nTHESIS {\"description\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\", \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\", \"period\": \"Calendar 2026-2027 memory contract cycles; set by the item's forward cost period (second half of 2026 and beyond).\", \"opens_as\": \"shared_quantity\", \"settled_by\": \"Quarterly contract-price releases from TrendForce/DRAMeXchange (DRAM, NAND, HBM) and Nvidia's rack BOM/cost disclosures.\", \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"Memory contract prices can move independently of the big four hyperscalers' aggregate data-center capex: a supply-driven memory squeeze can lift Nvidia's per-rack cost and price even if capex totals stall, and memory prices can fall while capex keeps climbing. An analyst would bet on the memory-price direction separately because it drives Nvidia's per-rack cost, pricing and supply availability.\"} \nARTICLE Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams put it to work with Executive Thought Leadership .\nKey facts, context, and what it means.\nMemory costs jumped 435% to roughly $2 million per rack due to increased LPDDR5X capacity and 3D NAND storage, making it a strategic budget variable across all technology purchases\nGPU share of bill of materials fell from 63% to 51% while memory rose to 25-30%, requiring infrastructure planners to model AI spend on total system cost not accelerator count\nMemory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\nTurn your Software & Technology expertise into content.\nWhat infrastructure planners should account for\nThe single most expensive part of Nvidia's next-generation AI rack is no longer just the GPU. According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation. For any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\nMorgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\nOnly a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\nFor enterprise technology buyers, that means memory has become a strategic budget variable rather than a rounding error, whether the purchase is a data center commitment or a fleet refresh.\nThree considerations follow directly from the Morgan Stanley breakdown.\nModel your AI spend on total system cost, not GPU count. The generation-over-generation story is that peripheral components, memory, printed circuit boards, networking, cooling, and power, are absorbing a larger share of every dollar. Budgets built around GPU headline prices will understate the real bill.\nTreat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\nRevisit the build-versus-rent math. As rack prices climb toward $8 million and memory pricing stays volatile, the calculus between owning infrastructure and consuming cloud capacity shifts. Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\nNvidia's Vera Rubin platform will deliver a step change in performance, and demand is not in question. What has changed is where the money goes. The AI infrastructure bill is increasingly a memory bill, and the organizations planning capacity for the next two years will be the ones that budget for the full system rather than the chip on the front of the box.\nNvidia's memory costs soar 485%, latest AI systems now cost $7.8 million to build ↗ · Tom's Hardware\nNvidia Vera Rubin rack hit with 435% memory price surge ↗ · Wccftech\nNvidia's $7.8 million Vera Rubin rack rewrites AI profit map as GPU share shrinks ↗ · BigGo Finance\nNVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack memory costs explode as HBM4 and LPDDR5X prices cross $2 million ↗ · OnMSFT\nEvery story in MarketScale Software & Technology starts with a company putting its solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers on the record. Buyers are already reading this topic. The only question is whose experts they find.\nBuyers ask AI engines who to consider, and published expert answers are what those engines cite.\nMarketScale gives Software & Technology B2B marketing teams a full content studio: record, produce, and distribute your own channel. No agency, no crew, no guessing.\nBefore they reach out, Software & Technology buyers ask AI engines which vendors to trust. Explore how your experts, customers, and partners can become useful content for buyers and AI search.\nThis article was produced through MarketScale. The same platform turns your solutions engineers, product teams, and customer engineers into the articles, video, and social content Software & Technology buyers are searching for. Start a free trial and see it with your own people. For qualifying companies, no credit card, no demo required.\nUber deployed ChatGPT Work to more than 30,000 employees seven weeks after cutting about 3,300 jobs (10% of corporate staff). The company framed layoffs as organizational restructuring, while the rollout expands a pilot that embedded AI engineers with teams to automate pieces of their work.\n01 Uber expanded ChatGPT Work from a 1,000-person pilot to over 30,000 employees across operations, finance, marketing, and product teams.\n02 During the pilot, 80% of early adopters were non-engineers, and AI pods of 30 engineers paired with teams to automate work such as analyzing operational data, localizing training materials, and building dashboards.\n03 Uber’s rollout shows AI deployments can go beyond licenses: curated integrations, embedded technical support, an internal skills library, and plans to measure adoption and outcomes.\nTwo critical unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619) with CVSS 9.2 scores affected 34.4 million vulnerable services as of October 23, with no patches available for PHP 8.1 and older. Exploitation attempts reached millions within hours of disclosure, though confirmed compromises remain unproven, while attackers tested a Mirai-derived botnet variant against production servers.\nUNICOM Engineering chief technologist Austin Hypes compares rear door, single-phase, two-phase and immersion cooling for AI racks on Flawless Execution. He says two-phase cold plates use dielectric refrigerant and can avoid chillers and evaporative towers in hot, dry sites, and immersion can keep servers up ten to 15 minutes and sometimes an hour or more after a cooling loss. Schneider Electric engineers cite estimates that single-phase holds about 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market.\n01 A Motivair by Schneider Electric engineer puts single-phase cold plates at an estimated 55% of the 2026 liquid cooling market and expects them to dominate for five to 10 years.\nStories like this one run on content MarketScale captures from real practitioners. See how your team's expertise becomes coverage in Software & Technology and beyond.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
